3e3-4d58-b507-b78796c24e77","sourceKey":"quiz:359","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager who represents the organisation at official meetings, resolves conflicts among employees, and motivates subordinates is primarily performing which category of managerial roles?","options":["decisional roles","administrative roles","informational roles","interpersonal roles"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"6d1fb69c-735d-51d0-a18d-492e28b751cd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dd7c7c7b-8b4f-45b8-bf9a-78c8e4d64165","sourceKey":"quiz:360","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"According to managerial role theory, a manager disseminating critical information to subordinates is primarily performing which role?","options":["resource allocator","liaison","monitor","disseminator"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"dd1de1d5-f1dd-51f5-80c3-af61a18ec794","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"de54538a-3c9c-496f-ad33-3e79687317ad","sourceKey":"quiz:361","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The existence of corporate governance frameworks ensures that organisational decisions reflect:","options":["executive dominance over shareholders","accountability, transparency, and responsible oversight","profit maximisation at all costs","competitive elimination of rivals"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"124bb00e-b41c-56d4-84ed-f82fabb9b6bd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2d798dc3-75d0-41fd-b50b-6bad51cbcfbe","sourceKey":"quiz:362","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The legal doctrine underlying a sole proprietorship treats the owner and the business as:","options":["separate legal entities","the same legal and economic entity","financially independent bodies","regulated by different tax laws"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"ad10e300-4944-5d02-b9ca-a17e7dee62c4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5264bfc4-ec6f-401e-8c0e-37c876bd8995","sourceKey":"quiz:363","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Long-term expansion of a sole proprietorship is often constrained by:","options":["mandatory governance structures","restricted capacity for large-scale capital mobilisation","excessive regulatory oversight","separation of ownership from management"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"d2c1cf24-4f95-5538-b112-d9f2ab11c06b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"eaea35e0-df59-4fd5-92ec-877fd5bcbafe","sourceKey":"quiz:364","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Directors fulfilling fiduciary duty act primarily in the interests of:","options":["government regulators","shareholders while ensuring transparency and accountability","competing firms","their personal enrichment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"b7e1da4f-da0c-546c-aa6c-49bad4c17def","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a4ec4561-0e03-4f4d-a092-06002ecae79b","sourceKey":"quiz:365","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A partnership agreement does not guarantee:","options":["dispute resolution procedures","predetermined levels of income generation","profit-sharing arrangements","allocation of responsibilities"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"68307056-6a59-5aff-a1fb-e816cc548bfa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9d889aab-0cef-495b-a4fe-4591e1a6ed90","sourceKey":"quiz:366","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Segmentation, targeting, and positioning (STP) enable firms to:","options":["standardise all products","identify and serve specific customer groups effectively","eliminate all competition","reduce all operational costs"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"1e903ac5-ad9d-5e73-92b2-d071d81e11c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5c0aa9af-87af-404b-b91c-4a5d66e39e59","sourceKey":"quiz:367","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Studying biographies of successful managers primarily illustrates:","options":["universal rules for success","how managerial judgement evolves within specific contexts","that personality alone determines success","the replacement of theory with intuition"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"36d90261-17ec-5248-97f1-118c8282d3e6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8976f4b9-59b0-4efb-b12d-39799f8abcd4","sourceKey":"quiz:368","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Organising the entire workforce to perform sales functions falls:","options":["within marketing responsibilities","outside the primary scope of marketing","under financial management","under production management"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"d1b6a160-fb1c-5207-adf3-4181d546752b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4c7549db-7964-4c9f-9de6-5191f368261f","sourceKey":"quiz:369","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A business NOT operating as a sole proprietorship would most likely have:","options":["single ownership","profits distributed among several owners","unlimited liability","direct owner authority"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"48dd19e2-68e6-52bf-a3ea-a7b6d3b3f9cf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7a6d8bda-751d-4413-bb3b-e1e11c1fdfff","sourceKey":"quiz:370","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Managers described as change agents primarily:","options":["enforce stability","facilitate organisational adaptation to environmental shifts","focus only on efficiency","ensure procedural compliance"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"56603639-1079-58fe-b28b-f106a892179a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"210b9ef0-8eb1-4a44-9843-2bc339782ed1","sourceKey":"quiz:371","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Change leadership differs from change management by focusing on:","options":["administrative procedures","shaping vision, culture, and employee commitment","operational supervision","technical implementation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"e2867983-7444-5185-ad75-05e66d647cf6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e73757a1-4d71-4f5c-af67-9ff89c882581","sourceKey":"quiz:372","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Urban consumer markets differ from rural markets primarily because urban consumers:","options":["rely on informal trading","have lower purchasing power","possess greater purchasing power and product exposure","prefer unbranded products"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"b2280f51-0285-5f1b-8e19-584cbc9f7eed","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"09cc5eb6-5841-48b7-8b6c-b3885e558c3b","sourceKey":"quiz:373","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Rising inflation and foreign exchange instability are classified as:","options":["micro-environmental forces","macro-environmental forces","internal variables","operational inefficiencies"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"fe55ce04-515b-5534-922e-3f1d1331fa6d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"27d703c1-16f0-4c96-8ca1-b0996883c389","sourceKey":"quiz:374","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A firm with resources but poor coordination is missing:","options":["market demand","organisational structure","regulatory compliance","entrepreneurship"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"9d20328c-ba4d-53e7-a7e4-88064803cfd2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9fbc5d7c-d610-4245-979e-29654d46213e","sourceKey":"quiz:375","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Core HRM responsibilities include:","options":["only recruitment","only training","recruitment, selection, training, and performance management","only compliance"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"a6d1d01d-26ce-52d4-9bc2-bc0aeff6794c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3d143184-01bd-4490-94fa-a990fc4a6f2a","sourceKey":"quiz:376","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A market in modern business is best defined as:","options":["a physical location only","a network of buyers and sellers interacting to exchange value","regulatory institutions","geographic trading spots"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"6f52d509-bfa0-5de5-9d41-702f174ad799","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e8ade7ce-60a0-43d0-8fe5-7a84bcf5ff14","sourceKey":"quiz:377","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Firms adopting digital technologies in response to competitive pressures demonstrate:","options":["market insulation","environmental adaptation","regulatory compliance","centralisation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"4fa01cec-12a8-5b94-8214-ac05400dcee9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b4a67911-c6aa-43f2-b9da-0014cec00df6","sourceKey":"quiz:378","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Business ownership structure is most influenced by:","options":["marketing strategy","financial liability, managerial control, and regulatory obligations","employee motivation","technological capabilities"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"2a57b63e-fcef-5d3a-8663-e86b25d91ca9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f2c5cbc9-4db9-4faa-b947-328e10916243","sourceKey":"quiz:379","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"PESTLE analysis evaluates:","options":["internal capabilities","macro-environmental forces","competitive behaviour","financial indicators"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"60fb4a1e-a4d7-528d-b68e-34e07132832e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"54924798-9729-4442-85fd-98a305324114","sourceKey":"quiz:380","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Financial statements provide managers with:","options":["replacement for planning","structured information about financial condition and performance","government compliance only","budgeting substitutes"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"82cf4f59-ce6a-5964-910e-a50cb2df542b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5ea6a723-ce85-4dbb-b619-8794a4ea14f4","sourceKey":"quiz:381","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Markets are fundamentally classified based on:","options":["production scale","the nature and purpose of buyers","physical location","distribution channels"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"011b05e6-4d32-50a2-a339-0e6bb9b19bfe","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"828ae1a0-d875-4a05-8d83-62065f33aad2","sourceKey":"quiz:382","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Market segmentation involves:","options":["price leadership","focusing on a defined group of consumers with distinct needs","product standardisation","competitive parity"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"421e1ff1-ad6a-59ab-a932-18774dbf2c40","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3699c865-9e6d-4d2c-ba0c-751f40de63e6","sourceKey":"quiz:383","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Process optimisation in manufacturing involves:","options":["market expansion","reducing waste and coordinating procurement with production","financial restructuring","product diversification"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"a540e958-9b1c-59c4-bdc0-349cf54ef185","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c9c412d3-74b8-4dff-9bcb-82aa7126dc85","sourceKey":"quiz:384","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Biographic studies extract:","options":["historical documentation only","transferable managerial lessons from real-life experiences","executive glorification","financial comparisons"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"fece9768-1bd7-5c15-ab02-ee43fe1929db","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"84d526ea-61b9-474d-a201-0b9844676624","sourceKey":"quiz:385","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Management functions (planning, organising, leading, controlling) are best understood as:","options":["isolated tasks","mutually reinforcing activities in a continuous cycle","sequential procedures","top executive obligations only"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"ae11b0c5-c5d5-54fd-9c9d-7134a95ac466","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ef22ebf6-8f88-400e-b986-132a470b665c","sourceKey":"quiz:386","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Sole proprietorship advantages include:","options":["limited liability","ease of formation with minimal regulatory procedures","perpetual succession","access to public capital"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"185fc50d-2f58-5e70-bf1a-37ba3ffcf7d4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c5e20c60-e453-46fa-8e25-eed00df40abc","sourceKey":"quiz:387","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Rural consumer markets in developing economies are shaped by:","options":["formal retail dominance","infrastructure and logistics constraints","e-commerce majority","higher disposable income"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"bbb4817e-4d63-5329-86a8-6d13af97f274","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"423533d5-6a95-4372-b17a-199b5d1df524","sourceKey":"quiz:388","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The marketing mix refers to:","options":["price adjustments only","a combination of strategic variables to influence target markets","distribution systems only","promotional activities only"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"23a7f13f-5452-5474-82c3-aacf37642f9a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7708a287-0702-463f-bbc8-61d478b79ee7","sourceKey":"quiz:389","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"To raise capital with limited liability and business continuity, an entrepreneur should choose:","options":["sole proprietorship","limited liability company","partnership","cooperative society"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"1abbe8b7-1d2c-5cf7-85f5-fc9698917cab","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"67005b2f-b976-4492-a120-309171747afc","sourceKey":"quiz:390","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"HRM is most comprehensively the system through which organisations:","options":["only hire employees","recruit, develop, and manage people to achieve objectives","handle documentation only","enforce discipline only"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Exam Practice (Past Test Questions)"}},{"itemId":"58dca9fe-1102-567a-bae2-6dec6a9b89a2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"course_overview","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"course-overview","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Business Statistics","body":"Data collection, analysis, and interpretation techniques including graphs, tables, statistical measures, probability distributions, and their application in business decision-making.","topics":[{"chapter":"1-2","description":"Data Collection Methods and Types"},{"chapter":"3-4","description":"Graphs, Tables, and Data Visualization"},{"chapter":"5-6","description":"Central Tendency: Mean, Median, Mode"},{"chapter":"7-8","description":"Dispersion: Variance and Standard Deviation"},{"chapter":"9-10","description":"Probability and Distributions"}],"examFocus":["Data collection and sampling techniques","Calculation of mean, median, mode","Standard deviation and variance calculations","Probability distributions","Problem-solving with formulas","Interpretation of statistical results"]}},{"itemId":"1fd509a0-0dc5-51f1-88c3-e42cd80de884","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:1","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Core Theoretical Framework - Descriptive vs Inferential Statistics","body":"Statistics is the science of collecting, organizing, presenting, analyzing, and interpreting data to make informed business decisions. The field is divided into descriptive statistics (summarizing data) and inferential statistics (making predictions about populations based on samples).","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Descriptive Statistics: Methods for organizing and summarizing information (e.g., mean, charts, frequency distributions).","Inferential Statistics: Using sample data to make predictions or draw conclusions about a larger population.","Population: The complete set of all individuals or items of interest.","Sample: A subset of the population selected for study.","Parameter: A descriptive measure calculated from a population (e.g., population mean μ).","Statistic: A descriptive measure calculated from a sample (e.g., sample mean x̄)."],"examTip":"Always distinguish between population (parameter) and sample (statistic). This is frequently tested in MCQs."}},{"itemId":"6a91fe26-f07e-5618-a772-babb501e8536","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:2","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Levels of Data Measurement - Nominal, Ordinal, Interval, Ratio","body":"Understanding the four levels of measurement is crucial for selecting appropriate statistical methods. Each level has specific characteristics and determines which operations (addition, ranking, etc.) can be performed.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Nominal: Labels or categories with no inherent order (e.g., gender, color, marital status). Only counting and mode are appropriate.","Ordinal: Data with a meaningful order but unequal intervals (e.g., ranking, education level, satisfaction rating).","Interval: Numeric data with equal intervals but no true zero (e.g., temperature in Celsius, IQ scores).","Ratio: Numeric data with equal intervals AND a true zero point allowing ratios (e.g., height, weight, income, age).","True Zero: A value of zero means \"none\" or \"absence\" - only found in ratio scales."],"examTip":"Remember the hierarchy: Ratio > Interval > Ordinal > Nominal. Ratio is the highest level with all properties."}},{"itemId":"1fcddbd3-bd14-5091-8c43-faa5bfb9ab4e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:3","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Data Sources and Variables","body":"Data can be classified by source (primary vs secondary) and by type (qualitative vs quantitative). Understanding variable types determines appropriate analysis methods.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Primary Data: Collected firsthand specifically for the current research purpose (e.g., surveys, experiments, interviews).","Secondary Data: Already existing data collected by others for different purposes (e.g., journals, government reports).","Qualitative Variables: Non-numeric categories or labels (e.g., gender, nationality).","Quantitative Variables: Numeric measurements that can be counted or measured.","Discrete Quantitative: Countable values with gaps (e.g., number of children, students in a class).","Continuous Quantitative: Measurable values on a continuum (e.g., weight, height, temperature)."],"examTip":"Number of children = discrete. Weight = continuous. This distinction appears frequently in exams."}},{"itemId":"368c510d-3ca9-5341-87d5-62879803b7c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:4","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Data Presentation - Frequency Distributions and Histograms","body":"Organizing large datasets into frequency distributions makes patterns visible. Understanding class intervals, boundaries, and midpoints is essential for constructing and interpreting frequency tables and histograms.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Range: Maximum value - Minimum value. First step in creating grouped data.","Class Width (Interval): The difference between upper and lower class boundaries.","Class Midpoint: (Upper Boundary + Lower Boundary) / 2. Represents the center of each class.","Histogram: A bar graph with adjacent bars representing continuous data.","Ogive: A cumulative frequency graph used to find medians and quartiles graphically.","Frequency Density: Used when class intervals are unequal. Calculated as Frequency / Class Width.","Relative Frequency: Frequency / Total Frequency. The sum of all relative frequencies = 1.0.","Pie Chart: Best for showing proportions or percentages of a whole."],"examTip":"Class Midpoint formula is heavily tested. Always add boundaries, never subtract when finding midpoint."}},{"itemId":"87327e34-e88a-5b33-baa1-06e4d66016c1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:5","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Measures of Central Tendency - Mean, Median, Mode","body":"Central tendency measures locate the \"center\" of a dataset. Each measure has strengths and weaknesses depending on data characteristics and the presence of outliers.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Arithmetic Mean: The average. x̄ = Σx / n for ungrouped data; x̄ = Σfx / Σf for grouped data.","Median: The middle value when data is ordered. Also Q2, D5, P50. Best for skewed data with outliers.","Mode: The most frequently occurring value. Can be used for qualitative data.","Symmetrical Distribution: Mean = Median = Mode.","Positively Skewed (Right Tail): Mean > Median > Mode.","Negatively Skewed (Left Tail): Mean < Median < Mode.","Weighted Mean: Used when some values have more importance. x̄w = Σ(w × x) / Σw.","Geometric Mean: Used for growth rates and ratios. GM = ⁿ√(x1 × x2 × ... × xn).","Harmonic Mean: Used for rates (e.g., speed). HM = n / Σ(1/x).","Property: Sum of deviations from the mean always equals zero: Σ(x - x̄) = 0."],"examTip":"For skewed distributions, Median is the best measure. Mean is most affected by outliers."}},{"itemId":"f93cba49-b589-5d73-9e0b-edd341a7064e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:6","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Partition Values - Quartiles, Deciles, Percentiles","body":"Partition values divide ordered datasets into equal parts. They are essential for understanding data distribution and identifying position within a dataset.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Quartiles (Q): Divide data into 4 equal parts. Q1 = 25th percentile, Q2 = Median = 50th percentile, Q3 = 75th percentile.","Deciles (D): Divide data into 10 equal parts. D1 to D9. D5 = Median.","Percentiles (P): Divide data into 100 equal parts. P1 to P99. P50 = Median.","Relationships: D7 = P70; Q1 = P25; Q3 = P75.","For Grouped Data: Use the formula with Lower Boundary (XL), cumulative frequency (f0), frequency of median class (fD5), and class width (c)."],"examTip":"Know the equivalencies: Q2 = D5 = P50 = Median. These appear in multiple forms on exams."}},{"itemId":"db0ed8dc-d660-53e4-8b27-44fe2249810e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:7","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Measures of Dispersion - Range, Variance, Standard Deviation","body":"Dispersion measures quantify the spread or variability in a dataset. They complement central tendency measures by describing how data points differ from the center.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Range: Maximum - Minimum. Simplest measure but highly affected by outliers.","Variance: Average of squared deviations from the mean. σ² = Σ(x - x̄)² / N.","Standard Deviation: Square root of variance. σ = √(Variance). Expressed in same units as data.","Interquartile Range (IQR): Q3 - Q1. Resistant to outliers.","Quartile Deviation (Semi-IQR): (Q3 - Q1) / 2. Also called semi-interquartile range.","Coefficient of Variation (CV): (Standard Deviation / Mean) × 100. Unitless, used to compare variability across different datasets.","Mean Deviation: Is minimized when calculated from the Median.","Property: If variance = 0, all values are identical.","Property: Standard Deviation is always positive or zero."],"examTip":"If SD = 4, then Variance = 16. CV allows comparison between datasets with different units or scales."}},{"itemId":"2bc84b85-e191-5fb3-acee-2fc48c259769","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:8","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Skewness and Kurtosis","body":"Skewness measures asymmetry in a distribution, while kurtosis measures the peakedness. These shape characteristics affect which statistical methods are appropriate.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Skewness: Measures the direction and degree of asymmetry in a distribution.","Positive Skew (Right Skew): Long tail to the right. Mean > Median.","Negative Skew (Left Skew): Long tail to the left. Mean < Median.","Kurtosis: Measures the flatness or peakedness of a distribution relative to the normal distribution.","Leptokurtic: Peaked distribution (tall and thin).","Mesokurtic: Normal distribution (bell-shaped curve).","Platykurtic: Flat distribution (short and wide)."],"examTip":"Right skew = positive skew = tail points right. Mesokurtic = normal distribution."}},{"itemId":"8ca95079-6045-5666-81dd-ebc20a37b065","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:9","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Probability Foundations - Sample Space and Events","body":"Probability quantifies the likelihood of events. Understanding basic probability rules is essential for inferential statistics and business decision-making under uncertainty.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Sample Space: The set of all possible outcomes of a random experiment.","Event: A subset of the sample space (one or more outcomes).","Probability Range: 0 ≤ P(E) ≤ 1. Impossible event = 0, Certain event = 1.","Complement Rule: P(not A) = 1 - P(A).","Mutually Exclusive Events: Cannot occur simultaneously. P(A and B) = 0.","Addition Rule (Mutually Exclusive): P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).","Independent Events: Occurrence of one does not affect the other.","Multiplication Rule (Independent): P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B).","Conditional Probability: P(A|B) = Probability of A given that B has occurred.","Classical Probability: Based on equally likely outcomes. P(E) = (Number of favorable outcomes) / (Total outcomes)."],"examTip":"For mutually exclusive events, use addition. For independent events, use multiplication."}},{"itemId":"6175c4aa-6405-5db9-be54-5784696733c9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:10","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Sampling Methods and Techniques","body":"Sampling involves selecting a subset of a population for study. Different sampling methods suit different research objectives and resource constraints.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Census: Studying every member of the population. Accurate but expensive and time-consuming.","Sampling: Studying a subset. More practical and cost-effective.","Simple Random Sampling: Every member has equal chance of selection.","Systematic Sampling: Selecting every kth member from a list (e.g., every 10th person).","Stratified Sampling: Divide population into strata (subgroups), then sample from each stratum.","Cluster Sampling: Divide population into clusters, randomly select clusters, study all members in selected clusters.","Convenience Sampling: Non-probabilistic. Select easily accessible members.","Quota Sampling: Non-probabilistic. Set quotas for different subgroups.","Sampling Error: Natural variation that occurs because we study a sample, not the entire population."],"examTip":"Stratified sampling ensures all subgroups are represented. Quota sampling is non-probabilistic."}},{"itemId":"e92041fa-d4e7-5033-8471-f5d122c0356e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:11","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Sampling Distributions and Central Limit Theorem","body":"The sampling distribution of the mean describes how sample means vary across different samples from the same population. The Central Limit Theorem is fundamental to inferential statistics.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Sampling Distribution: The probability distribution of a sample statistic (e.g., sample mean).","Standard Error: The standard deviation of a sampling distribution. SE = σ / √n.","Property: As sample size (n) increases, standard error decreases.","Central Limit Theorem (CLT): As sample size gets large (typically n ≥ 30), the sampling distribution of the mean approaches a normal distribution, regardless of the population's shape.","Importance: CLT allows us to use normal distribution methods for inference even when population distribution is unknown.","Point Estimate: A single numerical value used to estimate a population parameter (e.g., using sample mean to estimate population mean)."],"examTip":"CLT is one of the most important theorems in statistics. Sample size ≥ 30 is the general rule for CLT to apply."}},{"itemId":"b5ad2d4a-ea93-5f72-8016-bceaefdf1db9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bd815bac-0f5c-4910-9b04-063e3ec28066","sourceKey":"quiz:1","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is the best definition of Statistics?","options":["The study of mathematical numbers and equations.","The science of collecting, organizing, presenting, analyzing, and interpreting data.","The process of guessing business outcomes based on intuition.","A branch of accounting that deals with audit trials."],"correctOption":1,"section":"Basic Concepts & Data Collection"}},{"itemId":"b379f051-dd23-53db-b018-55d991d300c4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d7a73e5c-b442-482a-89ab-69ed8c5cbf7b","sourceKey":"quiz:2","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A branch of statistics that consists of methods for organizing and summarizing information is:","options":["Inferential Statistics","Theoretical Statistics","Descriptive Statistics","Analytical Statistics"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Basic Concepts & Data Collection"}},{"itemId":"59a17e56-56d4-50e8-bb51-240e3fb47d3b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId"