00, the position of the median is:","options":["50th","25th","100th","1st"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Advanced Interpretation"}},{"itemId":"1bc12fe9-8395-57ff-8c0c-00db92d45f46","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a52f8caa-0e03-4c9d-a699-b127e9aa4677","sourceKey":"quiz:93","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following cannot be negative?","options":["Mean","Median","Standard Deviation","Skewness"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Advanced Interpretation"}},{"itemId":"0ee54516-f9fa-5c66-9a94-e27ead92a840","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fb46977f-e491-4944-9072-b4be5780e286","sourceKey":"quiz:94","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The \"empirical rule\" applies to which distribution?","options":["Binomial","Normal","Poisson","Uniform"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Advanced Interpretation"}},{"itemId":"38566817-33e7-5ba7-b4c9-4da6ace1dd55","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5477cf13-d0f2-4363-a748-0f6b9627e1bf","sourceKey":"quiz:95","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The range of the set {7, 7, 7, 7} is:","options":["7","0","1","4"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Advanced Interpretation"}},{"itemId":"1bc8e79d-b468-5ced-9cc3-50194543a41c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1e266ada-6bce-445d-af4d-13b98fec051d","sourceKey":"quiz:96","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the formula for the Mean, Σ stands for:","options":["Sample","Summation (add all)","Standard","Square"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Advanced Interpretation"}},{"itemId":"10b56f53-d7cd-58a4-86f0-9f22e91d240b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4913096c-d5b4-4790-93db-24ac86633e37","sourceKey":"quiz:97","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Deciles divide data into how many parts?","options":["4","10","100","2"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Advanced Interpretation"}},{"itemId":"a3d99069-143c-5595-a439-eef5cb4ae96c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3e37e641-28cc-46a3-b070-5af6a092ebbc","sourceKey":"quiz:98","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Primary data is generally more ________ than secondary data.","options":["Expensive","Reliable (for specific use)","Time-consuming","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Advanced Interpretation"}},{"itemId":"2f7eba3b-dc23-582d-af25-4ff118d14103","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"85af4b6f-c531-45de-bcc8-a0242322d2d8","sourceKey":"quiz:99","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The probability of picking a Red card from a standard deck is:","options":["1/4","1/2","1/13","1/52"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Advanced Interpretation"}},{"itemId":"d6feeded-12f6-5efd-893f-df684680fb93","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e72a3015-8b79-4a6b-88b5-a49521b911e9","sourceKey":"quiz:100","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Statistics is essential in business for:","options":["Quality control","Market research","Financial forecasting","All of the above"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Advanced Interpretation"}},{"itemId":"971de73c-becb-5d2d-b08e-5e38624007c9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3db6dc54-2634-4891-8ad4-33e8ffcc77d2","sourceKey":"quiz:101","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In statistical terminology, a 'parameter' is best described as:","options":["A numerical summary computed from a sample","A numerical characteristic of a population","Any variable used in a regression model","A non-numerical attribute of data"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Statistical Terminology"}},{"itemId":"b9b28683-9cdb-5d85-804f-ffb176741631","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7b6ce1ed-0e9e-4373-9803-ea1c1741142c","sourceKey":"quiz:102","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which branch of statistics is concerned with drawing conclusions about a population based on sample data?","options":["Descriptive statistics","Inferential statistics","Exploratory data analysis","Applied mathematics"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Statistical Terminology"}},{"itemId":"77ecb5bf-5584-5dd2-8fee-8e166e2a15ed","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"217cf903-d57c-46db-a614-1e91f297cba7","sourceKey":"quiz:103","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An ordinal scale of measurement:","options":["Has a true zero point and equal intervals","Allows only the classification of data into named categories","Allows ranking but does not guarantee equal intervals between ranks","Has all properties of a ratio scale"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Statistical Terminology"}},{"itemId":"37605e91-ba5b-55c6-a8ab-576c79a25fed","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f896aed5-9ac0-4162-b43c-7e95b0b3da6a","sourceKey":"quiz:104","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The process by which a researcher ensures that every member of the population has an equal chance of being selected is called:","options":["Stratified sampling","Systematic sampling","Simple random sampling","Cluster sampling"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Sampling & Estimation"}},{"itemId":"f799816d-78fb-5d8b-aa25-a66d05f4e5d3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0bb48f73-2811-4804-8b8a-a695403853b0","sourceKey":"quiz:105","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"'Respondent fatigue' in survey research refers to:","options":["The physical tiredness of field enumerators","A reduction in data quality due to declining respondent attention during lengthy surveys","The refusal rate among selected respondents","The tendency of respondents to give socially desirable answers"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Basic Concepts & Data Collection"}},{"itemId":"3d478c33-3636-5e6e-967a-50ef356b5c4c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"49e7e9a0-6863-47c6-8ccb-2f50d2e811be","sourceKey":"quiz:106","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A variable whose values arise from a counting process (e.g., number of customers per day) is called:","options":["Continuous","Qualitative","Discrete","Interval-scaled"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Statistical Terminology"}},{"itemId":"45ffb001-2ce2-533b-9263-cd97b6ab5325","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"25cae864-d528-4827-8b0a-668347b38c52","sourceKey":"quiz:107","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In a stratified random sample, the population is first divided into:","options":["Clusters of convenience","Mutually exclusive, homogeneous sub-groups called strata","Equal-sized intervals along a sampling frame","Random blocks selected by computer"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Sampling & Estimation"}},{"itemId":"19302529-22f4-5309-adc6-c9e4f8e607e0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cb3dcf74-91a8-4e46-83a1-36846e2c4871","sourceKey":"quiz:108","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The sampling frame is best defined as:","options":["The formula used to determine sample size","The physical list or device from which the sample is actually drawn","The entire target population","The questionnaire used to collect data"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Sampling & Estimation"}},{"itemId":"e7bfd1bf-633d-53e3-9dcd-90329846dcc7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"11f4a012-156e-4754-a2a2-4383c1b3806f","sourceKey":"quiz:109","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following data collection errors arises when the selected sampling unit cannot be contacted?","options":["Response error","Interviewer bias","Non-response error","Coverage error"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Basic Concepts & Data Collection"}},{"itemId":"69a76b4e-7e58-5a84-8923-ddb53415670f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c1499326-b3b7-4087-b716-5621cf9a115d","sourceKey":"quiz:110","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The distinction between 'data' and 'information' in statistics is that:","options":["Data is always numerical whereas information is qualitative","Information is raw and unprocessed while data is processed","Data refers to raw, unprocessed facts while information is data that has been processed into a meaningful form","Both terms are interchangeable in modern usage"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Statistical Terminology"}},{"itemId":"80d7eb43-d5f1-50b9-9f8f-6a612f4ce9ef","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"39fddb98-39a0-46df-b2de-be1a2f8d5e13","sourceKey":"quiz:111","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A frequency distribution's class boundaries differ from class limits in that:","options":["Class boundaries are always whole numbers","Class boundaries eliminate gaps between classes by extending each limit by 0.5 units","Class boundaries are the midpoints of each class","Class boundaries equal the class limits for continuous data"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"f7fdec52-649a-517c-8e57-2b82e62a44ef","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c55d507-83c6-4e46-ace8-b36a4e5887c6","sourceKey":"quiz:112","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An ogive is constructed by plotting:","options":["Class midpoints against frequencies","Cumulative frequencies (or cumulative relative frequencies) against upper class boundaries","Lower class limits against class frequencies","Class widths against relative frequencies"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"dbb50d7a-6fc7-578b-b987-58b684263bc6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"065044ae-da73-4366-8010-bd79bf8bd341","sourceKey":"quiz:113","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following graphical displays is MOST suitable for showing the relationship between two continuous variables?","options":["Pie chart","Scatter diagram","Frequency polygon","Bar chart"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"9a2aa111-408f-58e6-9975-0cfda3cf4f91","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"32f095af-fc60-4671-9bff-2c92fa5367b0","sourceKey":"quiz:114","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The 'less than' ogive for the following data at the upper boundary of the class 30–39 (given f=12, cumulative f up to 29=11) gives cumulative frequency of:","options":["11","12","23","35"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Data Presentation & Frequency Distributions"}},{"itemId":"2f59c493-2dc2-5358-8603-477cfbb7d136","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3b2f683e-ae31-4489-a0e9-6a48628ff5cf","sourceKey":"quiz:115","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In constructing a histogram, the area of each bar represents:","options":["The class midpoint","The class width","The class frequency (or relative frequency)","The cumulative frequency up to that class"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"9adec2b2-c481-577e-ac8a-a53390408d81","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"68da1029-5071-4d3f-8955-25fe72f4fe19","sourceKey":"quiz:116","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A Pareto chart combines which two graphical elements?","options":["A pie chart and a line graph","A bar chart (ordered by frequency) and a cumulative frequency line","A scatter plot and a histogram","A stem-and-leaf plot and a box plot"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"e6037368-084f-5f8b-9c61-9373adaf66a1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3e7fc4ff-cf34-43fa-b489-18436e1e67ae","sourceKey":"quiz:117","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A time-series graph shows a consistent upward movement over years. This pattern is called a:","options":["Seasonal variation","Cyclical variation","Secular trend","Random fluctuation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"832fa2f3-78e8-5238-83ed-3aa95b2518c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c658e497-7a27-41f3-9691-ae907d466371","sourceKey":"quiz:118","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"For a distribution, if the histogram is bell-shaped and symmetric, which of the following is TRUE?","options":["Mean > Median > Mode","Mode > Median > Mean","Mean = Median = Mode","Mean < Median = Mode"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"e971139d-840b-5e14-b620-a84252c326c4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a5e0c692-8169-4ee5-ae0c-73aea3e0a1c9","sourceKey":"quiz:119","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"False-baseline graphs are considered misleading because they:","options":["Use too many colours","Truncate the vertical axis, visually exaggerating differences between values","Combine too many categories in a pie chart","Present data in three dimensions unnecessarily"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Graphical Representation"}},{"itemId":"3886a29c-dca3-57e3-bb1a-8416a3eb5b4d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e572bf76-2927-4730-ae6a-04859efd16ff","sourceKey":"quiz:120","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Data collected over successive time periods on the SAME variable are called:","options":["Cross-sectional data","Time-series data","Panel data","Longitudinal data"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Basic Concepts & Data Collection"}},{"itemId":"a2314e13-9466-5acf-a1a5-007d85d94cfb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"507a07d9-e955-4a03-9412-dee8b02f6db8","sourceKey":"quiz:121","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"For the dataset {4, 7, 7, 9, 12, 15, 15, 15, 20}, the MODE is:","options":["7","9","15","12"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"9abc1052-ba36-564f-9042-12ce53bcf098","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2d82939f-7531-4b87-9b23-ad2d5838fdc7","sourceKey":"quiz:122","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"For the dataset {3, 6, 8, 10, 13}, the MEDIAN is:","options":["6","8","9","10"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"5e261d79-90e1-5da8-b604-a03720eeb6e6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18ed7b9b-44f8-4e7e-85f5-eaff3d9d7439","sourceKey":"quiz:123","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The MEAN of the values 12, 15, 18, 21, 24 is:","options":["18","19","20","21"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"7adf00b5-becc-58eb-bf6d-a6fa76560c42","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6892e1f6-dbbf-4611-be07-e10b192c6d05","sourceKey":"quiz:124","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In a frequency distribution, the MODAL CLASS is the class with:","options":["The smallest class width","The highest frequency","The largest class boundary","The midpoint closest to the mean"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"15085c3c-20bd-5329-bbbe-c5c0d806540a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bb44e01e-c84d-426c-95ee-bfa6bc18c228","sourceKey":"quiz:125","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The GEOMETRIC MEAN is most appropriate for averaging:","options":["Income levels of employees","Annual growth rates or index numbers over time","Examination scores","Temperature readings"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Measures of Central Tendency"}},{"itemId":"84bfd045-ec64-5d82-8db0-b09f02f965ea","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a81ddc67-1dc2-42e5-abbd-0624a4820f59","sourceKey":"quiz:126","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"For the dataset {5, 8, 10, 12, 15}, the MEAN ABSOLUTE DEVIATION (MAD) is:","options":["2.8","3.0","3.2","3.6"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Measures of Dispersion"}},{"itemId":"e2987745-3c14-5129-9caf-8d27b6476580","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1919cceb-2d2e-46a9-8628-94e18d87ac80","sourceKey":"quiz:127","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"If Σ(x − x̄)² = 240 and n = 15, the population STANDARD DEVIATION is:","options":["4","16","√16 = 4","√15"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Measures of Dispersion"}},{"itemId":"48e6992b-9edb-5b2d-9023-a06c5ddcf00c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"06dbdf19-c6a3-4302-8d6d-939586099eff","sourceKey":"quiz:128","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The COEFFICIENT OF VARIATION is useful for comparing dispersion across two datasets because it:","options":["Eliminates the effect of sample size differences","Expresses standard deviation as a percentage of the mean, removing the unit of measurement","Converts all values to z-scores before comparison","Uses the range instead of the standard deviation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Measures of Dispersion"}},{"itemId":"ac3adcc7-a860-51c6-a2e0-5f91d7f62a9f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7705b618-bb1d-4646-bc6b-fa23ba0ab5a6","sourceKey":"quiz:129","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"For a dataset with mean = 50, median = 55, and standard deviation = 10, Pearson's first coefficient of skewness is:","options":["-1.5","-0.5","0.5","1.5"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Skewness & Kurtosis"}},{"itemI