Pi = 50Q - Q^2 - 200. The profit-maximizing output Q is:","options":["10","20","25","50"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Differential Calculus"}},{"itemId":"e0459bd1-2985-505a-9cb3-92d89bf622cd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1213e16d-56b4-409f-a146-8ad73fdcac76","sourceKey":"quiz:77","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The derivative of any constant term is:","options":["1","The constant itself","0","Undefined"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Differential Calculus"}},{"itemId":"21b91abb-d2ec-506d-92e4-bea93c1b9174","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7bc0c7a4-a51d-42af-be94-23f40c56b14e","sourceKey":"quiz:78","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"If the consumption function is C = 100 + 0.8Y, the Marginal Propensity to Consume (MPC) is:","options":["100","0.8","0.2","1.8"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Differential Calculus"}},{"itemId":"1eb4b6e4-f612-504a-9115-76979836c564","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b0bdb1f7-ce64-42b7-8a88-1e224644625c","sourceKey":"quiz:79","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The critical point of f(x) = x^2 - 6x + 9 where f'(x) = 0 is at x =:","options":["1","2","3","6"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Differential Calculus"}},{"itemId":"9bb02541-2050-5e1a-90f4-00e34312a378","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"69c9ff2f-b239-4dca-85bf-301c52632a68","sourceKey":"quiz:80","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The point of diminishing returns for P = -Q^3 + 9Q^2 + 12Q is found by setting P''(Q) = 0. What is Q?","options":["1","2","3","4"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Differential Calculus"}},{"itemId":"ae57238b-9a3b-5b9a-8421-9bc143a980f9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6bfda16d-2c54-4dd8-85d0-dc9475c15a3f","sourceKey":"quiz:81","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Evaluate: integral of (4x^3 - 2x + 5) dx.","options":["x^4 - x^2 + 5x + C","12x^2 - 2 + C","4x^4 - x^2 + 5x + C","x^4 - 2x + 5 + C"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"fc9cc76b-fb1f-503a-a7ff-b68dabb96fc9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a2e25ed4-ff1e-4aeb-bb9b-9d62386c653e","sourceKey":"quiz:82","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"If MC = 10Q + 5 and Fixed Cost = 200, what is the Total Cost function?","options":["10Q + 5 + 200","5Q^2 + 5Q + 200","10Q^2 + 5Q + 200","5Q^2 + 5 + 200"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"b4fdf373-ac3b-57cc-b576-6b81e29f6c1d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f29af5f1-c005-400f-bbe0-327dfabc9b38","sourceKey":"quiz:83","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Evaluate the definite integral of 3x^2 from x=1 to x=3.","options":["18","24","26","27"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"70ddddc5-0706-5002-a631-354916cb8214","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc1ddb57-8fa3-4506-9765-8d079645f04b","sourceKey":"quiz:84","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The area under the curve y = x^2 from x=0 to x=4 is:","options":["16","21.33","32","64"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"06b2ca53-d153-5774-a270-aa0640722d39","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4363b9b2-bfc2-47a6-903c-ba853904542f","sourceKey":"quiz:85","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Consumer Surplus is calculated as the area between:","options":["Supply curve and price line","Demand curve and price line (above price)","Demand curve and supply curve","The x-axis and the revenue curve"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"91b2b2ed-f326-576f-8083-cd42ac25bbc5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4b70a37e-c279-45b2-8ccb-d0688928d444","sourceKey":"quiz:86","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The integral of 1/x dx is:","options":["x^(-1) + C","ln|x| + C","-1/x^2 + C","e^x + C"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"806872d8-c361-5f06-afd2-049f45bcd04a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"67187ab9-5593-420a-acbb-0544ed7f8e04","sourceKey":"quiz:87","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"If MR = 50 - 4Q, then TR = integral of MR. At Q=5, TR is:","options":["150","200","250","300"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"d0b038d4-e7d4-5856-b99c-6cf7cee7325f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae026540-31cf-42b3-851a-662fecb5dcb2","sourceKey":"quiz:88","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The \"Constant of Integration\" C in a Total Cost function represents:","options":["Marginal Cost","Variable Cost","Fixed Cost","Average Cost"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"bdf019fa-73e9-5055-acdf-5c540a74bea7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"536c579c-0451-4383-b698-d5fe99115473","sourceKey":"quiz:89","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The definite integral is the geometrical representation of:","options":["The slope of a curve","The area under the curve between two limits","The tangent line","The rate of change"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"58de642d-7ec5-5060-ae82-6c37f7efe5cc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3d6352b7-69c8-45f6-88a2-dff66655acc7","sourceKey":"quiz:90","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Integrate: integral of e^(3x) + 2 dx.","options":["3e^(3x) + 2x + C","e^(3x)/3 + 2x + C","e^(3x) + 2x + C","3e^(3x) + C"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"964ca7ab-3efd-5649-8bd4-daf43cf870d4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"755f901b-583d-4ec3-8cf3-7dbdf4906fde","sourceKey":"quiz:91","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"If MR = 20 - 0.2Q, the Total Revenue function is:","options":["20Q - 0.1Q^2 + C","20 - 0.2Q + C","-0.2 + C","20Q - 0.2Q^2 + C"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"f6756d92-bd3c-5788-b133-a81038d36494","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"47446090-76e3-4d65-8652-241bd0d4ba07","sourceKey":"quiz:92","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Evaluate the definite integral of (4x - x^2) from x=0 to x=2.","options":["4/3","8/3","4","16/3"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"29f00178-9248-5a9f-a530-f94702b0deae","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"09f9f217-58af-40e5-846c-b7b221f1016a","sourceKey":"quiz:93","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"If MPS = 0.3 and S = 0 when Y = 100, the Saving function S(Y) is:","options":["0.3Y - 30","0.3Y + 30","0.3Y - 100","0.3Y"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"29c5dede-d323-583a-a025-30167a8a9739","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3700af63-0278-4173-8be6-01d28b3bdc38","sourceKey":"quiz:94","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The area between y = x and y = x^2 from x=0 to x=1 is:","options":["1/3","1/6","1/2","1/4"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"d12ab81f-66e7-5c43-b659-9e0c6830a17b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"890bf246-3274-4068-8989-8f23f4d96b2f","sourceKey":"quiz:95","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The integral of 5/x dx is:","options":["5x + C","5 ln|x| + C","-5/x^2 + C","5e^x + C"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"7bbddd5e-bb82-5e0e-b509-0ddcc0ab5302","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0282b9b1-8dd4-471d-8fb9-db973dfd13ec","sourceKey":"quiz:96","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Producer Surplus at Q=5 for supply curve P = 2 + Q where equilibrium price is 7 is:","options":["10","12.5","15","25"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"fbdee403-a7a1-5288-818a-f79ee4ae3fbf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"acba139d-b907-4a16-8d51-b515c84e9c7f","sourceKey":"quiz:97","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The definite integral of f(x) = 10 from x=2 to x=7 is:","options":["10","30","50","70"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"47a39cfd-080d-599d-8e90-ac98621596b4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0275042a-eb7b-4f8b-8e0c-883023a4710c","sourceKey":"quiz:98","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The integral of x^n dx equals (for n not equal to -1):","options":["nx^(n-1) + C","x^(n+1)/(n+1) + C","x^n/n + C","(n+1)x^n + C"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"16272858-e532-5fc0-b656-067a9914aa81","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e798118b-b5a6-47ee-ba26-3c33ef32913b","sourceKey":"quiz:99","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Differentiation finds the rate of change; Integration finds the:","options":["Rate of acceleration","Accumulation of value (area)","Maximum point","Slope of tangent"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"4a931ebf-6d8a-5d87-8a86-68d5bb4de1b9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"258e459b-97ff-478d-8cf5-dc04196bfc50","sourceKey":"quiz:100","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In business, finding TC from MC by integration is an example of moving from:","options":["Total data to marginal data","Marginal data to total data","Average data to total data","Fixed data to variable data"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Integral Calculus"}},{"itemId":"5aad5fe7-ca39-5228-9fd8-a71a4aa69e1c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"course_overview","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"course-overview","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Consumer Behaviour","body":"Study of consumer decision processes, buyer types, and the psychological, cultural, and social factors influencing consumer choices and market behaviour.","topics":[{"chapter":"1-2","description":"Consumer Decision-Making Process"},{"chapter":"3-4","description":"Types of Consumers and Buyer Behavior"},{"chapter":"5-6","description":"Cultural and Social Influences on Consumer Behavior"},{"chapter":"7-8","description":"Personality, Attitudes, and Perception"},{"chapter":"9-10","description":"Communication, Persuasion and Marketing Appeals"}],"examFocus":["Consumer decision-making models","Psychological factors affecting consumer choice","Cultural and social influences","Personality and attitude measurement","Communication theories in marketing","Theoretical explanations and applications"]}},{"itemId":"78bf7a81-885d-5d87-8009-ab27ecdddfbb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:1","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Introduction to Consumer Behaviour","body":"Consumer behaviour is the study of how individuals, groups, and organizations select, buy, use, and dispose of goods, services, ideas, or experiences to satisfy needs and wants. It draws from psychology, sociology, and anthropology to understand purchasing decisions.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Consumer vs. Customer: A customer purchases the product; a consumer is the end-user who benefits from it.","Types of Buyers: Impulse (spontaneous, emotional), Informed (research extensively), Brand Loyal (stick to trusted brands), Price Sensitive (hunt for discounts).","Economic Impact: Consumer spending stimulates production, supports GDP, and drives economic growth.","Online vs. Offline: Online shoppers value convenience and range; offline shoppers prefer tactile product experience."],"examTip":"Remember the distinction between customer (purchaser) and consumer (end-user) - they can be different people."}},{"itemId":"4b7f472f-271b-59de-a5c1-055f16c69c13","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:2","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"The 5-Stage Consumer Decision-Making Process","body":"Consumers move through five distinct stages when making purchase decisions: recognizing a problem, searching for information, evaluating alternatives, making a purchase decision, and evaluating post-purchase satisfaction.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["1. Problem Recognition: Triggered by internal stimuli (hunger) or external stimuli (advertisement).","2. Information Search: Internal (memory/past experiences) or external (reviews, friends, ads).","3. Evaluation of Alternatives: Comparing brands based on attributes, features, benefits, and costs.","4. Purchase Decision: Actual choice of what to buy, which brand, where to buy, and how to pay.","5. Post-Purchase Behaviour: Evaluating satisfaction - critical for loyalty and word-of-mouth."],"examTip":"Memorize the 5 stages in order. Post-purchase evaluation is critical because it drives future loyalty and recommendations."}},{"itemId":"2ad2e2e7-bc1b-5edd-8923-be6cf098777e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:3","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Cultural and Social Influences","body":"Culture is the broadest and deepest influence on consumer behaviour, dictating fundamental tastes, norms, values, and traditions. Social class and sub-cultures provide additional layers of influence on purchasing decisions.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Culture: Dictates fundamental tastes, norms, values, traditions (food, clothing, holidays).","Sub-Culture: Smaller groups providing specific identification (nationality, religion, racial groups, geographic regions).","Social Class: Division based on wealth, education, status - heavily affects media consumption and product choices.","Social Stratification: Measured by occupation, income, and education."],"examTip":"Culture is the BROADEST influence. Social class is measured using occupation, income, and education."}},{"itemId":"fd8f4fe0-5d7e-5cd5-80bf-5941f8da012b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:4","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Reference Groups and External Influences","body":"Reference groups serve as standards for evaluating behavior and purchasing decisions. They exert influence through informational guidance, normative pressure to conform, and value-expressive inspiration.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Reference Groups: Any group used as a standard for evaluating one's own behavior.","Aspirational Groups: Groups a person wants to join or emulate (celebrities, sports teams).","Dissociative Groups: Groups a person actively wants to avoid being associated with.","Informational Influence: Seeking explicit expert opinions (doctor, mechanic).","Utilitarian Influence: Buying to fit in, be rewarded, or avoid punishment by peers.","Value-Expressive Influence: Adopting group values to enhance self-image (eco-friendly products)."],"examTip":"Know the three types of group influence: Informational (expert advice), Utilitarian (fitting in), Value-Expressive (self-image)."}},{"itemId":"b0cac564-3ed1-5087-86e4-2e4386ea8de4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:5","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Individual Psychology: Motivation and Perception","body":"Motivation is the driving force behind consumer actions. Maslow's Hierarchy explains that humans satisfy basic needs first. Perception involves how consumers select, organize, and interpret information through selective attention, distortion, and retention.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Motivation: The driving force that impels consumers to action.","Maslow's Hierarchy: Physiological → Safety → Social → Esteem → Self-Actualization (satisfied in order).","Selective Attention: Consumers screen out most stimuli and notice only relevant information.","Selective Distortion: Consumers twist information to fit existing preconceptions.","Selective Retention: Consumers remember good points about liked products and forget competitors' advantages."],"examTip":"Maslow's hierarchy must be satisfied from bottom to top. The three selective processes are barriers marketers must overcome."}},{"itemId":"38bf5b23-0046-5e49-bb28-6aceb3aa35da","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:6","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Learning and Attitudes","body":"Consumer learning results from experience and influences future behavior. Classical conditioning associates brands with positive stimuli, while operant conditioning uses rewards. Attitudes consist of cognitive, affective, and conative components.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Classical Conditioning: Learning to associate a brand with positive stimulus (music, imagery).","Instrumental (Operant) Conditioning: Rewarding specific behavior (loyalty points for purchases).","Tri-component Attitude Model: (1) Cognitive (knowledge), (2) Affective (feelings), (3) Conative (action likelihood).","Cognitive Dissonance: Post-purchase tension or regret when expectations aren't met.","Attitudes: Enduring favorable or unfavorable evaluations toward objects/brands."],"examTip":"Classical = association, Operant = reward. The conative component predicts likelihood of purchase action."}},{"itemId":"407f56f9-0a82-53e1-88fe-8f0c8e38e796","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:7","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Communication and Persuasive Techniques","body":"Effective marketing communication requires understanding the five components: sender, message, medium, receiver, and feedback. Credibility is critical - informal sources (friends/family) typically have higher credibility than formal sources (advertisers).","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Five Components: Sender (initiator), Message (encoded thought), Medium (channel), Receiver (target audience), Feedback (response).","Credibility: Perceived honesty and objectivity of the source.","Informal Sources: Friends/family have higher credibility (no financial gain perceived).","Formal Sources: Commercial advertisers have lower credibility.","Communication Barriers: Psychological noise (competing messages, distractions) and selective perception."],"examTip":"Remember the 5 components. Informal sources > Formal sources in credibility because of perceived objectivity."}},{"itemId":"cde2d36f-4a3d-514b-8c7b-83d0b15b450a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:8","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Promotional Strategy and the AHP Model","body":"The Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) helps determine optimal promotional mix. For Nigerian insurance companies, advertising and personal selling were found most effective, while sales promotion received minimal investment priority.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["AHP Model: Determines best mix of promotional elements based on weighted criteria.","Best for Nigerian Insurance: Advertising (builds brand awareness and trust for intangible services).","Second Most Effective: Personal Selling (direct relationships, builds buyer conviction).","Minimal Investment: Sales Promotion (lowest direct impact on insurance policy sales).","Seven Promotional Tools: Advertising, Personal Selling, Sales Promotion, Public Relations, Publicity, Sponsorship, Direct Marketing."],"examTip":"AHP study: Advertising = best, Personal Selling = second, Sales Promotion = minimal investment."}},{"itemId":"1672c927-5703-5d2b-a865-e28711a08029","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:9","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Modern Decision-Making and Digital Influences","body":"Recent research shows consumer decision-making has shifted from sequential stages to continuous digital exploration. The Stimulus-Organism-Response (SOR) framework explains how external stimuli affect internal states and behavioral outcomes.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Shift to Continuous Exploration: Social media and digital platforms blur traditional decision-making stages.","SOR Framework: External stimuli (web design, social proof) → Internal states (emotions, cognition) → Behavioral outcomes (purchase).","Green Purchasing: Increasing priority due to health consciousness and sustainability concerns.","Choice Architecture: The way choices are presented to influence decision-making (nudging).","Digital Nudging: Subtly guiding consumer behavior in online environments (defaults, framing, badges)."],"examTip":"SOR framework: Stimulus → Organism → Response. Digital nudging uses choice architecture to guide behavior."}},{"itemId":"02a9f9dd-248e-5225-bff0-4f96a2db14d5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:10","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Pricing Strategies and Consumer Value Perception","body":"Pricing strategies must align with consumer value perceptions. Customer-value-based pricing starts with analyzing consumer needs rather than costs. Competitive advantage mediates the relationship between pricing and performance.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Cost-Based Pricing: Setting prices based on total expenses of providing the product/service.","Competition-Based Pricing: Setting prices based on what competitors charge.","Customer-Value-Based Pricing: Setting prices based on perceived value to the consumer.","Penetration Pricing: Low initial prices to attract large number of buyers quickly.","Skimming Pricing: High initial prices for new, unique products.","Psychological Pricing: Using prices like $9.99 instead of $10.00 to influence perception."],"examTip":"Customer-value-based pricing is most customer-centric - it starts with analyzing consumer needs, not costs."}},{"itemId":"4f30ad18-a227-52ec-8530-1387b4a0cda7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b2cbe46b-eb1b-426c-add7-a9cdfcc11447","sourceKey":"quiz:1","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Consumer behaviour is best defined as:","options":["The study of how companies manipulate people","The behavior consumers display in searching for, purchasing, using, and disposing of products","The mathematical calculation of price elasticity","The psychology of employee management"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Introduction to Consumer Behaviour"}},{"itemId":"fc979dbb-b10a-5777-8b21-f6b63ce498fc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"33a0df98-c0b8-464a-9780-2a08831369bf","sourceKey":"quiz:2","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the difference between a consumer and a customer?","options":["There is no difference","A customer purchases the product; a consumer is the end-user who benefits from it","A consumer always pays more","A customer never uses the product"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Introduction to Consumer Behaviour"}},{"itemId":"3a49a05d-9493-5ccb-ba1a-8cd3d53d6ec5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"65eaf7dc-0e38-443d-91dc-5f68618cb2a2","sourceKey":"quiz:3","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Impulse buyers are characterized by:","options":["Extensive research before purchase","Spontaneous buying based on emotion","Always buying the cheapest option","Never making purchases"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Introduction to Consumer Behaviour"}},{"itemId":"a18d83c8-0d0e-5118-afff-f738fbdbf0af","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a8e5c684-4281-45c8-907c-4d5c84682a76","sourceKey":"quiz:4","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Brand loyal buyers will:","options":["Switch brands for any discount","Stick to trusted brands regardless of price","Never buy the same product twice","Only shop online"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Introduction to Consumer Behaviour"}},{"itemId":"5e4787e6-03bf-51bb-8a1b-b2382ae2bffc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5f9e05d1-bea5-4dd6-ac7e-2760ab10fb37","sourceKey":"quiz:5","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Consumer spending is important because it:","options":["Has no economic impact","Stimulates production and drives GDP growth","Only benefits manufacturers","Reduces economic growth"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Introduction to Consumer Behaviour"}},{"itemId":"b1d22e4a-449d-5ae4-87db-8cb6252ee18c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"368ed5ff-4ecf-4abb-b69b-21a587baed81","sourceKey":"quiz:6","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The FIRST stage of the consumer decision-making process is:","options":["Information search","Need/Problem recognition","Evaluation of alternatives","Purchase decision"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Decision-Making Process"}},{"itemId":"c471abbd-5681-5d98-9774-db43bdf089ac","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"674df65b-13cd-4265-bee0-0e75a690bfc9","sourceKey":"quiz:7","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Internal stimuli that trigger problem recognition include:","options":["Seeing an advertisement","Feeling hungry","Friend recommendation","Store display"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Decision-Making Process"}},{"itemId":"4661be05-e6fc-5d5b-95cc-343f8fa4ffec","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9edd56e9-5c8d-49f6-b265-ded68e477fac","sourceKey":"quiz:8","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An external search for information includes:","options":["Relying on memory","Reading online reviews and asking friends","Personal past experiences only","Instinct"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Decision-Making Process"}},{"itemId":"8d9da4fe-bce3-5d5c-8acb-b0dcaea7a8a0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d7d8887d-45b8-4a2a-9b0e-638d453cf24a","sourceKey":"quiz:9","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the evaluation of alternatives stage, consumers:","options":["Buy the first product they see","Compare different brands based on attributes and value","Ignore all information","Only consider price"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Decision-Making Process"}},{"itemId":"b4fc986f-3183-5bbf-b7a3-b4999979763e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9a3a0e79-2c1f-4440-b0e8-714376071704","sourceKey":"quiz:10","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Post-purchase behaviour is critical because:","options":["It has no impact on future sales","Satisfied customers become loyal and generate positive word-of-mouth","Consumers always forget their purchases","It only matters for expensive items"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Decision-Making Process"}},{"itemId":"5e33bd76-79dc-58a1-a591-c0095d692e88","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a629492a-9832-48e5-8358-649c8ab586e5","sourceKey":"quiz:11","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Culture is the broadest influence on consumer behaviour because it dictates:","options":["Only language","Fundamental tastes, norms, values, and traditions","Nothing important","Only clothing choices"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Group & Cultural Influences"}},{"itemId":"4dd2f91d-faea-52d1-bd29-7c397ab9c4f0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7455a373-5a00-42b0-b027-74f5e48b312c","sourceKey":"quiz:12","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A sub-culture is:","options":["The main culture of a country","A smaller group within a culture providing specific identification (nationality, religion, region)","A group with no culture","Only applicable to rural areas"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Group & Cultural Influences"}},{"itemId":"296b2a8e-f1b1-5e82-b012-c4f705f0be20","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"da327322-fd8e-4c31-adbb-955f91ff8957","sourceKey":"quiz:13","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Social class affects consumer behaviour through:","options":["No impact whatsoever","Wealth, education, values influencing media consumption and product choices","Only job title","Physical appearance"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Group & Cultural Influences"}},{"itemId":"eed9cdf0-4890-53ed-bfa2-c724fa99f6a6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1c1d9485-0552-494e-927d-823dc331696e","sourceKey":"quiz:14","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Reference groups are:","options":["Groups used as a standard for evaluating one's own behavior or purchasing decisions","Groups with no influence","Only family members","Fictional characters"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Group & Cultural Influences"}},{"itemId":"f364eb86-6c44-534c-b486-9281c9e847e8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"18600cc3-097d-484d-8e98-86797786babe","sourceKey":"quiz:15","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Aspirational groups are:","options":["Groups a person actively avoids","Groups a person wants to join or emulate","Groups with no members","Family only"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Group & Cultural Influences"}},{"itemId":"7bce537b-beac-501a-a252-94a89cf35fe6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0332336e-b916-49d5-9682-6a79206f5975","sourceKey":"quiz:16","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Informational influence occurs when:","options":["A person ignores all advice","A person seeks explicit expert opinions (e.g., doctor, mechanic)","A person buys randomly","A person avoids information"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Group & Cultural Influences"}},{"itemId":"543a6b4b-430e-5db3-9d47-38cd3dd24807","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cda87dd8-74c4-40e0-8476-5b52eb7da3b6","sourceKey":"quiz:17","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Utilitarian (Normative) influence involves:","options":["Independent decision-making","Buying something to fit in or be rewarded/avoid punishment by friends or family","Avoiding all social groups","Only considering price"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Group & Cultural Influences"}},{"itemId":"cd965a3e-7758-52f4-aced-556b2ddae895","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"938f0ada-d2f4-4144-8778-9ad7c1a7a5c5","sourceKey":"quiz:18","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Value-expressive influence means:","options":["Ignoring group values","Adopting values of a group to enhance self-image (e.g., buying eco-friendly products)","Expressing anger","Hiding your values"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Group & Cultural Influences"}},{"itemId":"d9ab01fc-ee90-5b7f-bf03-5f39c2335dba","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3048441c-34b5-4460-ae1b-a53792a44faa","sourceKey":"quiz:19","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Social stratification (social class) is typically measured using:","options":["Height and weight","Occupation, income, and education","Favorite color","Shoe size"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Group & Cultural Influences"}},{"itemId":"b3e2a5fb-ae17-575d-bb34-f9afee36205d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9edc6c71-290c-4d1c-a427-dea79a903930","sourceKey":"quiz:20","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Opinion leaders are individuals who:","options":["Work for the government only","Influence the opinions and behaviors of others regarding specific product categories","Never share their opinions","Only buy expensive products"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Group & Cultural Influences"}},{"itemId":"7317a8eb-04b1-5b8a-a1a1-05fc1978bcf9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"49b5fd45-8959-40f3-8915-d91cf9213381","sourceKey":"quiz:21","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs, which need must be satisfied first?","options":["Self-actualization","Physiological needs (food, water)","Esteem needs","Social needs"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"08dd09c2-b6ce-5968-9b97-95d1ca50e86d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fd766262-d42b-495e-bba4-8ea46e836feb","sourceKey":"quiz:22","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Motivation is best described as:","options":["The final purchase act","The driving force within individuals that impels them to action","A set of advertisements","The price consumers pay"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"8176c440-f19d-5e10-a081-110b4af6760c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"27e90ae1-8ca6-4b78-8947-b972d9e95fcd","sourceKey":"quiz:23","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Selective attention means consumers:","options":["Pay attention to everything","Screen out most stimuli and only notice what is relevant to them","Never pay attention","Only notice expensive products"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"e13f615d-425b-5530-948b-8aa8ad567b3d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"424a158b-1d27-4368-8ef8-40280ce90f6f","sourceKey":"quiz:24","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Selective distortion occurs when consumers:","options":["Remember everything accurately","Twist or interpret information to fit their existing preconceptions","Ignore all information","Only buy one brand"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"0ca5a1d6-71e9-5046-8fdf-2da4fc280048","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"46326cf6-4f9b-430a-b10b-adc00f6f11b2","sourceKey":"quiz:25","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Selective retention means consumers:","options":["Remember everything equally","Tend to remember good points about products they like and forget good points about competitors","Have perfect memory","Never remember any advertisements"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"5446b422-5c45-59b3-8da2-947bda3acd82","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4277ede5-5949-421d-acdf-6c34f9cce314","sourceKey":"quiz:26","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Classical conditioning involves:","options":["Complex problem-solving","Learning to associate a brand with a specific positive stimulus (like music or imagery)","Ignoring all advertisements","Genetic inheritance"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"c461eea7-ce41-59b3-acd0-6f214727ef33","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7571f0d-451a-4e21-a278-4f7f91372013","sourceKey":"quiz:27","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Instrumental (Operant) conditioning occurs when:","options":["Consumers are punished for purchases","A consumer is rewarded for specific behavior (e.g., loyalty points)","Consumers forget brands","No learning occurs"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"fddaa8b3-1417-5882-bebf-2c319cf1f71c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e6a0d33a-84d0-460a-ae2a-5e63f5b6e4c7","sourceKey":"quiz:28","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The Tri-component Attitude Model consists of:","options":["Price, Place, Promotion","Cognitive, Affective, Conative components","Past, Present, Future","Internal, External, Neutral"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"18fb1965-bda7-5426-8fec-c64dbc28a8f7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5ca71946-d39f-40b8-a2b5-a54ac684d85c","sourceKey":"quiz:29","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The conative component of attitude refers to:","options":["What a person knows about a brand","How a person feels about a brand","The likelihood that a person will take action (purchase)","Brand logo design"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"0a04b808-05a4-5994-a329-42888b845fcc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"31595d44-38d7-4216-b2a9-8f0f3c51fe1b","sourceKey":"quiz:30","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Cognitive dissonance occurs:","options":["Before making a purchase","When a consumer experiences post-purchase tension or regret","Never","Only with free products"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"084a21e2-0f70-5593-bdff-9c2a7c273a93","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aeba837c-8dee-4b1d-a63f-8d9aa46c634c","sourceKey":"quiz:31","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Communication is best defined as:","options":["Only written messages","Transmission of a message from sender to receiver through a medium","Talking to yourself","Silence"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"4842f93b-c924-51aa-a890-dc004384f875","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"90044d2a-6ce1-4d50-b5da-0c48c057c758","sourceKey":"quiz:32","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The five components of communication are:","options":["Price, Product, Place, Promotion, People","Sender, Message, Medium, Receiver, Feedback","Past, Present, Future, Now, Later","Yes, No, Maybe, Always, Never"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"b665c66d-5173-5750-a32d-a3cffef77f06","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"56e30cd0-ca9c-4e4a-ad27-529666aea6d7","sourceKey":"quiz:33","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Why do informal sources (friends/family) typically have higher credibility than formal sources (advertisers)?","options":["They are smarter","Consumers perceive them as having nothing to gain financially from the recommendation","They are louder","They have better education"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"d2c7c4c5-61d1-57d5-8816-0994b525ddbf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"19b193d3-6b03-48ef-b141-a68eb706a4ca","sourceKey":"quiz:34","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Psychological noise in communication refers to:","options":["Loud sounds","Competing advertising messages and distractions","Music in stores","Silence"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"ac4fb4ac-4b59-5b24-97fa-8d6fff6cba2a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fdd65df0-488d-4c90-b4f8-172aef57c54a","sourceKey":"quiz:35","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Marketers overcome communication barriers by:","options":["Giving up","Repeating messages and using highly credible spokespersons","Hiding information","Reducing product quality"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"9ce93da5-fa2a-58bd-89c0-4f234817aed9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d8cf05f9-85ce-4f07-bd59-a0185d24a615","sourceKey":"quiz:36","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which promotional tool was determined to be best suited for Nigerian insurance companies in the AHP study?","options":["Sales Promotion","Advertising","Sponsorship","Direct Marketing"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"6bcb175f-ae89-5e81-8400-f2207a94d29f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7a402017-4ca5-4eb4-9428-d24afdaef69e","sourceKey":"quiz:37","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Personal selling is particularly effective because it:","options":["Is the cheapest form","Involves direct relationships and builds buyer conviction","Requires no training","Is non-personal communication"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"e863a575-07a3-5d84-aeda-70b7ad9b43e9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9583f7e4-f94b-46eb-a4e9-bc5e7de8fcf3","sourceKey":"quiz:38","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The main function of promotion is to:","options":["Distract consumers","Inform and persuade the target market to act positively","Reduce quality","Replace product development"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"47a18fac-d6eb-5c03-aeb4-3a06eca0d08d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"16a75955-9be8-4184-86e2-a01589c27ddf","sourceKey":"quiz:39","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Advertising is considered a:","options":["Personal communication form","Non-personal communication form","Private conversation","Secret message"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"c16a59bc-495e-5511-bb73-fca0e6b8dea9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"30a760f2-f22e-4903-871e-eaaeb3424a92","sourceKey":"quiz:40","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Publicity is defined as information that is:","options":["Always paid for by the organization","Not directly paid for by the organization","Only in newspapers","Never shared"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"1b57bd7e-d252-5281-82aa-116cae105783","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"80eb9b36-06d3-4f53-83b8-19cd82fcc884","sourceKey":"quiz:41","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The Stimulus-Organism-Response (SOR) framework explains how:","options":["Only price affects sales","External stimuli (like web design) affect internal states and lead to behavioral outcomes","Plants grow","Robots function"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Decision-Making Process"}},{"itemId":"01815816-7913-5e3b-918c-03b09ea1fe7e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ba8b617e-8965-48fb-bddf-aaf7419fdf98","sourceKey":"quiz:42","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Modern consumers are increasingly prioritizing green purchasing due to:","options":["Lower prices only","Health consciousness and sustainability concerns","Lack of other options","Government mandates only"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Decision-Making Process"}},{"itemId":"326336c9-ffa1-5a5e-95b7-2688e33d9e46","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d51a6040-ae41-4d08-b44b-553f8c3bda9e","sourceKey":"quiz:43","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Choice architecture refers to:","options":["Designing physical store layouts","The way choices are presented to influence decision-making (nudging)","Building architecture","Home design"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Decision-Making Process"}},{"itemId":"767e9a71-65d7-58af-ab16-ccf2aab8434a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ea97b12d-9321-4a27-93cb-dfc3f5ebf755","sourceKey":"quiz:44","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Digital nudging techniques are used to:","options":["Force consumers to buy","Subtly guide consumer behavior in online environments","Delete data","Slow internet"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Decision-Making Process"}},{"itemId":"33cded1b-d6dc-5a2f-b611-c55429d844c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"96af7a89-01e0-4d0d-899f-257062391591","sourceKey":"quiz:45","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The Theory of Planned Behavior is used to understand:","options":["Impulse buying only","The link between beliefs, intentions, and behavior","Manufacturing schedules","Why people stop shopping"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Decision-Making Process"}},{"itemId":"9dcfe105-bce3-5d0c-a523-c0b9971e08dd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4a0b4f5b-e955-4c6e-aa3d-ab07b71c993f","sourceKey":"quiz:46","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Heuristic processing in consumer behavior refers to:","options":["In-depth scientific analysis","Mental shortcuts used to make quick decisions","Computer programming","Mathematical formulas"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"e1f3b863-0540-5963-82e8-6f7b25b17c2b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8187eb97-e8c4-4adf-96d5-f0aa16d7281f","sourceKey":"quiz:47","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The Dual-Process Theory suggests consumers use:","options":["Two credit cards","System 1 (Intuitive/Fast) and System 2 (Analytical/Slow) thinking","Only digital tools","No thinking"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Individual Psychology"}},{"itemId":"d502afbb-3978-5b6b-8387-a397a3a39300","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"042a48b3-29cb-46b5-9a16-49eab6dccf37","sourceKey":"quiz:48","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Market segmentation is the process of:","options":["Selling to everyone the same way","Dividing a market into distinct groups of buyers with different needs","Closing stores","Pricing products"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Group & Cultural Influences"}},{"itemId":"64d9dba1-196d-5af1-83f3-b0c8900c736e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"af32364e-9288-46c5-a32a-8359ee1b1d2f","sourceKey":"quiz:49","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Brand loyalty is characterized by:","options":["Buying whatever is cheapest","Consistent preference and repeat purchase of a specific brand","Only buying once","Avoiding brands"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"471aee0a-a976-5b34-ad1b-37ae79b0a14c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a48145a2-aeda-4494-b40c-6fadd3f244b8","sourceKey":"quiz:50","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Consumer research is essential because it:","options":["Makes companies look smart","Identifies unmet needs and improves marketing effectiveness","Is legally required everywhere","Has no value"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Communication & Persuasion"}},{"itemId":"fc9a0adc-5f6f-5c98-a35f-7473c94a400a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"course_overview","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"course-overview","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Financial Accounting I","body":"Fundamentals of financial accounting under IFRS: the IASB conceptual framework and the key standards governing revenue, financial-statement presentation, inventories, accounting policies, property plant and equipment, government grants, and borrowing costs.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"IASB Framework for the Preparation and Presentation of Financial Statements"},{"chapter":"2","description":"IFRS 15 - Revenue from Contracts with Customers"},{"chapter":"3","description":"IAS 1 - Presentation of Financial Statements"},{"chapter":"4","description":"IAS 2 - Accounting for Inventory"},{"chapter":"5","description":"IAS 8 - Accounting Policies, Changes in Estimates and Errors"},{"chapter":"6","description":"IAS 16 - Property, Plant, and Equipment (PPE)"},{"chapter":"7","description":"IAS 20 - Government Grants"},{"chapter":"8","description":"IAS 23 - Borrowing Costs"}],"examFocus":["IASB Framework qualitative characteristics","IFRS 15 five-step revenue recognition model","IAS 1 complete set of financial statements","IAS 2 inventory valuation (lower of cost and NRV)","IAS 8 retrospective vs prospective application","IAS 16 depreciation calculations (straight-line & reducing balance)","IAS 20 government grants recognition rules","IAS 23 borrowing costs capitalization"]}},{"itemId":"5eb40a2c-bcd4-5574-a16f-24e35c1b47d5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:1","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"IASB Framework for the Preparation and Presentation of Financial Statements","body":"The Conceptual Framework is not an accounting standard itself, but it provides the foundational rules, concepts, and definitions that the International Accounting Standards Board (IASB) uses to develop IFRS. Its main objective is to ensure financial statements provide transparent, high-quality, and useful information to investors and creditors.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Fundamental Qualitative Characteristics: Information must be Relevant (capable of influencing user decisions) and a Faithful Representation (complete, neutral, and free from error).","Enhancing Qualitative Characteristics: Comparability, Verifiability, Timeliness, and Understandability.","Definition of an Asset: A resource controlled by the entity as a result of past events from which future economic benefits are expected to flow."],"examTip":"Know the difference between fundamental (Relevance and Faithful Representation) and enhancing qualitative characteristics."}},{"itemId":"30197d9b-d3db-5a45-b0b4-2a07215aade0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:2","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Introduction to IFRS 15 - Revenue from Contracts with Customers","body":"IFRS 15 establishes a single, comprehensive framework for determining when to recognize revenue and how much to recognize. The core principle is that revenue is recognized when control over goods or services is transferred to the customer.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["The 5-Step Model: (1) Identify the contract, (2) Identify performance obligations, (3) Determine the transaction price, (4) Allocate the transaction price, (5) Recognize revenue when control passes.","A contract only exists if collection of consideration is probable. If a customer has severe liquidity issues, you cannot recognize a contract.","Performance obligations are distinct promises to transfer goods/services (e.g., software license and technical support are separate).","Transaction price includes fixed and variable consideration (like volume discounts).","Control passes either at a point in time (goods) or over time (services).","Formula: Allocated Price = (Standalone Price ÷ Total Standalone Prices) × Transaction Price","🧮 CALCULATOR TIP: For bundle price allocation - Calculate percentage first (500 ÷ 800 = 0.625), leave in calculator, then multiply by bundle price (0.625 × 700 = 437.5). This avoids rounding errors."],"examTip":"Memorize all 5 steps - this is almost guaranteed to appear. Know when revenue is recognized \"at a point in time\" vs \"over time\"."}},{"itemId":"bd294dd5-b14f-5b96-ac88-e0b616959c46","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:3","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"IAS 1 - Presentation of Financial Statements","body":"IAS 1 sets the overall requirements, structure, and minimum content for general-purpose financial statements to ensure comparability with past periods and other entities.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Complete Set of Financial Statements: (1) Statement of Financial Position, (2) Statement of Profit or Loss and OCI, (3) Statement of Changes in Equity, (4) Statement of Cash Flows, (5) Notes.","Current vs. Non-Current: An item is \"current\" if expected to be realized/settled within 12 months after the reporting period, held primarily for trading, or part of the normal operating cycle.","Expenses Classification: Expenses can be presented by Nature (depreciation, raw materials, wages) or by Function/Cost of Sales (cost of sales, administrative, distribution)."],"examTip":"Know all 5 components and the strict current vs. non-current classification rules."}},{"itemId":"4fac2aba-ca43-567a-b2a8-3585165894bd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:4","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"IAS 2 - Accounting for Inventory","body":"IAS 2 dictates how to value inventories (raw materials, work in progress, and finished goods). The fundamental principle is that inventory must always be valued at the lower of cost and Net Realizable Value (NRV).","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Valuation Rule: Inventory = Lower of Cost and NRV.","NRV Formula: Estimated Sales Value - Estimated Costs to Sell.","Cost Components: Purchase price (less trade discounts), direct taxes, delivery/carriage inwards, and conversion costs (direct labor + factory overheads).","Strict Exclusions: Abnormal waste, storage costs (unless necessary for production), administrative overheads, selling costs, and interest.","Prohibited Methods: FIFO and Weighted Average are permitted; LIFO is strictly prohibited.","Write-down: If NRV falls below cost, write down to NRV and recognize the loss as an expense immediately.","🧮 CALCULATOR TIP: For NRV - If Cost = ₦100,000, Sales Value = ₦220,000, Selling Costs = ₦104,000. First calculate NRV (220,000 - 104,000 = ₦116,000). Then pick LOWER value between Cost (100k) and NRV (116k). Answer: ₦100,000."],"examTip":"Lower of cost and NRV is THE rule. LIFO is NEVER allowed. Know what to exclude (abnormal waste, admin costs, selling costs)."}},{"itemId":"fbd5318d-3689-51c0-bcd9-e988ec5fd8d5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:5","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Introduction to IAS 8 - Accounting Policies, Changes in Estimates and Errors","body":"IAS 8 prescribes the criteria for selecting accounting policies and the accounting treatment for changes in those policies, changes in estimates, and corrections of prior period errors.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Change in Accounting Policy: (e.g., switching from cost model to fair value model). Applied RETROSPECTIVELY - adjust opening balances and past comparative amounts as if the new policy had always been applied.","Change in Accounting Estimate: (e.g., changing the useful life of an asset, or changing from straight-line to reducing balance depreciation). Applied PROSPECTIVELY - only affects current and future years.","Errors: Prior period errors are also corrected retrospectively."],"examTip":"Key distinction: Policy changes = retrospective. Estimate changes = prospective. This is very common in exams."}},{"itemId":"7a831529-9b24-5e62-a49c-4239e9394c64","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:6","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"IAS 16 - Property, Plant, and Equipment (PPE)","body":"IAS 16 deals with the recognition, initial measurement, and depreciation of tangible non-current assets held for use in production, rental, or administration for more than one period.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Initial Cost: Purchase price + non-refundable taxes + directly attributable costs (site preparation, installation, delivery, testing, professional fees). Exclude general admin costs, training, and regular repair costs.","Depreciation: The systematic allocation of the depreciable amount (Cost - Scrap Value) over its useful life, obeying the matching concept.","Straight-Line Method: Depreciation = (Cost - Scrap Value) / Useful Life","Reducing Balance Method Rate: R = 1 - ⁿ√(Scrap Value / Cost)","Disposal: Profit/Loss on Disposal = Sales Proceeds - Carrying Amount (NBV)","🧮 CALCULATOR TIP (Reducing Balance): For 5-year life, scrap ₦163,840, cost ₦500,000. Type: (163840 ÷ 500000) ^ (1 ÷ 5) = 0.8. Then: 1 - 0.8 = 0.2 = 20% rate.","🧮 CALCULATOR TIP (Straight-Line): Example - Cost ₦150,000, Scrap ₦30,000, Life 4 years. Calculation: (150,000 - 30,000) ÷ 4 = ₦30,000 annual depreciation."],"examTip":"Know how to calculate both Straight-line and Reducing Balance depreciation. Practice the formulas with examples."}},{"itemId":"506f7eb2-b2f8-5240-966c-cc555e9e962a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:7","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"IAS 20 - Government Grants","body":"IAS 20 handles the accounting for government assistance and transfers of resources to an entity tied to compliance with operating conditions.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Recognition: Do not recognize a grant until there is reasonable assurance that the entity will comply with conditions AND the grant will be received.","Grants Related to Assets (Two Methods): (1) Capital Approach: Deduct the grant from the asset cost, lowering annual depreciation. (2) Income Approach: Keep asset at full cost, treat grant as Deferred Income and release to profit/loss over the asset's life to offset depreciation.","Grants Related to Income: Recognized in the income statement systematically over periods in which related costs/expenses are incurred."],"examTip":"Remember the two approaches for asset-related grants: deduct from cost OR treat as deferred income."}},{"itemId":"46259144-f5d2-5bb3-9325-5c706cd0dc07","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:8","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"IAS 23 - Borrowing Costs","body":"IAS 23 determines whether interest, finance charges, and amortization of discounts on debt should be expensed immediately or capitalized (added to the cost of an asset).","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Capitalization Rule: You MUST capitalize borrowing costs that are directly attributable to the acquisition or construction of a Qualifying Asset.","Qualifying Asset: An asset that takes a substantial period of time (like 2-5 years) to get ready for its intended use or sale.","Idle vs. Active Periods: Only capitalize interest during the active construction period. If construction is halted (e.g., strike) or hasn't started, expense the interest.","General Borrowings: If a pool of general loans is used, calculate a weighted average interest rate.","Capitalized Interest Formula (Specific Loan): Total Loan Principal × Interest Rate × (Active Construction Months / 12)","Weighted Average Rate: (Total Interest on all loans ÷ Total Principal on all loans) × 100","🧮 CALCULATOR TIP (Weighted Average): Add ALL loan interest first (₦1.5m + ₦1.2m = ₦2.7m). Then divide by total principal (₦70m). Result: 2.7 ÷ 70 = 3.86% rate. Apply this rate to construction expenditures."],"examTip":"Key question: \"Is this a qualifying asset?\" If yes = capitalize interest. If no = expense. Know when to stop capitalizing (when active construction stops)."}},{"itemId":"7055f2ab-06ae-53de-9cdc-f1841358d1df","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:9","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Bookkeeping Fundamentals and Errors","body":"Bookkeeping involves recording all financial transactions systematically. Understanding different types of errors and how to correct them is crucial for maintaining accurate financial records.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Accounting Equation: Assets = Capital + Liabilities","Error of Principle: Transaction entered in wrong class of account (e.g., motor expenses debited to motor vehicles).","Error of Transposition: Digits wrongly swapped (e.g., ₦4,466 written as ₦4,664).","Suspense Account: Temporary account opened when trial balance totals do not agree.","Unpresented Cheques: Cheques drawn but not yet presented to the bank for payment.","Bank Reconciliation: Bank charges not in cash book are deducted from cash book balance.","Imprest System: Fixed petty cash float maintained and exactly reimbursed.","Books of Original Entry: Purchases Daybook (credit purchases), Journal (non-standard entries)."],"examTip":"Know the different types of errors. Error of Principle is when you put a transaction in the wrong CLASS of account."}},{"itemId":"06c82082-6f32-58ed-8584-985f733f7871","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"note","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"topic-note:10","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Cost and Management Accounting Fundamentals","body":"Cost accounting involves classifying, recording, and allocating costs to determine product costs and support management decisions. Key concepts include prime cost, conversion cost, and break-even analysis.","noteType":"topic_note","keyPoints":["Prime Cost: Direct Material + Direct Labour + Direct Expenses","Conversion Cost: Cost of converting raw materials into finished products (Direct Labour + Factory Overheads).","Sunk Cost: A cost that has already been incurred and cannot be recovered.","Marginal Costing: Fixed production overheads treated as period costs and written off to Profit or Loss.","Absorption Costing: Fixed production overheads charged to cost units (included in product cost).","Break-even Point (units): Fixed Cost ÷ Contribution per unit","Margin of Safety: Budgeted Sales - Break-even Sales","Economic Order Quantity (EOQ): Considers Annual Demand, Ordering Cost, Holding Cost.","Variable Cost: Varies directly in proportion to activity level.","Fixed Cost: Remains constant in total regardless of output volume (within relevant range).","Contribution: Sales Revenue - Variable Cost"],"examTip":"Know the formulas for Break-even Point and Margin of Safety. Understand the difference between Marginal and Absorption Costing."}},{"itemId":"68d6eefe-8a32-5d48-911f-ae889dea972d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"df67ccc9-5d16-4ab5-be67-4ff195cf39c8","sourceKey":"quiz:1","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which historical figure is credited with the first published description of the double-entry bookkeeping system in 1494?","options":["Leonardo da Vinci","Luca Pacioli","Adam Smith","Benedetto Cotrugli"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Accounting Foundations & Framework"}},{"itemId":"cf0cc3c5-dfc9-5afe-ac4d-8edf5b40ccec","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f1926a2e-f124-408b-8a2e-5388403ad901","sourceKey":"quiz:2","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the conceptual framework, what are the two primary fundamental qualitative characteristics of financial information?","options":["Comparability and Verifiability","Timeliness and Understandability","Relevance and Faithful Representation","Materiality and Prudence"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Accounting Foundations & Framework"}},{"itemId":"53071fda-49a0-5119-a0f9-f3f532cb7941","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"11734133-59e5-48f5-9e14-9c081d25d1c9","sourceKey":"quiz:3","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The \"Going Concern\" concept assumes that:","options":["The business will be liquidated in the near future.","The business will continue operations for the foreseeable future.","The owner and the business are the same legal entity.","Revenue is recognized only when cash is received."],"correctOption":1,"section":"Accounting Foundations & Framework"}},{"itemId":"acf6cc4c-9dc9-5565-b56d-73ed4c3f9e3e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"be384af5-d14e-4c14-96c1-690b12a9fbad","sourceKey":"quiz:4","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which branch of accounting is primarily focused on providing information for internal planning and control?","options":["Financial Accounting","Auditing","Management Accounting","Tax Accounting"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Accounting Foundations & Framework"}},{"itemId":"482c325a-9a17-5ab2-888a-1597eba4f20e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"867ab94c-1547-4101-96c5-0cea50176c72","sourceKey":"quiz:5","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Under the Accrual Basis of accounting, when is revenue recognized?","options":["When cash is deposited in the bank.","When the invoice is printed.","When it is earned, regardless of cash timing.","At the end of the fiscal year."],"correctOption":2,"section":"Accounting Foundations & Framework"}},{"itemId":"13cf958d-d232-54f1-a3be-1f673dd1723c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2ae47f74-0ac3-44d4-be00-df44f1b08538","sourceKey":"quiz:6","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The accounting equation is correctly expressed as:","options":["Assets = Liabilities - Equity","Assets + Liabilities = Equity","Assets = Liabilities + Equity","Equity = Assets + Liabilities"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Accounting Foundations & Framework"}},{"itemId":"a9a51332-0b68-5d0a-8bc9-4cb641349401","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0120e232-84e5-4825-a143-9b746eb42a79","sourceKey":"quiz:7","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is classified as \"Capital Expenditure\"?","options":["Monthly electricity bill payment","Purchase of a delivery van for business use","Staff salary payments","Annual insurance premiums"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Accounting Foundations & Framework"}},{"itemId":"087b579d-e201-513e-8cac-a0a2e95fe0b3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b3fd4feb-15fa-45a9-8078-0002dd090ea0","sourceKey":"quiz:8","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the primary function of the \"Journal\" or \"Prime Books of Entry\"?","options":["To summarize the final profit and loss.","To list all assets owned by the firm.","To record transactions chronologically before posting to ledgers.","To reconcile the bank statement."],"correctOption":2,"section":"Accounting Foundations & Framework"}},{"itemId":"5863464b-b547-5db7-a658-2779ad740fe6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"38dc7799-1666-4ea8-a5df-7c3615722854","sourceKey":"quiz:9","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A \"Contra Entry\" in a three-column cash book involves:","options":["A transaction between the business and a debtor.","The transfer of funds between the Cash account and the Bank account.","A discount allowed to a customer.","The correction of a prior period error."],"correctOption":1,"section":"Accounting Foundations & Framework"}},{"itemId":"06fc9403-1db4-5aa3-bc04-99ab5b92e731","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8fd41dd5-5ff4-4bda-a18a-9e7bb34fefb1","sourceKey":"quiz:10","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In an Imprest System of petty cash, the \"Imprest Amount\" refers to:","options":["The total expenses incurred in a month.","The floating balance or fixed amount replenished at the start of a period.","The amount of cash stolen or lost.","The interest earned on petty cash."],"correctOption":1,"section":"Accounting Foundations & Framework"}},{"itemId":"59d3a199-f0c2-5597-a03b-684b94c9f245","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bb6cee00-3431-44ac-9462-c6249d15119d","sourceKey":"quiz:11","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 1 requires a complete set of financial statements to include all the following EXCEPT:","options":["Statement of Financial Position","Statement of Changes in Equity","A Board of Directors' Biography","Notes comprising a summary of significant accounting policies"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 - Presentation of Financial Statements"}},{"itemId":"86d0379f-3f6f-5ae9-9ecb-c13878f0a33b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"324c2056-b561-4436-80c5-b3e30a746758","sourceKey":"quiz:12","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Under IAS 1, the \"Balance Sheet\" is formally referred to as the:","options":["Statement of Assets and Liabilities","Statement of Financial Position","Statement of Wealth Distribution","Ledger Summary Report"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 - Presentation of Financial Statements"}},{"itemId":"52948240-913a-5ed7-8422-0d4163feacaa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f508668e-078d-401c-a360-3303ab7232d2","sourceKey":"quiz:13","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An asset is classified as \"Current\" if it is expected to be realized within:","options":["Five years","The entity's normal operating cycle or 12 months","Six months only","The lifetime of the business"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 1 - Presentation of Financial Statements"}},{"itemId":"7d2f8966-582b-5edd-bff2-12bf1ea0a258","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fe5dcb21-f589-4da7-b459-6073f1e2446a","sourceKey":"quiz:14","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the Statement of Profit or Loss, IAS 1 allows expenses to be classified by:","options":["Nature or Function","Size or Color","Owner's preference only","Geographical location"],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 1 - Presentation of Financial Statements"}},{"itemId":"670f7513-5181-5751-b692-7abc66af3f76","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"65f6ff65-ce10-4732-8140-7af3faa93369","sourceKey":"quiz:15","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a \"Current Liability\"?","options":["10-year Bank Loan","Share Capital","Trade Payables","Revaluation Reserve"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 1 - Presentation of Financial Statements"}},{"itemId":"bfadf2d5-6f27-5703-9f2a-87158cbdf8c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"791adc5b-1b20-40c9-a36e-0e3a170d5cde","sourceKey":"quiz:16","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"IAS 2 defines inventories as assets held for sale in the ordinary course of business, or:","options":["Assets held for long-term investment.","In the process of production for such sale.","Intellectual property and patents.","Cash held in foreign currencies."],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 - Inventories"}},{"itemId":"c93f0e24-6566-587c-8308-42c25d58fb4d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"57a8b285-4bec-4411-a9f7-b689fb928a78","sourceKey":"quiz:17","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the fundamental measurement principle for inventories?","options":["Lower of Cost and Fair Value","Higher of Cost and Net Realizable Value","Lower of Cost and Net Realizable Value","Replacement Cost"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 - Inventories"}},{"itemId":"03454adc-f91a-59d2-9bf7-fb8ab05b16f4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"10d21c8a-d135-49a8-8eeb-16327ff4e6be","sourceKey":"quiz:18","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Net Realizable Value (NRV) is calculated as:","options":["Estimated selling price minus estimated costs of completion and sale.","Original purchase price plus inflation.","Selling price plus transportation costs.","The value of the inventory if sold at an auction today."],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 2 - Inventories"}},{"itemId":"68402922-6f9d-53a0-a386-9af096599eb2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bdc12eb4-6036-49f8-83fd-de4bcf47d8d5","sourceKey":"quiz:19","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following costs must be EXCLUDED from the cost of inventory?","options":["Costs of purchase","Abnormal amounts of wasted materials","Costs of conversion","Import duties"],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 - Inventories"}},{"itemId":"76619049-4371-56ef-acdf-292325f80cc0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"37927c46-c112-4459-b05c-14ebbcebc7ba","sourceKey":"quiz:20","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which inventory valuation method is explicitly PROHIBITED by IAS 2?","options":["First-In, First-Out (FIFO)","Weighted Average Cost (WAC)","Last-In, First-Out (LIFO)","Specific Identification"],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 - Inventories"}},{"itemId":"4db0ab38-d15c-5197-8b6c-b4fd26df7aa2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e10f2c71-7e55-4dc9-8edb-927a8ec3b545","sourceKey":"quiz:21","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"When inventories are sold, their carrying amount shall be recognized as an expense in the period in which:","options":["The cash is received.","The related revenue is recognized.","The inventory was purchased.","The inventory was manufactured."],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 - Inventories"}},{"itemId":"9633f418-1012-586b-99e8-253131ac588c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3d3c59a4-5c68-4973-baf4-a276309950a7","sourceKey":"quiz:22","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Storage costs are generally excluded from inventory cost UNLESS:","options":["They are necessary in the production process before a further stage.","The warehouse is owned by the company.","The storage period exceeds one year.","They are incurred after the goods are ready for sale."],"correctOption":0,"section":"IAS 2 - Inventories"}},{"itemId":"0e509dcf-23d6-5edb-a0b3-d65df42607ff","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c13c50d0-e3d1-4248-9b1e-e5614ff4e536","sourceKey":"quiz:23","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a disclosure requirement for IAS 2?","options":["The names of the suppliers.","The accounting policies adopted, including the cost formula used.","The specific location of every warehouse.","The profit margin on each individual item."],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 2 - Inventories"}},{"itemId":"3caff14c-2ef9-5a10-8fce-293247f69290","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"650c6e44-0a2e-423a-9080-61bec8bca60a","sourceKey":"quiz:24","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"If the cost of an item is $1,000 and the NRV is $800, the inventory should be valued at:","options":["$1,000","$1,800","$900","$800"],"correctOption":3,"section":"IAS 2 - Inventories"}},{"itemId":"cec00f1a-5c70-59aa-a433-f476305ec82b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"50ce7dac-5457-4cbf-823e-63fb07e348bb","sourceKey":"quiz:25","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Trade discounts and rebates should be:","options":["Ignored in cost calculation.","Added to the cost of inventory.","Deducted in determining the costs of purchase.","Recognized as \"Other Income.\""],"correctOption":2,"section":"IAS 2 - Inventories"}},{"itemId":"b0afa289-756f-51b5-9e19-fd63b65b6c0c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"330a62f3-8681-4fe5-b6a3-c7bceff52637","sourceKey":"quiz:26","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"What is the primary objective of IAS 8?","options":["To dictate how to calculate depreciation.","To enhance the relevance and reliability of financial statements.","To provide a list of all acceptable tax deductions.","To regulate the issuance of new shares."],"correctOption":1,"section":"IAS 8 - Policies, Estimates, and Errors"}},{"itemId":"49f90dfe-4611-5547-acd3-a004b5cd43e0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ba1bfd3f-8ea9-4258-9772-c9c55b779e99","sourceKey":"quiz:27","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A change 