levels","instruments and institutions that allow participants to hedge against uncertainty","the registration of new business names"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"ac77a786-fbf2-5d5d-bd05-4a338ce2a815","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"40995617-fa23-47e6-b395-b13f37c41f93","sourceKey":"quiz:201","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following would be classified as a financial regulatory authority within Nigeria's financial system, according to the course notes?","options":["NYSC and FRSC","NNPC and NEPA","WAEC and JAMB","CBN and SEC"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"7af5bec1-ab21-5064-b9c5-7cc6b11940c4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2f5193fa-dfd3-439d-b402-8411c6baa4cb","sourceKey":"quiz:202","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"According to John Storey (1995), HRM seeks to achieve competitive advantage through:","options":["Outsourcing all HR functions","Cutting employee wages","Reducing the size of the workforce","The strategic deployment of a highly committed workforce using cultural, structural, and personal techniques"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"c31ace36-2590-53e6-8089-6dcd535e15db","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e0346b15-4d2c-4746-945e-472804a4d889","sourceKey":"quiz:203","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Classical management functions (procurement, development, compensation, integration, maintenance, separation) were established by:","options":["Frederick Herzberg","Henri Fayol","Abraham Maslow","Herbert Simon"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"b6aa820a-2b62-57de-bd46-87f506133d4e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"841bd5e7-c509-40e2-a1a4-5954a544c404","sourceKey":"quiz:204","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Workforce planning, productivity enhancement, cost management, and talent acquisition fall under which HRM objective?","options":["Personal Objective","Organisational Objective","Functional Objective","Societal Objective"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"c3a76c38-c81e-5cae-8430-5c52cc442be8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"42030323-a00a-4c81-884c-1f25f038dd8e","sourceKey":"quiz:205","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Career development, job satisfaction, work-life balance, and employee wellbeing are examples of:","options":["Functional Objectives","Societal Objectives","Financial Objectives","Personal Objectives"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"2d3415cd-f6c1-5c89-b133-b2a0f190599f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3915b695-0f1d-4f1a-a852-e7de2d794fd9","sourceKey":"quiz:206","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Gary Dessler's (2000) definition of HRM emphasizes:","options":["The lens of managerial responsibility and specific functional practices such as recruiting, screening, training, and appraisal","Shareholder profit maximisation only","The elimination of employee benefits","A purely legal perspective"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"87733475-5c51-5229-9a99-a850e20d0cc2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e652626f-8ecb-4d43-b36e-dd0ac998c3d1","sourceKey":"quiz:207","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Wendell French's (1974) definition of HRM emphasizes:","options":["The recruitment, selection, development, utilization of, and accommodation to human resources by the organisation","Only legal compliance","Only employee termination","Only compensation and payroll"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"7c795bc7-f5e9-57e7-88d8-41e70777a00e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d42728bd-b80a-4232-9cf8-0358b374dfdf","sourceKey":"quiz:208","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A more contemporary HRM perspective described in the notes signals a shift from viewing employees as a cost to be managed toward:","options":["Ignoring employee wellbeing","Reducing all employee benefits","Eliminating the HR department entirely","Organisations adapting to the needs, expectations, and limitations of their people"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"4de1919b-6ef7-5ae6-b45f-94b021e98e1f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"389d539e-f653-4341-88f0-8c1d9e8682e5","sourceKey":"quiz:209","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these falls under the 'Functional Objectives' of HRM?","options":["Career development","Corporate social responsibility","HR systems quality, HR metrics and analysis, compliance and governance","Work-life balance"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"fc9e9e57-5cd7-527c-b2bb-fdeb65b880d5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"582fcb41-0f0a-414e-a3be-3d5216b3cb71","sourceKey":"quiz:210","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"'Knowledge retention' as an HRM concern primarily involves:","options":["Preserving valuable organisational knowledge and expertise within the organisation","Reducing training budgets","Ignoring succession planning","Discarding institutional knowledge when employees leave"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"662f8caf-1f8e-5704-9945-16f94d13684c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"53d93ca0-8cbb-4640-ad7a-549637b03d6a","sourceKey":"quiz:211","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is one of the operational dimensions of Personnel Management identified through classical management functions established by Henri Fayol?","options":["Procurement","Advertising","Currency exchange","Manufacturing"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"6eba2b95-436d-5b38-8ba6-bbe9b9014346","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8c257b71-6fe6-4171-bd0c-8a7c38787b5e","sourceKey":"quiz:212","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"HRM's role in 'maintaining employee relations' primarily involves:","options":["Managing the relationship between the organisation and its workforce to sustain a productive work environment","Outsourcing all communication with staff","Only handling employee complaints once a year","Avoiding all contact with employees"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"ae3701e2-c7b9-5664-b3ea-4393d477cd2d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7448c4f7-8d0b-49ef-b5b5-c07b187073e0","sourceKey":"quiz:213","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"'Talent acquisition', listed under organisational objectives of HRM, refers to:","options":["Only training existing staff","Firing underperforming staff","Reducing the size of the HR department","The process of identifying and recruiting skilled individuals to meet organisational needs"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"62488534-1732-5709-beed-24ef136fcf17","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4a258421-0332-4c0a-940c-8c185198228e","sourceKey":"quiz:214","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Michael Armstrong (1994) described human capital as:","options":["The organisation's most valued asset, not a liability","An unnecessary expense","A liability on the business","Irrelevant to organisational strategy"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance"}},{"itemId":"311fbc53-7945-5c0e-be4c-03c84c1c2fc3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"efd6e9d5-daa3-400d-b56f-439ea83169d7","sourceKey":"quiz:215","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a factor that necessitates organisational change?","options":["Increased competition","Technological changes and innovation","A fixed and unchanging economy","Changes in customer expectations"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"bda41acf-4488-5362-961d-cb136b781756","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6a955bd6-93cd-4b7a-aa7e-495a15748031","sourceKey":"quiz:216","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Organisational 'structure' in the context of change refers to:","options":["The physical building where the company operates","The way an organisation is arranged, including departments, reporting lines, and responsibilities","The list of company shareholders","The company's marketing strategy"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"0b3347c0-6569-545e-9016-8c2d4d25e434","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"701e4e51-b1c7-4f9b-bade-8557563c9f17","sourceKey":"quiz:217","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Change management involving training and development programs to help employees acquire new skills relates to:","options":["Changes in structure","Changes in systems","Changes in employees' skills","Changes in strategy"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"d4a21243-c8d1-5351-8d20-064bc3e81212","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3c2428c0-cadc-41bf-9515-c74cd850660c","sourceKey":"quiz:218","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Fields (2008), how many types/levels of organisational change are identified?","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"44b8f166-a817-5109-a2f2-5b27bd4cdad4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4d80085b-5aac-4a1c-8c67-b727c5da3397","sourceKey":"quiz:219","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"First level change is best described as:","options":["Incremental in nature, seeking to maintain existing alignment among systems","Org-wide efforts that reorder beliefs and values","Strategic in nature and questions organisational purpose","A change that only affects the IT department"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"6f128394-5e9b-53f2-a670-7d6161cce341","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0fcd906b-5a16-4c97-ab0f-4985d3062754","sourceKey":"quiz:220","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is an example of a 'systems and processes' change?","options":["Changing the company's mission statement","Recruiting a new CEO","Merging two departments","Adopting new software to improve productivity"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"26661bcd-6364-5783-9a7e-0b58e7d12511","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"73ad3b93-02dd-408e-b8ed-875653bbae90","sourceKey":"quiz:221","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which two models are commonly used to guide organisational change according to the notes?","options":["Maslow's hierarchy and Herzberg's theory","Kotter's 8-step model and Lewin's 3-step model","SWOT and PESTLE analysis","Porter's Five Forces and BCG Matrix"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"b4fbe612-4c60-5251-984b-2bd312aa38ad","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"88afa5b9-0442-4631-91c6-31ac6af38569","sourceKey":"quiz:222","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Globalization and the emergence of a knowledge-based workforce are examples of:","options":["Stages of group development","Internal reasons employees resign","Factors that necessitate organisational change","Types of financial instruments"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"59f7603b-edd5-5fb4-bb19-04b6f87b1a62","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1cb99137-21f4-44f3-a563-3c4d1ca3c6fe","sourceKey":"quiz:223","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Increased competition, as a driver of change, primarily forces organisations to:","options":["Shut down operations","Remain competitive by adapting their strategy or operations","Avoid all innovation","Ignore market conditions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"be6b1ea1-a8fa-53d8-aa01-daf841ef7fdb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9d8783d3-57f1-4f2b-b7fe-21ff4b4d1bdf","sourceKey":"quiz:224","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Change in 'systems and processes' is primarily intended to:","options":["Increase advertising budgets","Improve productivity and ensure tasks are completed effectively","Reduce the number of shareholders","Change the company's logo"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"c94efd0b-f67a-5dff-b58d-21f498462399","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cf7298b3-06ed-43dd-bf52-1c38076553d2","sourceKey":"quiz:225","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which level of organisational change is strategic in nature and questions the organisation's purpose and objectives?","options":["Second level change","Zero level change","Third level change","First level change"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"617abdf7-ecff-50b8-a804-6a1df82d4003","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9c7500af-c464-4feb-a425-85711237bf2a","sourceKey":"quiz:226","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Encouraging positive attitudes, teamwork, and involvement in change through communication and motivation primarily addresses:","options":["Changes in strategy","Changes in technology","Changes in structure","Changes in employees' attitudes"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"66965fea-47c9-5f68-9a28-5acc390f6231","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d86bfe00-5449-42e4-bc29-ac6a49370774","sourceKey":"quiz:227","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Change management is described as the application of tools and techniques to move an organisation from its present situation to:","options":["A random unrelated state","A future desired position","Its original founding state","Bankruptcy"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"3ba8cba9-5b6b-5610-ae1c-2b0fd81c4560","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"47a5573b-e2b3-40d6-a7ad-487d75dc4f0d","sourceKey":"quiz:228","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"New government regulation and deregulation, as a factor necessitating change, primarily affects an organisation's:","options":["Choice of office furniture","Employee birthdays","Logo design","Compliance requirements and operating environment"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"44e1133b-b30b-5047-975c-e15340e30899","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a9044c0a-1964-4278-9774-3d536153ce66","sourceKey":"quiz:229","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which best distinguishes second level change from third level change?","options":["They are identical","Second level change is strategic and questions organisational purpose; third level change reorders beliefs, values, and interrelationships of all subsystems","Second level change is org-wide and reorders values; third level is incremental","Second level change never affects strategy"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"30792771-f525-5215-a85d-81d062360e42","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"31493bcd-04ef-46b8-bbe5-3150744b5f2b","sourceKey":"quiz:230","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"According to the notes, which two named models guide the process of implementing organisational change?","options":["SWOT and PESTLE analysis","Kotter's 8-step model and Lewin's 3-step model","Maslow's and Herzberg's theories","Porter's Five Forces and BCG Matrix"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management"}},{"itemId":"b421cf25-cd5c-5858-88ae-400faa1e7d32","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ef55f584-1457-4112-962b-428a309a6dfc","sourceKey":"quiz:231","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A decision is said to exist when:","options":["A manager is on leave","No problem has been identified","A problem or opportunity is identified, more than one course of action is possible, and a choice must be made","There is only one possible course of action"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"51a549b6-4fbf-540e-bfbf-2e2b1bb47c1e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ed4a3ce7-b04a-47c5-a57d-fa0d402ee42e","sourceKey":"quiz:232","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Uncertainty in decision making refers to:","options":["Not knowing the future outcome of some probabilities","Knowing the exact outcome of a decision","A guaranteed profit","A decision made without any consequences"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"4efedc6e-52cf-52e8-99d0-06f7dbebe54c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bcd779e1-1d32-4cf1-8646-26a300c01344","sourceKey":"quiz:233","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Programmed decisions are best described as:","options":["Unique, complex, and unfamiliar situations","Routine and repetitive, often guided by rules, policies, and standard operating procedures","Decisions that are never repeated","Decisions made only by external auditors"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"36f7a3d0-ed8a-5db4-aa76-2757c4bba75a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0135b68c-e400-480f-b942-6115b844c97c","sourceKey":"quiz:234","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Selecting an option that is 'good enough' rather than the absolute best is known as:","options":["Escalating","Anchoring","Satisficing","Optimizing"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"9d05501e-a552-5530-996b-622ecb93581c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cbe77e3f-5090-4a8c-bc36-5dcdbb269c0e","sourceKey":"quiz:235","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best illustrates 'risk' as opposed to 'uncertainty' in decision making?","options":["No decision is required","Possible outcomes can be identified and probabilities can be reasonably estimated","The outcome is already known with certainty","Possible outcomes are difficult to identify and probabilities cannot be estimated"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"e2f5670d-bd7a-52a5-a77d-0449e7e69b72","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d9e9ded5-3d95-42f7-9bdb-056bad00f0f4","sourceKey":"quiz:236","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"'Unintended consequences' in managerial decision making refer to:","options":["Outcomes guaranteed to be positive","Planned outcomes that were fully expected","The complete absence of any effect","Effects of a decision that were not planned for"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"b8af9527-9078-5a1b-b0bf-32b3619b397e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0bb65847-5f5a-4877-b058-5858f5ab8c6a","sourceKey":"quiz:237","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Quality of managerial decisions can affect all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Profitability","The weather","Customer satisfaction","Employee morale"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"0f389df9-5f85-522e-929e-6e87abd7c201","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7d6bdc41-6f52-46ff-8c77-35c7b9a86a0f","sourceKey":"quiz:238","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which step in the decision-making process involves investigating the underlying causes of a problem?","options":["Identify the problem or opportunity","Generate alternatives","Diagnose the problem","Implement the decision"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"0c8b123d-115f-5aa9-998b-ec146aa2406c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0d91c294-f531-449c-a3fe-2c6f59896737","sourceKey":"quiz:239","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A key strength of the Rational Decision-Making Model is that it:","options":["Eliminates the need for information","Encourages systematic analysis and reduces impulsive decisions","Requires no analysis at all","Guarantees a perfect outcome every time"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"838f6f81-1ae1-5482-9f8d-d8235ceb5d19","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7c9f46c9-f479-429e-94f1-a27e22deef7d","sourceKey":"quiz:240","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Herbert Simon's concept of bounded rationality suggests that managers operate under limitations including:","options":["Complete certainty about outcomes","No organisational constraints whatsoever","Unlimited time and perfect information","Incomplete information, limited cognitive capacity, and time pressure"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"5c56434c-8e6c-583b-a5a9-dc21f739ae46","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4279afa4-e5e9-4cd1-b7c6-e4e6f55dad67","sourceKey":"quiz:241","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Overconfidence bias in decision making refers to:","options":["The tendency to overestimate one's knowledge or ability","Relying only on the first piece of information received","Investing more resources into a failing project","Underestimating one's own ability"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"912d9b8c-ce44-5085-8195-3e21f8f6967e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"025cbae8-ce91-4d9a-a3f0-4d95ed5a42b2","sourceKey":"quiz:242","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Anchoring bias occurs when a decision maker:","options":["Escalates commitment to a failed project","Seeks only information that confirms existing beliefs","Refuses to make any decision","Relies too heavily on the first information received"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving"}},{"itemId":"03594d7c-c70b-526a-865f-9276cdfaa9f3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3e4f35c4-2568-4984-acc5-d10795de467b","sourceKey":"quiz:243","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Organisational Behaviour (OB) refers to:","options":["The legal structure of a business","The financial performance of a company","The systematic study of human behaviour within an organisation","The marketing strategy of a firm"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"b5e2ca8e-f832-5f71-b551-b4a2e16bb38f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"17ae2bca-56ef-47f9-bb07-bdf142106d02","sourceKey":"quiz:244","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a nature/characteristic of OB?","options":["Purely financial in focus","Human oriented","Inter-disciplinary nature","Scientific approach"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"4a9decf3-1b00-5df0-8c11-09fd71b9a4ca","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ed4756fd-44bf-407c-a94f-07d23ec9e968","sourceKey":"quiz:245","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"OB draws knowledge from which combination of disciplines?","options":["Only mathematics and statistics","Law, medicine, and architecture","Psychology, sociology, anthropology, political science, and economics","Chemistry, physics, and biology"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"acd4077a-6472-5b3d-a7b3-46ecb2a0550e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cb10ee0b-ffa7-4201-a266-cb4195e49969","sourceKey":"quiz:246","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"At which level of OB scope would 'personality, perception, and motivation' be studied?","options":["Organisational level","Individual level","National level","Group level"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"c2800a22-46ec-5f68-b54b-6582822e9a8c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fe7ea987-6f32-43d0-9ed7-34c8971b0d18","sourceKey":"quiz:247","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Group dynamics, teamwork, and leadership are studied under which scope of OB?","options":["Group level","Individual level","Organisational level","Societal level"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"9f857aad-456a-5211-8daf-b43d080b81a4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"64fae194-36d8-49df-b2eb-27fa84dc05f7","sourceKey":"quiz:248","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these is one of the four elements of OB?","options":["Currency","Inflation","Capital","Technology"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"d4711ad4-7715-542a-aadd-3cdf1076a4ec","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dc710892-6fa0-465f-b1c8-436996a7a743","sourceKey":"quiz:249","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A group is best defined as:","options":["A collection of individuals who interact, share common interests/goals, and work together to accomplish a specific objective","Any two strangers standing in the same room","A single manager and their assistant only","A department with more than 50 employees"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"d8e1d05d-adaf-5d9f-ab74-f4d4067c03e9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1278c882-ea07-4344-be38-52818a6a22e4","sourceKey":"quiz:250","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Command groups and task groups are examples of:","options":["Informal groups","Interest groups","Formal groups","Friendship groups"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"8c652ae7-114f-5ffe-8c57-0af601b460b3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fffb84a7-1164-412b-be68-6652441bc95e","sourceKey":"quiz:251","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Informal groups such as interest groups and friendship groups:","options":["Develop naturally through social interactions among employees","Are created by top management through a memo","Are illegal in most organisations","Only exist in unionised organisations"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"b89fab99-d49b-5314-b23f-35f435f7d37f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b170fee5-2fe2-439e-8864-7e48beb727cb","sourceKey":"quiz:252","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The correct order of the stages of group development is:","options":["Storming, Forming, Norming, Adjourning, Performing","Norming, Storming, Forming, Performing, Adjourning","Forming, Storming, Norming, Performing, Adjourning","Forming, Performing, Storming, Norming, Adjourning"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"a1fe0ef7-c4a3-51a0-afe4-6f2a2093f1ef","courseId":"