ersonality and perception","Organisational culture and organisational development","Team communication"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"5e2b49f8-62d9-50c6-b073-c0c1ce182629","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0d26170c-df2a-4f18-95da-44decc816d1b","sourceKey":"quiz:257","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"'Application oriented', as a nature of OB, means that:","options":["OB principles are applied to solve workplace problems and improve performance","OB principles are purely theoretical with no real use","OB cannot be applied outside a classroom","OB is irrelevant to managers"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"24562c18-a10d-53c8-b04d-02d0f93280da","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7b99ccd8-6bd3-4d4a-b85f-3a0620fced44","sourceKey":"quiz:258","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a characteristic of a group, as defined in the notes?","options":["Independence among members","A single individual working alone","No shared norms or values","Common goal or objective, and interdependence among members"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"879c80b3-cab7-5b35-acfc-4e05c0d622df","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c9bdb6b9-63db-4711-8712-21b5fbba1f98","sourceKey":"quiz:259","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A committee formed to complete a specific project is an example of:","options":["An informal group","An interest group","A task group (a type of formal group)","A friendship group"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"6f265ef6-cb50-5edd-8fe5-e52786df81dd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9530df12-b1e2-40fb-9d92-0195871b69fe","sourceKey":"quiz:260","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which factor is NOT listed among the factors affecting group development in the notes?","options":["Group cohesion","Group norms","The company's tax rate","Group size"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"473b7bf5-b6f3-5a76-9769-7c09ea938b9d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"fa45f3fe-569f-4939-9aa3-9c20a9329327","sourceKey":"quiz:261","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The importance of groups in an organisation includes all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Facilitates communication","Improves decision making","Guarantees zero conflict","Increases employee motivation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics"}},{"itemId":"492d9864-2a4d-59e4-8b9a-ea35dc0f5c6e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"adfd805f-3b65-41d2-b5f0-b2c23cff07d1","sourceKey":"quiz:262","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the communication process, 'encoding' refers to:","options":["The medium through which a message travels","The response given by the receiver","Interpreting a received message","The process of converting ideas into words, symbols, images, or other forms of communication"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"dd625f99-3b1e-529d-ae79-1b5af53c9a5e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8b9b0ce8-3244-45bb-b3bb-e3be4a1ebc27","sourceKey":"quiz:263","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The person or organisation that initiates a message is known as the:","options":["Receiver","Sender","Channel","Decoder"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"21394906-780c-5de4-917d-5bc239f7a3ce","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"499d246d-5180-4408-9f55-caf905c9379d","sourceKey":"quiz:264","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which characteristic of effective communication requires that a message be factually correct?","options":["Clarity","Accuracy","Conciseness","Timeliness"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"d377e704-1ba1-55ad-9dd7-d32de7c50ad2","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"140d37ef-14a0-4ed1-8862-eb06bf5a04d7","sourceKey":"quiz:265","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A short message that omits essential information fails to be:","options":["Complete","Concise","Timely","Appropriate"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"96fc4daf-3ad2-54b0-9318-3c038dcdb0af","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ce0a4fa2-7b87-4bc6-9525-4a0c51afad47","sourceKey":"quiz:266","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Formal communication channels are characterised by being:","options":["Based only on rumours","Documented and traceable to an identifiable source, following officially recognised organisational structures","Always verbal and informal","Undocumented and untraceable"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"0b5fa2be-b340-5b95-9c34-1ee5c2141a32","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6f4c04b2-78c4-41b3-bc69-2ba7edf739c1","sourceKey":"quiz:267","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The grapevine is best described as:","options":["The unofficial, informal communication network through which information spreads within an organisation","A type of formal report","A scheduled team meeting","An official memo issued by top management"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"6074c5aa-d8ef-5be3-a450-69f8ad27c7d3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2579d4e9-2fca-4045-88bf-ec49efa407ab","sourceKey":"quiz:268","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"'Decoding', in the communication process, refers to:","options":["The initial idea before it is converted to words","The medium used to send a message","The process by which the receiver interprets the message","Noise interfering with the message"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"f2a9abf4-f2b7-5e16-b2ac-32d35c6bfb67","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"af5a25c4-d8c1-411e-b43b-509b5a109885","sourceKey":"quiz:269","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"'Feedback', in the communication process, is best described as:","options":["The channel used to transmit a message","Interference in the communication process","The person who initiates communication","The response provided by the receiver"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"6abb453d-1304-5f6a-8bb3-fc48e09319ad","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"693737e1-bf11-461f-a823-43e1992d1b16","sourceKey":"quiz:270","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"For communication to be 'timely', information must be:","options":["Repeated multiple times daily","Delivered whenever convenient, regardless of relevance","Communicated while it is still useful","Withheld until a formal meeting"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"131eb1b4-02d7-5c1a-9ecf-3ebad276bc92","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"840d4749-f23a-44e7-a3bd-e641d1495a1e","sourceKey":"quiz:271","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Formal communication channels are distinguished from informal ones mainly because they are:","options":["Based on rumours","Documented and traceable to an identifiable source, following recognised organisational structures","Never used in large organisations","Always verbal"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"d78cde5d-0f10-5c6e-810c-946e17fcb405","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e91fd561-ec15-4d1a-9037-185591b894ef","sourceKey":"quiz:272","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"When an employee reports an operational problem to their manager, this is an example of:","options":["Grapevine communication","Horizontal communication","Downward communication","Upward communication"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"939407f0-f04d-5bc7-a236-be4bc4999892","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"47327e17-96bc-472e-96cd-ae670f289223","sourceKey":"quiz:273","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Departments sharing information with each other at a similar organisational level illustrates:","options":["Horizontal communication","External communication","Upward communication","Downward communication"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"dd173ce3-3493-549a-9809-482fd6194feb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ece0a663-1e82-4925-8702-e60a7a3696f1","sourceKey":"quiz:274","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A key disadvantage of the grapevine (informal communication) is that it:","options":["Always improves employee trust","Is always slower than formal communication","Is illegal in most organisations","May distort information, create anxiety, and damage reputations"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"e306e931-c2f6-5fe2-ba54-64d859af8a04","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5479538b-65d5-4dea-a91c-8cb0eae2d7ae","sourceKey":"quiz:275","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A short message that omits essential information fails to meet which characteristic of effective communication?","options":["Completeness","Timeliness","Appropriateness","Conciseness"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"1cf1c5cb-ae17-568a-9e52-0f8750bf598a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4a609d3b-01e1-4b1d-a5b0-790c0b350248","sourceKey":"quiz:276","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of these best describes the role of 'noise' in the communication process?","options":["It is the same as feedback","It always improves message clarity","It only occurs in written communication","It is anything that interferes with accurate communication"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management"}},{"itemId":"f0a7d75a-24dc-527c-8377-9fda90389fdb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"864626ae-0234-4b3a-823f-0229e12bc7e8","sourceKey":"quiz:277","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is NOT one of the functions of a financial system?","options":["Price discovery","Mobilization of savings","Risk management","Manufacturing of goods"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"4af4f7da-ada0-5bbf-9387-9d19d01077db","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"774df25d-2824-40be-a533-a124bd1fd914","sourceKey":"quiz:278","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In a financial market, the money market deals with:","options":["Foreign embassies","Short-term funds","Long-term funds","Real estate only"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"f76feb4d-e1dd-5a2e-84e3-60d3028a60fb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"02e7d43a-22fa-4283-afeb-57d39e3126ce","sourceKey":"quiz:279","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation (NDIC) primarily functions to:","options":["Issue government bonds","Manage pension contributions","Insure deposits and protect depositors against bank failure","Regulate the insurance industry"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"2258c7ad-c8a9-5f8b-9b3a-2baecf0afc73","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c2768fbd-b2ab-4c2b-84ad-7d7004dfe1b1","sourceKey":"quiz:280","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Merchant banks in Nigeria are primarily focused on:","options":["Wholesale banking, investment banking, and medium-to-long term financing","Retail banking for individual savers","Issuing national currency","Regulating insurance companies"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"00dc2838-1270-5ddb-9814-b1de9efca078","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2349d368-8e38-4f54-83f7-7286e5b0c704","sourceKey":"quiz:281","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Financial intermediation refers to the process where financial institutions:","options":["Set exchange rates for all countries","Act as middlemen between those with surplus capital and those who need capital","Print new currency","Audit government spending"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"81f64208-ec04-5010-a726-a52d43eda433","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f9c113c8-2222-493b-b681-91dabb2cddee","sourceKey":"quiz:282","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In a primary market:","options":["Companies and government issue new securities to the public for the first time","Only foreign currencies are exchanged","Already existing securities are traded among investors","Goods are auctioned to the highest bidder"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"9f7a539f-3648-5a1f-9904-58bb0b036dcd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5ec5342e-7a52-48e3-b562-8e95fe767d6e","sourceKey":"quiz:283","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The capital market, as opposed to the money market, deals primarily with:","options":["Short-term funds","Government salaries","Long-term funds","Foreign currency only"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"d9d410c9-73c9-5ed2-ab8e-72f442d2417e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2eb6db94-32c0-4a93-b16f-61ebf1959bb5","sourceKey":"quiz:284","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria (FMBN) is best described as:","options":["A commercial bank for retail customers","The apex mortgage bank that drives housing finance","An insurance regulator","A foreign exchange bureau"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"8478f3ae-e18c-5898-aa7e-1d575d591765","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ad39df46-e564-4464-bd62-f4588b683ddb","sourceKey":"quiz:285","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Pension Fund Administrators (PFAs) primarily function to:","options":["Print government bonds","Issue currency","Manage and invest retirement savings","Regulate capital markets"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"e8b09e5f-8002-55b4-8dd9-14f0f856519b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aad7edd6-63a9-48c1-a892-5d18563e9e13","sourceKey":"quiz:286","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"'Maturity transformation', as a key function of financial intermediation, refers to:","options":["Converting cash into gold","Transforming a bank into an insurance company","Currency devaluation","Converting short-term deposits into long-term loans"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"276f103e-eedb-5f94-80c7-a2d742a40551","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"591dd25f-30ed-4e39-a41e-9cc6e486b8b8","sourceKey":"quiz:287","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In a secondary market such as the Nigerian Exchange (NGX):","options":["Companies receive direct funding from every trade","Only government bonds can be traded","New securities are issued to the public for the first time","Already-existing securities are traded, and proceeds go to the selling investor rather than the original issuer"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market"}},{"itemId":"e8aaaf63-27d6-5f56-bc77-843c2afddb99","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8a51ae82-8988-4718-8be3-edd1ce519061","sourceKey":"quiz:288","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The primary distinction between Human Resource Management (HRM) and traditional personnel administration is that HRM:","options":["Treats employees as strategic resources linked to organisational objectives","Focuses exclusively on employee welfare","Transfers all employee-related decisions to line managers","Is concerned only with recruitment and selection"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"HRM takes a strategic view of people as resources whose capabilities should support organisational objectives."}},{"itemId":"b6128495-151b-5358-b078-49da8f82e1e3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f4c656b2-4725-448f-bb1b-6d9dfff130ef","sourceKey":"quiz:289","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best describes human resource planning?","options":["Selecting candidates after interviews","Forecasting an organisation's future human-resource requirements and availability","Determining only the annual salary budget","Evaluating employee performance after training"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"HR planning forecasts future labour demand and supply so the organisation can have the right people at the right time."}},{"itemId":"49be591e-f915-5b79-a275-d23d6c205c5b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e581af27-f270-46a2-9a7a-2e5daad55bf8","sourceKey":"quiz:290","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The main purpose of a job description is to:","options":["Identify the best applicant for a job","Measure organisational profitability","State the duties, responsibilities and reporting relationships associated with a job","Determine an employee's annual bonus"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"A job description states what the job involves, including duties, responsibilities and reporting relationships."}},{"itemId":"2f409592-6747-53ee-8a13-1eb164c770f8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"19bbca7e-e43f-450a-a65c-632a8b304024","sourceKey":"quiz:291","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is not normally an objective of HRM?","options":["Acquiring competent employees","Maintaining effective employee relations","Maximising employee turnover","Developing employee capabilities"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"HRM generally seeks to retain effective employees, not maximise unnecessary turnover."}},{"itemId":"98cd0646-b780-50c6-b05e-04c9e5fac3a6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"33eb1c80-8c3d-4900-90e2-14957c25a6a0","sourceKey":"quiz:292","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A company first determines the number and type of employees required, then assesses whether existing staff can meet those requirements. This illustrates:","options":["Performance appraisal","HR planning","Employee socialisation","Job enrichment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"HR planning compares forecast workforce requirements with workforce availability."}},{"itemId":"aef6d808-7fd0-57b6-92f8-b6ebb183f5f7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2d5b5310-cf18-4a00-9c96-3c13fcbcca5d","sourceKey":"quiz:293","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A job specification differs from a job description because it primarily identifies:","options":["The company's financial objectives","The tasks and responsibilities of the position","The organisational mission","The qualifications, skills and characteristics required of the job holder"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"A job specification describes the human requirements of the job holder, such as skills, qualifications and attributes."}},{"itemId":"c18afcf4-b5f4-5a3b-9bdc-40ba39f0cb57","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d5670973-a96f-4ac9-8e72-30f838336502","sourceKey":"quiz:294","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following best demonstrates employee empowerment?","options":["Employees are given authority, information and resources to make appropriate decisions","Employees receive responsibilities without authority","Employees are prohibited from making operational decisions","Managers retain every decision"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Empowerment requires appropriate authority, information and resources, not responsibility without decision rights."}},{"itemId":"4dc85423-79c1-5d3d-8165-1cdb80b90949","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1dba7b50-9c3b-404e-a139-b283ceaeb8a2","sourceKey":"quiz:295","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The process of systematically determining the tasks, responsibilities, skills and conditions associated with a job is:","options":["Promotion","Job analysis","Induction","Recruitment"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Job analysis systematically examines job duties, responsibilities, requirements and working conditions."}},{"itemId":"e9647ef1-c3d0-5b4b-b0d3-e8582047eb3d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5f8673d2-3903-4454-bcde-7ff6681a3eb5","sourceKey":"quiz:296","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"If an organisation has many employees approaching retirement age, HR planning should particularly focus on:","options":["Increasing job ambiguity","Succession planning and future talent requirements","Reducing all training","Eliminating recruitment permanently"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Retirement risk makes succession planning and forecasting future talent needs especially important."}},{"itemId":"cfc91402-bde4-5dce-86b3-f710bbbc78e7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b5297bec-edf9-4a7b-bff7-be4defda80ed","sourceKey":"quiz:297","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A company advertises a vacant position on its website and receives applications from both current employees and external candidates. The activity is primarily part of:","options":["Recruitment","Performance appraisal","Orientation","Job evaluation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Recruitment involves attracting a pool of potential applicants for vacancies."}},{"itemId":"bea7fcb1-58f7-508f-8c7d-c50f9c2b96f4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ba72b24-1e86-4c53-984c-cc6b2ea445ae","sourceKey":"quiz:298","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which recruitment method is most likely to improve the organisation's access to a wider external talent pool?","options":["External advertising","Job rotation","Promotion from within","Succession planning"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"External advertising exposes vacancies to candidates beyond the existing workforce."}},{"itemId":"ecb74344-2b41-581d-9c31-28c7bb4f1e09","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2117c7a6-6a68-4083-812a-e0945f06c4e1","sourceKey":"quiz:299","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The fundamental purpose of employee selection is to:","options":["Replace performance appraisal","Generate as many applications as possible","Identify candidates whose qualifications and characteristics best fit the job and organisation","Increase employee turnover"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Selection evaluates applicants and identifies those most suitable for the position and organisation."}},{"itemId":"2e58e0c6-d5d9-5903-aa39-363e9ee38d6e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e4537653-4fe3-4fd6-83b2-c6e7af0e52c8","sourceKey":"quiz:300","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is most likely to improve the validity of a selection interview?","options":["Allowing interviewer stereotypes to determine decisions","Using structured questions linked to job requirements","Selecting candidates based on appearance","Asking every applicant unrelated questions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Structured, job-related questions improve consistency and relevance and reduce subjective bias."}},{"itemId":"a5f55ae8-7e11-579d-87be-55764e9e75a0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"03e408b2-30c7-440b-867b-de191a38e93f","sourceKey":"quiz:301","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best distinguishes training from development?","options":["Training focuses mainly on current job requirements, while development has a broader future-oriented emphasis","Development is exclusively technical","Training is only for senior managers","Training never involves learning new skills"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Training is commonly directed toward present job performance, while development prepares employees for broader future responsibilities."}},{"itemId":"1559d7c6-3ccb-549c-8137-6d339a21571f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"93ca533c-c803-42e5-8c4c-bcd8e89bc729","sourceKey":"quiz:302","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The main purpose of an employee orientation programme is to:","options":["Introduce new employees to the organisation, job, policies and work environment","Determine employee salaries","Determine whether employees deserve promotion","Replace recruitment"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Orientation helps new employees understand the organisation, their roles, policies and work environment."}},{"itemId":"dc7e3907-de76-5bb9-98b7-f8c6a029c3cd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6c69950b-c332-4a27-be26-6f830e923170","sourceKey":"quiz:303","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A company promotes an employee because of strong technical performance even though the employee has never demonstrated managerial competence. This illustrates a potential problem with:","options":["Employee orientation","Job rotation","Recruitment advertising","Promotion decisions based solely on technical performance"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 1 — Human Resource Management: Nature, Scope, and Importance","explanation":"Strong technical performance does not automatically establish leadership, interpersonal or managerial competence."}},{"itemId":"e31e5f53-2fbd-53b5-8b9a-31c4e23d748f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"28bae8f1-82cf-4c3c-853c-f07d0ce5cc0b","sourceKey":"quiz:304","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager notices that employees are dissatisfied despite receiving competitive salaries. According to Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory, the most likely explanation is that:","options":["Salary is always a motivator","Expectancy has become negative","Hygiene factors prevent dissatisfaction but do not necessarily create satisfaction","Employees have reached self-actualisation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Salary is primarily a hygiene factor; improving hygiene factors can remove dissatisfaction but does not necessarily create intrinsic satisfaction."}},{"itemId":"ab6eb3ef-4944-54a2-bdfa-c58d6b0d8638","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"80bae5a4-a0f7-4418-9222-d7bf0559356f","sourceKey":"quiz:305","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Under McGregor's Theory X, managers generally assume that employees:","options":["Always prefer participative leadership","Are intrinsically motivated by challenging work","Naturally seek responsibility","Tend to avoid work and may require direction and control"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Theory X assumes employees may dislike work, avoid responsibility and require direction or control."}},{"itemId":"f2c56854-8707-572a-92b2-31ea8c70a17f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"07af298a-5daa-459e-ae4a-186ebb37f5d6","sourceKey":"quiz:306","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is the best example of intrinsic motivation?","options":["Receiving a ₦50,000 bonus","Enjoying the satisfaction of mastering a difficult task","Receiving an additional transport allowance","Earning a promotion"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Intrinsic motivation comes from the inherent satisfaction of the activity itself, such as mastery or achievement."}},{"itemId":"2b80a32c-b466-5b62-a22e-c11d4db792ff","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ae0b628d-6e16-471b-a9ab-34560df69341","sourceKey":"quiz:307","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"According to Maslow's hierarchy, an employee seeking recognition, status and respect is primarily seeking:","options":["Self-actualisation needs","Safety needs","Esteem needs","Physiological needs"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Esteem needs include recognition, status, respect and a sense of achievement."}},{"itemId":"b801b21f-110c-50a1-a62c-19118d6b4f4a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"80aa3eec-4a00-4e53-9cd0-0154665656ff","sourceKey":"quiz:308","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In Expectancy Theory, instrumentality refers to an employee's belief that:","options":["Rewards have no value","Performance will lead to a particular outcome or reward","Employees are naturally lazy","Effort will lead to performance"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Instrumentality is the perceived link between performance and outcomes or rewards."}},{"itemId":"0569ff17-8f51-5e57-8963-6021ea28eb19","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9493c44b-cd5f-4a4e-a0c5-96980be83dd0","sourceKey":"quiz:309","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An employee believes that putting greater effort into work will improve performance, but doubts that improved performance will result in promotion. In Expectancy Theory, the major weakness is:","options":["Valence","Instrumentality","Expectancy","Equity"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"The employee believes effort leads to performance but doubts performance will produce the desired reward; that is low instrumentality."}},{"itemId":"24f70ddf-9136-52fe-a802-6dc03ad04fbb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9b7182eb-0f8a-4e8a-a92b-f37cdab7e943","sourceKey":"quiz:310","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"When employees receive bonuses based on measurable performance targets, the reward is primarily:","options":["Extrinsic and performance-contingent","Unrelated to motivation","Non-financial and intrinsic","Informal and non-monetary"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"A bonus is an external reward tied to performance, making it extrinsic and performance-contingent."}},{"itemId":"5cb10fed-b861-5174-959c-373d93dc14ba","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"53f340db-c0bc-47b0-9e9f-073183dc2bf8","sourceKey":"quiz:311","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In Maslow's hierarchy, self-actualisation refers primarily to:","options":["Protection from physical harm","Social acceptance","Realising one's potential and personal growth","Physical survival"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Self-actualisation concerns fulfilling one's potential, creativity and personal growth."}},{"itemId":"a6a5d934-e646-538b-bd6b-dba3af3c45d7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b40bdab6-14b9-43bc-9fce-1ef30a8d5466","sourceKey":"quiz:312","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager increases an employee's autonomy, responsibility and meaningfulness of work. According to Herzberg, this is most likely to increase:","options":["Job insecurity","Motivators","Absenteeism","Hygiene"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Autonomy, responsibility and meaningful work are associated with Herzberg's motivating factors and job enrichment."}},{"itemId":"de54b662-b607-5c86-82fe-323dbf24b412","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d2befd19-db05-4dc3-83a0-366c83d17402","sourceKey":"quiz:313","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"McGregor's Theory Y assumes that:","options":["Employees can exercise self-direction when committed to objectives","Employees avoid responsibility under every circumstance","Employees inherently dislike work","Employees must always be coerced"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Theory Y views employees as capable of self-direction, responsibility and commitment under appropriate conditions."}},{"itemId":"909a9028-ad7e-533b-9a01-46bcc3428908","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9f775bb2-9e2b-4b21-ba5e-3c1b0e6bebe9","sourceKey":"quiz:314","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An employee's perception that another employee receives greater rewards for similar contributions may create:","options":["Perceived inequity","Job enrichment","Self-actualisation","Organisational commitment automatically"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Perceived imbalance between contributions and rewards can create a sense of inequity."}},{"itemId":"c06aa147-8f9d-515c-8e8c-8495da0efa77","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0cd043de-31d0-42e0-844e-7d30773da40a","sourceKey":"quiz:315","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Equity Theory suggests that employees compare:","options":["Their job description with the company logo","Their salary only with the CEO's salary","Their personality with management","Their input-output ratio with relevant comparison others"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Equity Theory focuses on comparisons between one's perceived input-output ratio and that of relevant others."}},{"itemId":"687f9ffd-6cec-5226-bf8b-1bc36a0469c8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5dd64397-38f6-4a25-a7d5-1398faa7f2a8","sourceKey":"quiz:316","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A reward has high valence in Expectancy Theory when:","options":["Performance is unrelated to the reward","The reward is impossible to obtain","The employee does not care about the reward","The employee strongly values the reward"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Valence is the value or attractiveness an employee places on a particular outcome."}},{"itemId":"2142ac0d-4d40-55c7-836e-66e1be11d08e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e0cb7a91-a6a6-485c-8c3e-0d0a9a97c34d","sourceKey":"quiz:317","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best describes organisational citizenship behaviour (OCB)?","options":["Formal disciplinary action","Behaviour that deliberately violates organisational rules","Mandatory attendance at work","Discretionary employee behaviour that supports organisational functioning beyond formal job requirements"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"OCB consists of discretionary helpful behaviours that support the organisation beyond formal role requirements."}},{"itemId":"71b28e16-f6b0-5679-8af7-c3f373021b41","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9cf60bc4-ec79-4fbd-a0c2-566150b8c6e5","sourceKey":"quiz:318","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager compares actual sales performance with predetermined targets and investigates significant deviations. This activity is most closely related to:","options":["Performance management","Job analysis","Recruitment","Socialisation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Performance management involves monitoring performance against expectations and using feedback to improve results."}},{"itemId":"f94c3844-f2b0-51ae-9092-3f348a3c9ed5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"da02ff2c-0d25-41bf-b1d4-62454f8a90cc","sourceKey":"quiz:319","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A performance appraisal system is most useful when it:","options":["Is conducted only to punish poor performers","Provides feedback that can support development and performance improvement","Measures personality rather than job performance","Ignores predetermined standards"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Effective appraisal provides evidence-based feedback that supports development and improved performance."}},{"itemId":"ab7f773a-f6ad-5501-a983-7d7da89650f4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"27675d8d-f7e9-4804-ac79-7cb2a90518a4","sourceKey":"quiz:320","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An effective performance management system should primarily:","options":["Operate as a once-a-year punishment mechanism","Focus exclusively on employee weaknesses","Eliminate employee participation","Align individual performance with organisational objectives through ongoing feedback and development"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Performance management is continuous and links individual objectives, feedback, development and organisational strategy."}},{"itemId":"6a6bcde0-7e65-5b4d-9253-5201e47d0adc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b70daa17-2744-49d3-a0d5-e1b4d14144bc","sourceKey":"quiz:321","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A 360-degree appraisal obtains performance information from:","options":["Only external recruiters","Only the employee's family members","Multiple sources such as supervisors, peers, subordinates and sometimes customers","Only the employee's immediate supervisor"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"360-degree feedback uses multiple perspectives, often including supervisors, peers, subordinates and customers."}},{"itemId":"e8d432b4-e9aa-5292-b680-c2a4defb491c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"df3696a3-c12d-4e4c-8408-d839efb011ab","sourceKey":"quiz:322","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which appraisal error occurs when one positive characteristic causes an evaluator to rate an employee highly across unrelated dimensions?","options":["Contrast effect","Recency effect","Central tendency","Halo effect"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"The halo effect occurs when one favourable characteristic influences ratings on other dimensions."}},{"itemId":"862107c8-1910-51a1-85f3-68d57847a07f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6f390d67-14ce-4e27-8308-c45cbf3cf7f2","sourceKey":"quiz:323","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager rates nearly all employees as average despite significant differences in performance. This is an example of:","options":["Central tendency bias","Self-serving bias","Halo effect","Recency bias"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Central tendency bias causes evaluators to cluster ratings around the middle rather than distinguish performance levels."}},{"itemId":"efb11823-03f6-5caa-8dea-c98765ce151a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8eea4a6f-d5e1-41fc-bb51-c972364f2382","sourceKey":"quiz:324","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager who involves employees in decision-making and encourages participation is demonstrating:","options":["Laissez-faire avoidance","Democratic/participative leadership","Autocratic leadership","Coercive leadership"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Participative leadership involves employees in decisions and encourages their contribution."}},{"itemId":"17bbb6ed-b385-59af-979a-e07290daf57b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9473def2-9f07-4d25-ac9d-d913dce53e7d","sourceKey":"quiz:325","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which leadership style is characterised by substantial employee freedom and limited direct supervision?","options":["Bureaucratic","Democratic","Laissez-faire","Autocratic"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 3 — Employee Motivation, Leadership, and Performance Management","explanation":"Laissez-faire leadership gives employees substantial autonomy with relatively limited direct supervision."}},{"itemId":"b0413823-3356-593b-b784-c6528423dd90","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"11b435d7-654d-4e3f-9e35-60b64b03377d","sourceKey":"quiz:326","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which stage of Lewin's change model involves challenging existing assumptions and preparing employees psychologically for change?","options":["Refreezing","Unfreezing","Institutionalising","Reinforcing"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Unfreezing prepares people to abandon established patterns and recognise the need for change."}},{"itemId":"3aa06bf0-685b-5e7e-aecd-cd7671817881","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"38a0992f-5835-4d0c-a5c5-25a0a5c36f4c","sourceKey":"quiz:327","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In Kotter's change model, creating a sense of urgency is important because it:","options":["Guarantees that change will succeed","Replaces the need for communication","Eliminates all employee resistance immediately","Creates recognition that maintaining the status quo may be undesirable"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Urgency establishes why change is necessary and why maintaining the status quo is risky or undesirable."}},{"itemId":"b1139bc4-d854-5abc-9593-445fe57251a8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1b91be0d-1b5e-4972-872c-a7349a6d721e","sourceKey":"quiz:328","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An organisation introduces a new accounting system. Employees resist because they fear they may not possess the skills needed to use it. The resistance is primarily caused by:","options":["Social loafing","Excessive organisational culture","Goal displacement","Fear of the unknown/inadequacy"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Resistance can arise when employees fear unfamiliar technology or doubt their ability to cope with the new requirements."}},{"itemId":"a045fdb8-428d-5cae-a720-d0123dafeaea","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"06ca59bb-ead2-4501-a8a3-6c61d4c8707c","sourceKey":"quiz:329","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is most likely to reduce resistance to organisational change?","options":["Withholding information","Avoiding feedback","Threatening employees immediately","Involving employees and communicating the reasons for change"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Participation and clear communication can build understanding, trust and commitment to change."}},{"itemId":"f5aca503-fb8a-534c-902b-1fdcca55bc0b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5d9a56fd-424d-4299-8326-968b311bc468","sourceKey":"quiz:330","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Kotter's model emphasises the creation of a guiding coalition because:","options":["One manager should make every change decision","It eliminates the need for urgency","Change often requires influential individuals working together to lead and support transformation","Employees should be excluded from change"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"A guiding coalition provides the influence, credibility and leadership needed to drive transformation."}},{"itemId":"0670d158-bc44-5887-9074-ff5fb98d6c4a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5b85325e-c5cd-4837-849b-6304fbeb85e4","sourceKey":"quiz:331","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"During organisational change, the refreezing stage in Lewin's model seeks to:","options":["Identify the initial problem","Create awareness of the need for change","Generate resistance","Stabilise and reinforce new behaviours and practices"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Refreezing embeds new behaviours and practices so the changed state becomes stable."}},{"itemId":"994d4aed-cc59-542d-bfca-2dc1a469f3e3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5fdb316d-947b-4bef-a26a-c5f671371df0","sourceKey":"quiz:332","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which sequence correctly represents Lewin's three-stage change model?","options":["Refreeze → Change → Unfreeze","Change → Unfreeze → Refreeze","Unfreeze → Change/Move → Refreeze","Plan → Recruit → Refreeze"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Lewin's model progresses from unfreezing the current state, through movement/change, to refreezing the new state."}},{"itemId":"7f7859f9-ee83-5921-9ab6-e9f93c021c2d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e8bcaa07-c9f7-4424-9f4e-64686dfde163","sourceKey":"quiz:333","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In Kotter's model, short-term wins are important because they:","options":["Replace the guiding coalition","Make long-term change unnecessary","Eliminate the need for a vision","Demonstrate progress and help sustain support for the transformation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Visible early successes build credibility, demonstrate progress and help maintain momentum."}},{"itemId":"4d3ed79b-516c-56a0-9437-803487d8550e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"871af934-52d9-4932-970b-3e78554730fc","sourceKey":"quiz:334","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A company communicates a new strategic vision but fails to alter reward systems that reinforce old behaviour. This is likely to undermine change because:","options":["Employees do not need incentives","Organisational systems can reinforce the old state","Communication always causes resistance","Vision is irrelevant to change"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 4 — Organisational Change and Change Management","explanation":"Systems, structures and reward mechanisms can reinforce old behaviours unless they are aligned with the desired change."}},{"itemId":"ae4e74ee-b1b2-55f7-b846-066ed5b54313","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"702821ba-73f9-4307-831c-af2407a4a8a0","sourceKey":"quiz:335","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager identifies several possible solutions to a problem before selecting one. The activity represents which stage of decision-making?","options":["Evaluation of results","Implementation","Problem recognition","Generation of alternatives"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Generating alternatives expands the set of possible solutions before evaluation and selection."}},{"itemId":"35c39a42-bea7-5b1a-a446-55c38bf66509","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"537a5b50-1b27-4b3a-b23e-9893cee91aa8","sourceKey":"quiz:336","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager faces a situation where several alternatives are available, but none provides a perfect outcome. The manager is operating under:","options":["Certainty","Risk","Complete information","Uncertainty"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Where outcomes cannot be predicted with certainty, decision-making may involve uncertainty; risk specifically implies estimable probabilities."}},{"itemId":"67c4e6d0-d540-5bb6-b4c0-518d6747d096","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aaf1fd13-bc4c-4752-b981-c4fa55540daf","sourceKey":"quiz:337","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A decision-maker uses a rule of thumb developed from experience to solve a recurring problem. This is an example of:","options":["Benchmarking","Job enrichment","Heuristic","Formalisation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"A heuristic is a practical rule of thumb that simplifies decision-making."}},{"itemId":"ddbb7a03-206e-5015-a528-7968d07f9333","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4b9645b3-ea74-4da3-8597-bf9ca6d18d67","sourceKey":"quiz:338","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A problem-solving approach that first asks 'What is the root cause?' rather than treating symptoms is based on:","options":["Cause-and-effect analysis","Job enlargement","Social loafing","External recruitment"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Cause-and-effect analysis seeks underlying causes rather than merely addressing visible symptoms."}},{"itemId":"af9aca34-0510-5803-9d70-29a5c5a1ef70","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1fed6f6f-e6e3-4649-979c-0afb8d2aa58a","sourceKey":"quiz:339","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The 5 Whys technique is primarily used to:","options":["Identify underlying causes of a problem by repeatedly questioning why it occurred","Measure market share","Determine employee salaries","Calculate employee turnover"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"The 5 Whys method repeatedly asks why a problem occurred to move from symptoms toward root causes."}},{"itemId":"225b2831-b6b4-5cb4-b6c4-2f85825e0b5c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"00efff9c-af59-47ce-8430-fe82c1a71282","sourceKey":"quiz:340","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager generates ideas without initially judging them, then evaluates the ideas later. This method is:","options":["Benchmarking","Auditing","Brainstorming","Forecasting"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Brainstorming separates idea generation from evaluation to encourage a wider range of alternatives."}},{"itemId":"58ca85f8-e2bf-5dfc-bede-52326be19b3a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7993a6f-993b-45fb-8bc6-3308f65ba87b","sourceKey":"quiz:341","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A decision made in a situation where the probabilities of alternative outcomes can reasonably be estimated is a decision under:","options":["Ignorance","Certainty","Risk","Complete ambiguity"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Risk exists when possible outcomes are uncertain but their probabilities can reasonably be estimated."}},{"itemId":"bcee6685-f9b2-5ba1-b79f-9b33578b3a9c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3eef2de4-c4f8-4857-9fba-4c12f04ed508","sourceKey":"quiz:342","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager must choose between alternatives without knowing the probabilities of future outcomes. This represents decision-making under:","options":["Risk","Certainty","Uncertainty","Perfect information"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 5 — Managerial Decision-Making and Problem Solving","explanation":"Uncertainty exists when the probabilities of future outcomes cannot reasonably be assigned."}},{"itemId":"0b784216-87a6-5861-8146-945934bb8bc4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aa825757-53c0-4b56-89e5-f865e6c5d569","sourceKey":"quiz:343","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A team agrees to accept a poor-quality proposal because members are more concerned with maintaining harmony than critically evaluating alternatives. This is most closely associated with:","options":["Delegation","Social facilitation","Groupthink","Organisational citizenship"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Groupthink occurs when the desire for consensus suppresses critical evaluation of alternatives."}},{"itemId":"49f948af-7936-53b3-b7e4-85d8ba491097","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a98feb0f-768f-4527-80c1-c247c3c9f97b","sourceKey":"quiz:344","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In organisational behaviour, role conflict occurs when:","options":["An employee has no assigned responsibilities","Employees agree completely about their responsibilities","A manager delegates effectively","An individual faces incompatible expectations associated with different roles"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Role conflict occurs when expectations from different roles or sources are incompatible."}},{"itemId":"989262e7-b1c8-511e-a0c0-2e3ed32e7d88","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8e3b4ae4-5dba-4cd6-9d2a-cd32ec5bb7c3","sourceKey":"quiz:345","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is a team process loss?","options":["Collective learning","Social loafing","Knowledge sharing","Synergy"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Social loafing reduces group effort when individuals exert less effort collectively than they would individually."}},{"itemId":"fc73c4e7-e63a-5576-a946-34b6436098fa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"963b43b5-be69-4075-a490-bc6e5701269d","sourceKey":"quiz:346","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The tendency for individuals to exert less effort when working collectively than when working alone is:","options":["Social loafing","Group cohesion","Socialisation","Group polarisation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Social loafing is reduced individual effort in group settings."}},{"itemId":"0b05bd4d-0810-56d9-a870-48f4d8265f9a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0febf889-c88b-44e6-997e-efa5b1b4d158","sourceKey":"quiz:347","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Team cohesion is generally strengthened when:","options":["Members share goals, trust one another and experience positive interaction","Communication is discouraged","Roles are completely undefined","Members have incompatible objectives"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Shared goals, trust and positive interaction strengthen attachment and cooperation within teams."}},{"itemId":"cc93659b-375b-5a8e-bdaf-a1ad7856a466","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"96999dd3-eddb-4481-b744-ee5e82458a2b","sourceKey":"quiz:348","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A team whose members possess complementary skills and work toward a shared purpose is more likely to demonstrate:","options":["Role ambiguity","Bureaucracy","Synergy","Dysfunction"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Synergy occurs when coordinated team effort produces benefits greater than the simple sum of individual contributions."}},{"itemId":"98a47084-f8cb-535d-a781-751946fd1c34","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"243644df-97c6-42b9-9e0e-219e957985d3","sourceKey":"quiz:349","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is most characteristic of an organic organisational structure?","options":["Flexible roles and decentralised decision-making","Extensive bureaucratic controls","Strict formalisation and narrow communication channels","Rigid hierarchy and centralisation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Organic structures favour flexibility, decentralisation, collaboration and adaptable roles."}},{"itemId":"3fbe33c3-8f1c-59f2-a365-cc11589c8147","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"27a40ff6-f847-42ae-909c-92d63cd8f9ef","sourceKey":"quiz:350","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The term span of control refers to:","options":["The number of departments in a company","The number of organisational objectives","The number of subordinates directly supervised by a manager","The geographical area covered by an organisation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Span of control is the number of employees who report directly to a manager."}},{"itemId":"0e947f2c-b008-5ef2-ab5e-4e2d90bc4450","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3de4a983-1bc0-46fe-abfd-924aca7be216","sourceKey":"quiz:351","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An organisation's deeply held beliefs about 'how things are done around here' primarily represent its:","options":["Job specification","Formal structure","Performance standard","Organisational culture"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Organisational culture consists of shared values, assumptions, beliefs and norms shaping behaviour."}},{"itemId":"04b6c75a-3df8-5edc-ae60-5a4b7069599f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3cdd3821-7c40-4dfc-a0ac-2c308396a1af","sourceKey":"quiz:352","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A strong organisational culture can become problematic when it:","options":["Clarifies expected behaviours","Supports employee identity","Provides shared values","Prevents adaptation to environmental changes"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"A strong culture can create rigidity if established assumptions prevent adaptation to changing conditions."}},{"itemId":"90e516e6-1ab1-52e0-b40e-e89c35dabaea","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"062cf73a-9abf-43e8-be98-067e50d491fd","sourceKey":"quiz:353","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which organisational culture is most likely to emphasise innovation, flexibility and adaptability?","options":["Rigid bureaucratic culture","Adhocracy culture","Clan culture","Hierarchy culture"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Adhocracy culture emphasises innovation, entrepreneurship, flexibility and adaptation."}},{"itemId":"a8875586-bf56-59c3-a4d4-788fe581b811","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a993dee5-6c97-436d-accf-6097193e196a","sourceKey":"quiz:354","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An organisation that emphasises formal procedures, stability, control and clearly defined authority is most consistent with:","options":["Adhocracy culture","Entrepreneurial culture","Hierarchy culture","Informal culture"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Hierarchy culture values formal procedures, stability, control and clearly defined authority."}},{"itemId":"4978d7ff-a702-57bf-bc2c-5596429e91ce","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9e5759da-e512-4d41-b752-83168281b3ce","sourceKey":"quiz:355","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following represents the best example of CSR?","options":["Investing in community education while maintaining responsible business practices","Increasing prices solely to maximise short-term profit","Ignoring environmental regulations to reduce costs","Manipulating financial statements"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"CSR involves responsible conduct toward stakeholders and society, including meaningful community and environmental initiatives."}},{"itemId":"73596be6-b379-511b-aa63-fd8ec7346376","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d5fcfd39-25ed-42c6-90c4-5c5bd5c23549","sourceKey":"quiz:356","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is an example of ethical decision-making?","options":["Following competitors' unethical practices","Hiding material information from customers","Choosing the option that maximises profit regardless of harm","Considering stakeholders, consequences, fairness and applicable ethical principles"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Ethical decision-making considers moral principles, stakeholder interests, consequences, fairness and applicable rules."}},{"itemId":"db563e72-e3ef-5548-9164-f2d3ae59a15b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6b24e259-f3e4-4c68-8d1c-691cd87af342","sourceKey":"quiz:357","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which statement best describes organisational politics?","options":["It involves attempts to influence decisions or outcomes, sometimes using informal power","All political behaviour is necessarily illegal","It occurs only in government organisations","It is always beneficial to employees"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Organisational politics involves influence tactics and the use of formal or informal power to affect outcomes."}},{"itemId":"ace08885-60e2-51c8-b865-940726f3f933","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"35d8c374-20a0-41f9-b4de-c49a7a697065","sourceKey":"quiz:358","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"When an employee uses personal relationships to influence a promotion decision rather than relying solely on formal procedures, this may be considered:","options":["Job analysis","Organisational politics","Formalisation","Performance management"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Using informal influence and relationships to shape organisational outcomes is a form of organisational politics."}},{"itemId":"0bb53573-4553-594c-888a-116796b3aeae","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"00b02a28-3a35-4701-b3da-ba53ec073765","sourceKey":"quiz:359","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Business ethics is primarily concerned with:","options":["Avoiding all competition","Maximising revenue regardless of consequences","Ensuring that managers always agree","Principles and standards that guide morally appropriate business conduct"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Business ethics applies moral principles and standards to decisions and conduct in business."}},{"itemId":"93b56543-af5e-5e09-bcbe-a960b7956a5c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ba5cd764-6d61-4437-a1d4-701a02fd9778","sourceKey":"quiz:360","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) extends the organisation's responsibility beyond shareholders to include:","options":["Only competitors","Only senior managers","Only government agencies","Relevant stakeholders and broader social/environmental impacts"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"CSR recognises responsibilities to wider stakeholders and social and environmental impacts."}},{"itemId":"1c8bca9e-6c8f-5cb6-afff-752e07c1f168","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0b2f1a34-8164-4875-84c6-8fe77f0d0aa8","sourceKey":"quiz:361","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A company reduces pollution because it recognises a responsibility to the community and environment, even beyond minimum legal requirements. This illustrates:","options":["Job enrichment","Recruitment","CSR","Centralisation"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"Going beyond minimum legal compliance to address social and environmental responsibilities is consistent with CSR."}},{"itemId":"d7e97271-928a-5bb4-b896-bfe06fc759c1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b9058f71-83b5-4ce9-9ad4-ddc70cb5f87f","sourceKey":"quiz:362","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager discovers that a supplier has offered a personal financial benefit in exchange for awarding a contract. Accepting it would most clearly create a:","options":["Performance appraisal","Market opportunity","Job enlargement","Conflict of interest"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 6 — Organisational Behaviour, Communication, and Team Dynamics","explanation":"A personal benefit that could improperly influence a business decision creates a conflict of interest and may also constitute bribery."}},{"itemId":"c2337cae-2f73-506b-b789-f8bf9aef40cf","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6bd496d7-beaf-4266-8a5b-eb1fdb9621e4","sourceKey":"quiz:363","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The informal network through which employees exchange unofficial information is commonly known as:","options":["Grapevine communication","Vertical reporting","Downward communication","Formal communication"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"The grapevine is an informal communication network through which unofficial information spreads."}},{"itemId":"07e98a9f-a84a-5a10-aef0-4140bacaed2d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e9d80fcd-052f-4bd2-a52a-06fe1e3d9dde","sourceKey":"quiz:364","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which communication channel is generally most appropriate for conveying complex, sensitive interpersonal information?","options":["Face-to-face communication","A generic billboard","A mass advertisement","An automated public notice"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Face-to-face communication provides rich cues, immediate feedback and greater opportunity to manage sensitive issues."}},{"itemId":"85af9ff5-1fd7-5042-b24b-5c2ba143d437","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e52663d9-eba3-462f-a952-a1f860503501","sourceKey":"quiz:365","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Business communication is most effective when:","options":["The sender uses complex language regardless of the receiver","Feedback from the receiver","The communication objective","Anything that interferes with accurate transmission or interpretation of a message"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Feedback allows the sender to determine whether the message was received and understood as intended."}},{"itemId":"e3a63463-2d1a-51ed-82bf-1806e8dfe396","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4b564e65-29e5-49ff-9387-4e9f068c4411","sourceKey":"quiz:366","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is most likely to constitute a communication barrier in an organisation?","options":["Appropriate choice of communication channel","Clear feedback from the receiver","Noise, ambiguous language or information overload","A well-defined communication objective"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Communication barriers are factors such as noise, ambiguity and information overload that interfere with accurate transmission or interpretation of a message."}},{"itemId":"eaff040a-f720-58f6-ac4b-b1a3bd0e2126","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5b7c0385-f534-487a-8a0d-c629fe84d3b1","sourceKey":"quiz:367","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"When a manager communicates instructions to subordinates, the direction of communication is:","options":["Upward","Downward","Diagonal only","Horizontal"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Communication from managers to subordinates is downward communication."}},{"itemId":"724d5b47-ba57-5688-9d1a-485ac946bd15","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3eb9815b-8860-465c-bb19-3e65be920f73","sourceKey":"quiz:368","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"When employees provide performance feedback and concerns to management, this represents:","options":["External communication","Lateral communication only","Downward communication","Upward communication"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Upward communication flows from employees/subordinates to managers and senior decision-makers."}},{"itemId":"87f6db81-ba94-5072-82f4-02c16bd8f4a9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8503b5a9-1201-4dbc-9d5b-fd171e0b0244","sourceKey":"quiz:369","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An information system that records routine transactions such as sales, receipts and payments is primarily a:","options":["Decision Support System","Executive Support System","Expert system","Transaction Processing System"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"A TPS captures and processes routine, repetitive transactions."}},{"itemId":"18b48f80-c297-5ce7-9772-70afd61707b9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2066e175-2a3c-499d-b457-d7acf3d9e43f","sourceKey":"quiz:370","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A manager uses historical data and analytical models to evaluate alternative business decisions. This is characteristic of:","options":["Manual filing system","Decision Support System","Payroll register","Transaction Processing System"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"A DSS supports analysis and evaluation of alternatives using data and analytical models."}},{"itemId":"83e05c38-fc68-531a-a685-821c1a71e16c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"23e6c917-1421-4395-ab1f-79d453ca2260","sourceKey":"quiz:371","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Digital management is primarily concerned with:","options":["Replacing strategy with technology","Restricting information access","Using digital technologies, data and platforms to improve organisational processes and decision-making","Eliminating all human employees"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Digital management applies digital technologies, data and platforms to improve processes, coordination and decisions."}},{"itemId":"0cfb13c1-6a40-57b2-ada5-846b5e75853c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a7a86df0-d3bf-41e9-9508-420ca95f6f1d","sourceKey":"quiz:372","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A major advantage of an effective HR information system (HRIS) is that it:","options":["Guarantees employee loyalty","Facilitates the collection, storage and analysis of employee information","Eliminates the need for HR professionals","Prevents all labour disputes"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"HRIS improves the management and analysis of employee data; it does not replace HR judgement or guarantee outcomes."}},{"itemId":"706b5f92-b788-51af-9d72-6023b515f1aa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9a4b05fc-29a3-47f0-ae2a-aee5f72e1887","sourceKey":"quiz:373","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An enterprise resource planning (ERP) system primarily seeks to:","options":["Prevent managers from accessing information","Eliminate accounting records","Integrate major organisational processes and information across functions","Isolate departmental data"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"ERP integrates data and processes across functions such as finance, procurement, operations and HR."}},{"itemId":"bc81328f-31cb-5253-8f63-823e07bd2344","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0b79ae17-0fe8-4fec-be88-48538f104618","sourceKey":"quiz:374","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"One major strategic benefit of data analytics is that it can:","options":["Make business ethics unnecessary","Convert organisational data into insights that support evidence-based decisions","Eliminate uncertainty completely","Replace all managerial judgement"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Analytics transforms data into insights that improve evidence-based decision-making, although managerial judgement remains important."}},{"itemId":"eca96ada-5c59-50d0-b9d3-4fba713683f4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"185c5bc5-d31a-40be-821c-ccbb295b5438","sourceKey":"quiz:375","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A company that collects customer data should be particularly concerned with:","options":["Maximising data collection regardless of purpose","Removing cybersecurity controls","Data privacy and responsible data governance","Publishing confidential information"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 8 — Business Communication, Information Systems, and Digital Management","explanation":"Responsible data governance includes privacy, appropriate use, security, access controls and compliance."}},{"itemId":"fb6fdee3-6f10-5923-92b1-ba7714ef2a19","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a1a9f289-258d-4f73-8a39-fa274286ca79","sourceKey":"quiz:376","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigerian financial system can broadly be understood as a system that:","options":["Facilitates the mobilisation, allocation and transfer of financial resources","Exists only to provide bank loans","Deals exclusively with government taxation","Operates independently of financial institutions"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"A financial system channels funds from surplus units to deficit units and supports payments, investment and resource allocation."}},{"itemId":"5e04edaa-1435-5874-9e51-7f063d64a74f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a612fcdf-7262-46dd-8620-af5021980d97","sourceKey":"quiz:377","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which institution is the apex monetary authority in Nigeria?","options":["Corporate Affairs Commission","Central Bank of Nigeria","Securities and Exchange Commission","Nigerian Exchange Group"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"The Central Bank of Nigeria is the country's central monetary authority and banking-system regulator."}},{"itemId":"063cca4c-ef5e-5738-aa88-c022e7787125","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"95eb9409-f263-42a4-a306-14f95eb463fd","sourceKey":"quiz:378","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) primarily performs which function?","options":["Formulating and implementing monetary policy and regulating the banking system","Managing the Nigerian Exchange directly","Operating every private company in Nigeria","Conducting corporate recruitment"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"The CBN conducts monetary policy and performs major regulatory and supervisory functions in the financial system."}},{"itemId":"f227f3fb-fd32-55bb-aade-a19f02eae542","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e6521429-51b9-4654-8549-20f98dd4afbe","sourceKey":"quiz:379","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Commercial banks primarily perform financial intermediation by:","options":["Setting stock-market prices directly","Mobilising deposits and providing credit to borrowers","Converting all deposits directly into government taxes","Issuing corporate shares on behalf of every company"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"Banks mobilise deposits from surplus units and channel funds to borrowers through credit facilities."}},{"itemId":"fd0c434b-e2ec-50de-a9ea-035ba0733d52","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"3d35c029-eeec-4b2c-90da-ce53d3dd358b","sourceKey":"quiz:380","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigerian capital market differs from the money market mainly because the capital market deals predominantly with:","options":["Medium- and long-term funds and securities","Short-term funds only","Daily cash transactions only","Foreign currency exclusively"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"Capital markets facilitate medium- and long-term financing through securities such as shares and longer-term bonds."}},{"itemId":"7147ed23-4018-5d31-914a-974c249c79dd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"61be849b-55fd-4593-9578-b87057929762","sourceKey":"quiz:381","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following is most closely associated with the money market?","options":["Treasury bills","Long-term corporate bonds exclusively","Ordinary shares","Preference shares"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"Treasury bills are short-term government securities and are classic money-market instruments."}},{"itemId":"8633da72-c318-598c-b981-1237ec927497","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d5016435-8431-4767-a8c6-f7a959540482","sourceKey":"quiz:382","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The Nigerian Exchange (NGX) primarily provides a platform for:","options":["Trading listed securities","Setting monetary policy","Issuing Nigerian currency","Regulating commercial banks"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"The NGX provides an organised market for trading listed securities."}},{"itemId":"bf08902a-cad6-5c8e-9334-72a37080a1a4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"704f16b0-0f51-447c-ba99-aed77b7648d3","sourceKey":"quiz:383","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) in Nigeria is primarily concerned with:","options":["Managing individual bank accounts","Regulation and development of the Nigerian capital market","Setting commercial bank reserve requirements","Printing currency"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"The SEC is the apex regulatory institution for Nigeria's capital market."}},{"itemId":"916601c4-9602-5f81-a532-de2e49ec962f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"58eb5c00-d6a4-4977-9cb7-faab6fd02a37","sourceKey":"quiz:384","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"When a company raises long-term capital by issuing ordinary shares to investors, it is participating primarily in the:","options":["Money market","Labour market","Capital market","Foreign exchange bureau market only"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"Ordinary shares are long-term capital-market securities."}},{"itemId":"59e3f7d8-b6c3-5a58-93c3-50565f5f4577","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d51fcb10-34c1-41c4-8510-0ce005b2fb72","sourceKey":"quiz:385","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An investor purchases shares of a listed Nigerian company through the securities market. The investor's return may arise primarily through:","options":["Bank overdraft interest paid by the investor","Dividends and capital appreciation","Salary and pension contributions","Employee bonuses"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Week 10 — Nigeria's Banking, Financial System, and Securities Market","explanation":"Shareholders may earn dividends and gains from increases in the market value of their shares."}},{"itemId":"88bb9b97-531e-513d-971f-6277d4e6223e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"course_overview","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"course-overview","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Quantitative Analysis in Management","body":"A foundational course examining how mathematical and statistical techniques support evidence-based managerial decision-making. The course moves from core quantitative concepts (break-even analysis, revenue/cost/profit formulas) through data collection, descriptive and inferential statistics, correlation and regression, linear programming, and decision theory under risk and uncertainty. Assumes Business Statistics.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"Week 1: Introduction to Quantitative Analysis in Management — Definition, Features, Importance, Areas of Application, Analytical Tools"},{"chapter":"2","description":"Week 2: Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management — Core Concepts, Revenue/Cost/Profit Formulas, Break-Even Analysis"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Week 3: Data Collection, Organization, and Presentation — Primary vs Secondary Data, Quantitative vs Qualitative, Charts and Graphs"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Week 4: Statistical Analysis for Management — Foundations of Statistics, Descriptive Statistics, the Normal Distribution, Hypothesis Testing, Confidence Intervals, ANOVA"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Week 5: Operational Modelling — Linear Programming, the Graphical Solution Method, Applications"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Week 6: Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making — Decision Environments, Criteria Under Uncertainty, EMV, Decision Trees"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Week 7: Analysis and Interpretation of Results — Data Analysis vs Interpretation, Correlation Analysis, Regression Analysis, Common Pitfalls"},{"chapter":"8","description":"Week 8: Exam Hotspots & Complete Formula Quick-Reference — Revision, Priority Topics, Master Formula Sheet"}],"examFocus":["Know the definition of QAM, its six features, and its five areas of application; distinguish QAM from purely intuitive decision-making","Calculate TR, TC, Profit, BEQ, BER, and Target Profit Output; solve simultaneous equations for product-mix problems; apply FV = P(1+r)^n","Distinguish primary vs secondary data and quantitative vs qualitative characteristics; construct a frequency table and identify the correct chart type for a scenario","Know the three classical definitions of statistics (Bowley, King, Boddington) and the difference between descriptive and inferential statistics","Know when to use the Z-test vs t-test vs chi-square vs ANOVA; state H0 and H1 for any scenario; understand Type I and Type II errors; know the 68-95-99.7 rule","Formulate a linear programming problem and solve it graphically — identify the feasible region, evaluate all corner points, and state the optimal solution","Construct a payoff table, apply all five uncertainty criteria, calculate EMV, and solve a decision tree using the rollback method","Distinguish data analysis from data interpretation; interpret a regression equation (intercept, slope, R2) in plain business language","Know correlation vs causation and the other three common data-interpretation pitfalls (confirmation bias, small sample size, data quality issues)","Know the types of correlation (positive/negative, simple/multiple, partial/total, linear/non-linear) and when to use Pearson vs Spearman","Master the full Master Formula Sheet — revenue/cost/profit, descriptive statistics, probability and distributions, confidence intervals, correlation/regression, decision analysis, and LP formulas","Apply the study guide's “Common Exam Question Approaches” step-by-step methods for break-even, hypothesis testing, test selection, regression interpretation, payoff tables, LP formulation, mean/SD calculation, and simultaneous equations"],"formulaSheet":[{"name":"Break-Even Quantity (BEQ)","formula":"BEQ = FC / (P − v)","explanation":"The output level at which total revenue equals total cost — fixed cost divided by the contribution margin per unit.","example":"Selling Price = N5,000, Variable Cost = N3,000, Fixed Cost = N1,000,000; Contribution Margin = 5,000 − 3,000 = N2,000 per unit; BEQ = 1,000,000 / 2,000 = 500 units."},{"name":"Break-Even Revenue (BER)","formula":"BER = BEQ × P","explanation":"The sales revenue generated at the break-even point — break-even quantity multiplied by selling price."},{"name":"Total Revenue (TR)","formula":"TR = P × Q","explanation":"Total money earned from sales before deducting any cost — unit selling price times quantity sold.","example":"A company sells 2,500 units at N8,000 each: TR = 2,500 × N8,000 = N20,000,000."},{"name":"Total Cost (TC)","formula":"TC = FC + (v × Q)","explanation":"All costs combined — fixed cost plus the variable cost per unit times the number of units produced."},{"name":"Profit (π)","formula":"Profit = TR − TC = Q(P − v) − FC","explanation":"What remains after all costs: positive is a profit, negative is a loss — equivalently, contribution per unit times quantity, less fixed cost."},{"name":"Contribution Margin (CM)","formula":"CM = P − v","explanation":"The amount each unit sold contributes toward covering fixed costs (and then profit) — price minus variable cost per unit.","example":"Contribution Margin = 5,000 − 3,000 = N2,000 per unit."},{"name":"Average Cost (AC)","formula":"AC = TC / Q","explanation":"The cost per unit at a given output level — total cost divided by quantity."},{"name":"Marginal Cost (MC)","formula":"MC = change in TC / change in Q","explanation":"The cost of producing one additional unit — the change in total cost divided by the change in quantity."},{"name":"Target Profit Output","formula":"Target Profit Output = (FC + Target Profit) / (P − v)","explanation":"The number of units needed to reach a desired profit — fixed cost plus target profit, divided by contribution margin per unit.","example":"Target profit of N25,000 (FC = N45,000,000, P = N9,000, v = N6,000): Q = (45,000,000 + 25,000) / 3,000 = 15,009 units."},{"name":"Future Value (Compound Interest)","formula":"FV = P(1 + r)ⁿ","explanation":"The value of a present sum after n periods of compound growth at rate r per period.","example":"An investment of N1,000,000 growing at 10% annually for 3 years: FV = 1,000,000 × (1.10)³ = 1,000,000 × 1.331 = N1,331,000."},{"name":"Mean (x̄)","formula":"x̄ = Σ(x) / n","explanation":"The arithmetic average — the sum of all values divided by the number of values."},{"name":"Variance (s²)","formula":"s² = Σ(x − x̄)² / n","explanation":"The average of the squared deviations from the mean — a measure of how spread out the data is."},{"name":"Standard Deviation (s)","formula":"s = √[ Σ(x − x̄)² / n ]","explanation":"The square root of the variance — spread of the data in the same units as the data."},{"name":"Range","formula":"Range = Max − Min","explanation":"The simplest measure of spread — the largest value minus the smallest."},{"name":"Interquartile Range (IQR)","formula":"IQR = Q3 − Q1","explanation":"The spread of the middle 50% of the data — the third quartile minus the first quartile."},{"name":"Z-Score","formula":"Z = (X − μ) / σ","explanation":"How many standard deviations a value X lies from the mean — used with the normal distribution."},{"name":"Confidence Interval (CI)","formula":"CI = x̄ ± z × (σ / √(n))","explanation":"A range likely to contain the true population mean, at a chosen confidence level (z)."},{"name":"ANOVA F-Statistic","formula":"F = MSB / MSW","explanation":"Tests whether the means of three or more groups differ — the ratio of Mean Square Between to Mean Square Within; reject H0 if F-calculated > F-critical."},{"name":"Linear Programming (Objective & Corner-Point Method)","formula":"Maximise / Minimise Z = cx + dy  (evaluate Z at each corner point of the feasible region)","explanation":"Optimise an objective subject to linear constraints; the optimum occurs at a corner (vertex) of the feasible region, found by evaluating Z at every corner.","example":"Maximise Z = 5x + 4y subject to 6x + 4y ≤ 24, x + 2y ≤ 6, x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0. Corner points: (0,0) → 0, (4,0) → 20, (3,1.5) → 21, (0,3) → 12. Optimal: x = 3, y = 1.5, Z = 21."},{"name":"Expected Monetary Value (EMV)","formula":"EMV = Σ [ P(state) × Payoff(state) ]","explanation":"The probability-weighted average payoff of a decision alternative; choose the alternative with the highest EMV.","example":"Large Plant: 0.6(200,000) + 0.4(−180,000) = N48,000; Small Plant: 0.6(100,000) + 0.4(−20,000) = N52,000 → build the Small Plant (highest EMV of N52,000)."},{"name":"Pearson's Correlation Coefficient (r)","formula":"r = Σ[(x − x̄)(y − ȳ)] / √{ Σ(x − x̄)² × Σ(y − ȳ)² }","explanation":"Measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two variables, from −1 to +1; correlation does not imply causation.","example":"n=5, Σx=15, Σy=28, Σx²=55, Σy²=190, Σxy=102: r = [5(102) − (15)(28)] / √{[5(55) − 15²][5(190) − 28²]} = [510 − 420] / √{[275 − 225][950 − 784]} = 90 / √(50 × 166) = 90 / √8300 = 90 / 91.10 ≈ 0.988 (strong positive relationship)."}]}},{"itemId":"8acb15cb-763c-5bde-963c-569098304819","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ccb72f45-30e7-41b2-9b06-c44dc5bcd53c","sourceKey":"quiz:1","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which of the following expresses a constraint in Linear Programming, e.g. ‘labour ≤ 100 hours’?","options":["Simultaneous equation","Logarithm","Linear equation","Linear inequality"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"82228255-a182-54af-b039-3ce8b46eb2bc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"d78d7c64-5bcf-43e0-a458-9d49b6105f7c","sourceKey":"quiz:2","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which mathematical concept is used to solve product-mix problems involving multiple unknowns, such as finding the number of Tables and Chairs sold?","options":["Simultaneous equations","Logarithms","Indices","Linear inequality"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"836c9c36-9691-5c48-adcb-0899e64bdf36","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4296b492-d898-4394-b8c5-4e81bc389d23","sourceKey":"quiz:3","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The formula for Total Cost (TC) is:","options":["TC = FC + (v × Q)","TC = FC × VC","TC = P × Q","TC = FC − VC"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"0665a3b5-471b-56af-b729-3e3fc292e572","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5373e57e-dd76-4712-ae2c-7910b50003b9","sourceKey":"quiz:4","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Contribution Margin (CM) is calculated as:","options":["TC / Q","FC / Q","P − v","TR − TC"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"a3a9b840-03bd-581e-ab21-25a6320169b3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1ea76e63-f040-4c6e-bed1-28bbfcde4830","sourceKey":"quiz:5","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the class break-even example, Selling Price = ₦5,000, Variable Cost = ₦3,000, Fixed Cost = ₦1,000,000. What is the Contribution Margin per unit?","options":["₦8,000","₦3,000","₦1,500","₦2,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"5f52fa85-9049-5d36-9e5f-e4c665d08b5f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"874a3571-5113-4391-9caa-7d0f9914610b","sourceKey":"quiz:6","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A firm has Selling Price = ₦5,000, Variable Cost = ₦3,000, and Fixed Cost = ₦1,000,000. What is the Break-Even Quantity (BEQ)?","options":["333 units","200 units","500 units","1,000 units"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"f3129b73-53b2-5134-adeb-6b81dbed7d2b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c4d21843-6ade-4426-ba06-1c1595eb8007","sourceKey":"quiz:7","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A firm has Selling Price = ₦5,000, Variable Cost = ₦3,000, and Fixed Cost = ₦1,000,000. What is the Break-Even Revenue (BER)?","options":["₦5,000,000","₦1,000,000","₦2,500,000","₦1,500,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"4982699c-8396-5d35-b5ec-6b842f05af3c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9abddfa4-57c3-4932-a10d-f3c021ec3079","sourceKey":"quiz:8","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A company sells 2,500 units at ₦8,000 each (class example). What is the Total Revenue?","options":["₦20,000,000","₦25,000,000","₦16,000,000","₦10,000,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"ad3ae0e8-c5f3-56ea-bbef-fb60ff602d77","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6231e474-ba8f-4a9c-965c-762334805939","sourceKey":"quiz:9","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the Product Mix worked example (Tables at ₦20,000, Chairs at ₦10,000, 200 total products, ₦2,800,000 total profit), how many Chairs were sold?","options":["80","120","100","150"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"229ffed6-0011-5ba4-a1cc-0a0878fb24f9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"30ed641b-d9dd-421b-84db-caddc564da70","sourceKey":"quiz:10","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the Product Mix worked example (Tables at ₦20,000, Chairs at ₦10,000, 200 total products, ₦2,800,000 total profit), how many Tables were sold?","options":["90","60","80","70"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"658bdea9-bc7b-5da2-bd83-13bad259ce4d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"89145f05-2a1e-44da-a307-c1bb8ef2212a","sourceKey":"quiz:11","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An investment of ₦1,000,000 grows at 10% annually (class example). What is its future value after 3 years?","options":["₦1,100,000","₦1,331,000","₦1,300,000","₦1,310,000"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"8784afe3-0c09-5f88-8b60-aa7b983db1c7","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b1948ed0-8309-4cc6-a8f4-e52e2dcda865","sourceKey":"quiz:12","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which formula is used to calculate future value under compound interest?","options":["FV = P/(1+r)^n","FV = P × r × n","FV = P(1+r)^n","FV = P + rn"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"27fce025-e344-5ff9-a25c-a78847e53240","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b43b43af-a307-41a0-b57a-fb4863dee2a1","sourceKey":"quiz:13","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A circular waste reservoir has a radius of 10m (class example, π = 3.142). What is its area?","options":["314.2 m²","628.4 m²","31.42 m²","100 m²"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"d4f17848-84df-5d8e-94e5-519bfaef5247","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5e0bc704-2551-419d-9331-d8e65ccd5f1a","sourceKey":"quiz:14","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A warehouse is 40m long, 25m wide, and 8m high (class example). What is its volume?","options":["5,000 m³","1,000 m³","800 m³","8,000 m³"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"bf95d9aa-1cd0-5f27-b772-154ee37e8afa","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5cd60a04-9395-4387-b889-375d42be4a68","sourceKey":"quiz:15","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A businessperson wants a profit of ₦600,000 and makes ₦3,000 profit per unit (class example). How many units must she sell?","options":["250 units","180 units","150 units","200 units"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"70d74bfb-a899-532a-aaa4-cda761371ba5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9dcb019c-7cb6-47d8-a66a-268b25a0a39b","sourceKey":"quiz:16","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the extended manufacturing problem (class notes), Fixed Cost = ₦45,000,000, Selling Price = ₦9,000/unit, Variable Cost = ₦6,000/unit. What is the Break-Even Quantity?","options":["15,000 units","5,000 units","7,500 units","10,000 units"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"77786125-cb07-570c-8821-ce4b01f36763","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8d39faa1-9489-4091-8129-72772585aaa6","sourceKey":"quiz:17","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the extended manufacturing problem (Fixed Cost = ₦45,000,000, Selling Price = ₦9,000/unit, Variable Cost = ₦6,000/unit, Break-Even Quantity = 15,000 units), if the firm targets an additional profit of ₦25,000, what output is required?","options":["15,009 units","15,000 units","15,500 units","16,000 units"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"9fc3798c-b5b7-5c3f-8cee-c52fd3b461c4","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"33199999-3006-48e3-aa18-f6da6afe733e","sourceKey":"quiz:18","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the extended manufacturing problem (Fixed Cost = ₦45,000,000, Selling Price = ₦9,000/unit, Variable Cost = ₦6,000/unit, Break-Even Quantity = 15,000 units), if market demand is only 5,000 units, what is the correct managerial implication?","options":["The firm easily breaks even with room for extra profit","Demand has no effect on break-even status","The firm should immediately shut down operations permanently","The firm cannot break even and must consider reducing costs, raising price, or finding new markets"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"0d78f4f5-d592-58b4-8918-5dcac86cb40f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a8e95d47-bfaf-49ea-afe6-4b356d96bbd7","sourceKey":"quiz:19","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which formula defines Marginal Cost (MC)?","options":["MC = FC/Q","MC = TR−TC","MC = ΔTC / ΔQ","MC = TC/Q"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"a3162b89-8f7d-5ce6-9cfa-da5dac793b5f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"57cf296c-6ee6-4409-b1fe-0893ed2a772c","sourceKey":"quiz:20","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Average Cost (AC) is defined as:","options":["AC = FC / Q","AC = TC / Q","AC = TC − FC","AC = TR / Q SECTION B Correlation Analysis Dr. O. G. Oyenuga | Session 4 / 7 | 20 Questions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section A — Basic Mathematical Concepts for Management"}},{"itemId":"a7ad9403-ff22-5bf7-831f-dbc1458f4e8b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ef9fc021-fb24-484a-923a-00594eb8e23a","sourceKey":"quiz:21","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Correlation analysis measures:","options":["Causation between two variables","The average of two variables","The strength and direction of a linear relationship between two variables","The variance of a dataset"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"67737515-a77c-5ea6-bb80-4ab29bfcd119","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ed408ff3-9397-46b6-9ca5-965dbde6338d","sourceKey":"quiz:22","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The value of a correlation coefficient (r) ranges between:","options":["-1 and +1","0 and 1","-100 and +100","0 and 100"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"85fa6f40-9f44-5c68-8be6-3018ac2f28af","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"70e4b166-ef8f-46e0-816b-a372f803bca9","sourceKey":"quiz:23","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"If advertising expenditure increases and sales also increase, this is an example of:","options":["Positive correlation","Negative correlation","Partial correlation","Zero correlation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"bed89e62-7c8c-5b0a-9cad-e734cf93ecc8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c3e4d341-f058-41a6-bc35-e01f855ad163","sourceKey":"quiz:24","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"If price increases and demand decreases, this illustrates:","options":["Negative correlation","Positive correlation","Total correlation","Multiple correlation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"b50e99c9-efd1-5504-a79b-29b4665937dd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ffc5b723-c393-4f31-a30e-c4f5d675307e","sourceKey":"quiz:25","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A correlation coefficient of r = +1.0 indicates:","options":["Perfect negative correlation","Perfect positive correlation","No relationship","Weak positive correlation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"a26b6eaf-c15e-5059-850c-3ac71e5ebbc9","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"38c14dbf-2ff5-48d8-9558-c30a9e19bda2","sourceKey":"quiz:26","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"An r value between 0.7 and 0.9 is interpreted as:","options":["Perfect correlation","Strong positive correlation","Weak positive correlation","Moderate positive correlation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"00dfa95b-bd80-518c-9b67-0736089ba677","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"9ace8c44-a9dd-48bc-bec3-3e6954b9c423","sourceKey":"quiz:27","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Studying the relationship between only two variables (e.g. demand and supply), assuming a linear relationship, is called:","options":["Partial correlation","Multiple correlation","Total correlation","Simple correlation"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"4aedfa97-350e-5480-bcad-94a11a993667","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"815baf52-4866-46b9-bb88-259e4978f686","sourceKey":"quiz:28","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Studying the relationship between yield and TWO factors — rainfall and chemical fertiliser — simultaneously (Y = a + b1x1 + b2x2) is an example of:","options":["Partial correlation","Multiple correlation","Simple correlation","Non-linear correlation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"9f26978e-0287-5eea-aa55-331cff29818c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a948a204-6802-41c4-b635-23d7e90a5425","sourceKey":"quiz:29","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Studying the relationship between two variables while holding all other variables constant is called:","options":["Partial correlation","Total correlation","Multiple correlation","Simple correlation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"00a5f9b8-57a7-50cf-affd-79a90d713b66","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ecc55d34-6041-4c9b-8071-a2943fc48bf2","sourceKey":"quiz:30","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Correlation in which the rate of change between two variables is constant is called:","options":["Linear correlation","Non-linear correlation","Partial correlation","Zero correlation"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"8d216704-239f-5c3d-9487-61695929a6f0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0c71da2b-9607-45a7-8f84-d0a782c7c864","sourceKey":"quiz:31","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"On a scatter diagram, points falling exactly on an upward-sloping straight line represent:","options":["Perfect negative correlation","Perfect positive correlation","Zero correlation","Weak correlation"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"5a37c13b-88bd-5d96-befd-11e0d3548498","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"11c4598e-0dcc-425e-b456-5bcfa6ff3a98","sourceKey":"quiz:32","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which method is used to calculate correlation for ranked/ordinal data?","options":["Spearman Rank Correlation","ANOVA","Pearson Correlation Coefficient","Regression Analysis"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section B — Correlation Analysis"}},{"itemId":"b0e7f568-661d-50cb-b9ae-8a3ecd9b77d1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a72860c6-29df-45f0-b9d9-7a5df500becc","sourceKey":"quiz:33","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the class worked example, a manager recorded advertising expenditu