andomly assigned to treatments","Intent is to generalise findings to a larger population","Uses probability sampling to select participants","Open-ended and less structured in protocol"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section D — Data Collection, Organization, and Presentation"}},{"itemId":"4f1f12f6-f75d-5eca-8d90-d1496e218401","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"a4daf09d-3a34-451c-a577-892ee20f4bee","sourceKey":"quiz:52","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which sampling approach is typically used in qualitative research?","options":["Random assignment","Probability sampling","Systematic sampling","Purposive / theoretical sampling"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section D — Data Collection, Organization, and Presentation"}},{"itemId":"13a3f2f1-77cb-517b-8bce-d0bb7b6d2772","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45b858e9-5c79-4085-b40c-54fd4c0d547c","sourceKey":"quiz:53","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which chart is like a bar chart for continuous data, with no gaps between bars?","options":["Histogram","Bar Chart","Ogive","Pie Chart"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section D — Data Collection, Organization, and Presentation"}},{"itemId":"34a7e39b-6e8e-555d-a927-f5488c5e518d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"256a14a9-f268-41d5-afde-3b2afef8c23b","sourceKey":"quiz:54","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which chart shows proportions using angle = (freq/total) × 360°?","options":["Scatter Diagram","Pie Chart","Line Graph","Histogram"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section D — Data Collection, Organization, and Presentation"}},{"itemId":"8baf9f90-a30c-5c34-a1db-bcd76c4847ea","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"472009fc-12f7-4e0f-8e38-5b1d2ebd1999","sourceKey":"quiz:55","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which tool is the basis for correlation and regression analysis?","options":["Ogive","Scatter Diagram","Bar Chart","Frequency Distribution Table"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section D — Data Collection, Organization, and Presentation"}},{"itemId":"b41eb892-0a12-5c44-935c-0863c7789785","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5d6b4f4b-7700-4d42-bf3b-7b4ef57881d6","sourceKey":"quiz:56","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"A cumulative frequency curve used to find the median/quartiles graphically is called a(n):","options":["Line Graph","Ogive","Pie Chart","Histogram"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section D — Data Collection, Organization, and Presentation"}},{"itemId":"e8cdf40e-7f45-542b-b463-89f5c5c0358d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f7159190-83c3-44c7-9b8c-7838438497c1","sourceKey":"quiz:57","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Extended one-on-one conversations used to uncover deep insights describe:","options":["In-depth Interviews","Document Review","Observation","Surveys with close-ended questions"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section D — Data Collection, Organization, and Presentation"}},{"itemId":"f0226c6e-eece-506a-a1e3-c2a523e27c60","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"53258225-3856-4ce4-9103-1cfa4cc03ef5","sourceKey":"quiz:58","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Using multiple data collection methods together to strengthen findings is called:","options":["Randomisation","Triangulation","Standardisation","Stratification SECTION E Statistical Analysis — Distributions & Hypothesis Testing Dr. O. G. Oyenuga | Session 4 | 14 Questions"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section D — Data Collection, Organization, and Presentation"}},{"itemId":"9fc8acd5-e8fd-5008-881f-9e8c503029ab","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"972775a9-cb53-4819-a8af-bda1e21ae54e","sourceKey":"quiz:59","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which author defined statistics as “the science of counting” and “the science of averages”?","options":["Ragsdale","King","Boddington","Bowley"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section E — Statistical Analysis: Distributions & Hypothesis Testing"}},{"itemId":"6451f66d-59db-5b78-863d-5c4275c6f4fd","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"33479795-6780-45a3-86fd-cb363ec0b300","sourceKey":"quiz:60","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which author defined statistics as “the science of estimates and probabilities”, used when counting is not possible?","options":["Bowley","King","Sharma","Boddington"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section E — Statistical Analysis: Distributions & Hypothesis Testing"}},{"itemId":"bc5c9dc9-08f4-58ca-85bc-04e91c123bdb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"10f6e852-655b-4455-b457-e732bd8cc71b","sourceKey":"quiz:61","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Descriptive statistics deals with:","options":["Drawing conclusions from a sample about a population","Only qualitative, non-numerical data","Testing hypotheses using probability alone","Collection, presentation, and interpretation of data using tables, graphs, and diagrams"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section E — Statistical Analysis: Distributions & Hypothesis Testing"}},{"itemId":"666563c2-13fc-5aa4-814a-c5eac04233f8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"13c0fddb-9282-4f1b-8f71-c26d59c4fe05","sourceKey":"quiz:62","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Inferential statistics deals with:","options":["Only presenting data in tables and charts","Making estimates and drawing conclusions from limited (sample) information","Calculating averages for a full population census","Collecting primary data only"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section E — Statistical Analysis: Distributions & Hypothesis Testing"}},{"itemId":"1a9e963d-4ccc-570f-bf46-e