g the best of several alternatives","A purely mathematical formula with no room for judgement","A method used only for financial forecasting"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"45c361af-4ea1-5635-a00f-6f0027c799f6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"c53feb47-149b-4f89-8905-9f981bd9700b","sourceKey":"quiz:102","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Variables under the control of the decision maker, also called alternatives/options, are:","options":["Controllable variables","Exogenous variables","Uncontrollable variables","State of nature"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"b9d123a8-003c-5f2c-8e93-3c9568aa2be1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"58ee0ab0-136b-44fb-b326-1d79d998e791","sourceKey":"quiz:103","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Variables that are NOT under the control of the decision maker, but can influence the decision, are called:","options":["Performance measures","Controllable variables","Decision variables","Uncontrollable variables"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"8bcc08f9-09e0-54f6-9625-1de265d20775","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1c0aaf13-62c4-4750-a326-f6a4c9454bf6","sourceKey":"quiz:104","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In which decision-making environment is all information about the uncontrollable variable known for certainty?","options":["Ambiguity","Certainty","Uncertainty","Stochastic / Risk"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"f30dc0f2-5e1f-5243-82c3-69d3b70fc94f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"db5f5270-c56f-4cb9-a31f-959eaee45ddb","sourceKey":"quiz:105","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In which decision-making environment is the occurrence of the uncontrollable variable unknown, but its likelihood is known?","options":["Certainty","Uncertainty","Determinism","Stochastic / Risk"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"34aa0939-65eb-5be0-8f27-64a5c019b643","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"7754b9af-8265-4944-be69-acff6166067e","sourceKey":"quiz:106","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In which decision-making environment can you NOT ascribe a probability to the uncontrollable variable's occurrence?","options":["Certainty","Stochastic / Risk","Determinism","Uncertainty"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"98d9c2c8-9349-5a04-a35f-73caa89f506d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"efb2db38-d4ce-443d-9a03-6acd329219a3","sourceKey":"quiz:107","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which element of decision making refers to “making a choice out of different options”?","options":["Action","State of Nature","Payoff","Alternatives"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"06088287-f4f0-5415-a0f0-3d75fa21d1be","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"2fcb1f40-e4ac-4e49-bd79-d20e57388eb7","sourceKey":"quiz:108","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which element of decision making refers to the uncontrollable decision variables?","options":["Alternatives","Payoff","Action","State of Nature"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"2a052665-c36a-5de1-95c3-b9a86b5b3da8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8c02cb5a-152f-468e-84cd-bc956d1cf2ca","sourceKey":"quiz:109","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which element of decision making refers to the benefits accrued to you for making a decision?","options":["Payoff","Goal / Objectives","Action","State of Nature"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"556f7a02-0601-5e4a-bad7-2f85f76b5562","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"0de1148a-7d1b-40a0-a448-007dada9db0f","sourceKey":"quiz:110","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the detailed 9-stage decision-making process (class notes), which stage comes immediately after “Gather Information”?","options":["Identify the possible outcomes","Definition of the decision problem","Screening Alternative Courses of Action","Evaluate the result"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"5b09d607-3c19-5de2-9e51-09af783a24cb","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"961933ac-bc5f-4201-89f6-832acc24a53c","sourceKey":"quiz:111","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which is the LAST of the 9 stages in the decision-making process (class notes)?","options":["Analysis of physical/alternative actions","Selection of best optimal action","Evaluate the result","Implementation of decision"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"8727be62-80a2-58ea-8e6a-a727f23089f6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e6541204-debe-4939-a1e5-221a27ac0935","sourceKey":"quiz:112","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) What payoff does the Full product line generate under a Poor market?","options":["18,000","(2,000)","15,000","4,000"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"7f44364a-e3a4-57a8-9dde-12c99d42247e","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f0973a85-bac0-42b4-ad07-114794646f29","sourceKey":"quiz:113","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) What payoff does the Minimal product line generate under a Moderate market?","options":["4,000","18,000","(500)","6,000"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"5badc7aa-ceb0-53c0-bfb2-e1da5e7ac6f8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"09e14f21-d111-4839-b27b-897e4551f987","sourceKey":"quiz:114","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) Using the Maximax criterion, which product line(s) should be chosen?","options":["Partial only, at 18,000","Minimal only","Full only, at 15,000","Full or Partial (tied at 20,000)"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"c55b63b8-6233-5dea-9b10-ad40c6c5da8b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"1173eb00-0510-4edd-b963-21730cc42854","sourceKey":"quiz:115","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) Using the Maximin criterion, which product line should be chosen?","options":["Partial","Minimal","Full","All three are tied"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"c855def3-58cd-5cd2-abfc-b6315441f0cc","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"8c836d5b-b270-4ab8-acd1-610973c54411","sourceKey":"quiz:116","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) What is Partial's row minimum (used in the Maximin calculation)?","options":["1,000","(500)","500","(2,000)"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"5378b524-dddd-5aba-aa2c-7ca7c3d4d85f","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6b5454ff-565f-48a0-b4f3-d90630d501e2","sourceKey":"quiz:117","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) Using the Laplace criterion (average of each row, equal weighting), what is the average payoff for the Full product line?","options":["₦8,500","₦9,250","₦6,875","₦6,625"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"230e9ce3-1b40-59b2-a5af-358370dfb994","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f90b78ff-1361-40ce-9c0e-f10ab7e4d5f6","sourceKey":"quiz:118","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) Which product line does the Laplace criterion (average of each row) recommend?","options":["Full","Full and Partial (tied)","Partial","Minimal"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"6f44ea37-23f9-5629-b3b7-c9baa955492c","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"ed550871-1ea2-4967-ad10-97ca9834c43c","sourceKey":"quiz:119","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) Applying the Hurwicz criterion with α = 0.55 (Hurwicz value = α×Max + (1−α)×Min), what is Partial's Hurwicz value?","options":["₦10,100","₦11,000","₦9,675","₦11,225"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"4ae7d2a7-4fe7-5d7b-b8ab-4bb57d591297","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"e8d7d092-40a3-49e2-8c52-b4361baeb1fa","sourceKey":"quiz:120","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) Which product line does the Hurwicz criterion (α=0.55) recommend?","options":["Minimal","Partial","Full and Minimal (tied)","Full"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"4bb5982f-6ef6-5ded-8b67-4347e542d099","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b28f5a0c-2b2e-4365-8e4b-e1a661730e85","sourceKey":"quiz:121","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In the Savage (Minimax Regret) method, regret for a given cell is calculated as:","options":["Row average minus column average","Column maximum minus the actual payoff in that cell","Actual payoff minus the column minimum","Row maximum minus the actual payoff"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"04987aaf-4b7a-5004-ac92-22495660e2e8","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"98806d60-f99a-49c0-ae6b-49c2aad39b8b","sourceKey":"quiz:122","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) The column maximums are: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. What is Full's regret value in the Low market column?","options":["8,000","3,000","2,000","6,000"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"b78b4753-96af-5cb8-b929-7d0867422df6","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"cf6c46ef-0b65-471b-8469-f85492293c91","sourceKey":"quiz:123","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) The column maximums are: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. Using the Savage (Minimax Regret) criterion, which product line is recommended?","options":["Full","Full and Partial (tied)","Minimal","Partial"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"88912b1d-e858-5db0-9a0a-819e72f31376","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"235990f7-24e3-4eae-8e08-09357ccf0af6","sourceKey":"quiz:124","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Mr. Tiwalade, the marketing manager of a food manufacturing company, faces an uncertain market for a new product. The payoff data (₦) is as follows — Full product line: High=20,000, Moderate=15,000, Low=(2,000), Poor=4,000. Partial product line: High=20,000, Moderate=5,000, Low=1,000, Poor=500. Minimal product line: High=4,000, Moderate=(500), Low=6,000, Poor=18,000. (Bracketed figures are losses.) Which two criteria both recommend “Partial” as the best choice (Maximin picks the best worst-case; Hurwicz uses α=0.55; Laplace averages each row; Savage minimises maximum regret)?","options":["Laplace and Savage","Maximax and Laplace","Maximin and Hurwicz","Savage only SECTION H Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression Dr. C.I. Omoera | Session 7 | 12 Questions"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section G — Decision Theory and Managerial Decision-Making"}},{"itemId":"89cc290f-9acb-5086-ac04-a5dc77e43ef0","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"b7be4241-5400-4874-94b7-8d6beaa79909","sourceKey":"quiz:125","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Data analysis (as distinct from data interpretation) answers which questions?","options":["How? and What?","Who? and Which?","So what? and Why?","When? and Where?"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"cf602aa4-634d-55ef-af85-0404d7af3084","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"aadd1bae-f19d-4cb2-945d-75616b8066be","sourceKey":"quiz:126","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Data interpretation (as distinct from data analysis) answers which questions?","options":["So what? and Why?","How? and What?","When? and Where?","Who? and Which?"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"3343168e-7d56-52e5-827f-2d7ebb837254","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4f1ba6b4-b4c8-4f19-89c4-d171b1a42975","sourceKey":"quiz:127","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which is the FIRST step in the 5-Step Data Interpretation Framework?","options":["Analyse the Data","Clean and Organise the Dataset","Define Questions and Goals","Visualise and Communicate Findings"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"1b9f6d8c-1c28-5dec-a8ab-893559a2e7ab","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"45a2bd09-6dd9-4cf3-9448-7ec41218bda0","sourceKey":"quiz:128","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In simple linear regression (ŷ = a + bx), ‘a’ represents:","options":["The correlation coefficient","The intercept — the value of y when x = 0","The predicted value of y","The slope — change in y per unit x"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"d03b9759-e48a-509a-845c-7edeaa22ad50","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"6ac5c4d7-d9d9-4900-b7d6-93e1e509019e","sourceKey":"quiz:129","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"In simple linear regression, ‘b’ represents:","options":["The slope — change in y for each 1-unit increase in x","The intercept","The predicted value ŷ","R-squared"],"correctOption":0,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"de4ecbd5-9644-5e09-ba00-66bfa06fe9c1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"bc225b16-1181-493b-8c88-b83d87d1984d","sourceKey":"quiz:130","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"If b < 0 in a regression equation, this indicates:","options":["A positive relationship","A perfect relationship","No relationship","A negative relationship — y decreases as x increases"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"d0038c40-3d5e-5686-aa98-b7a25233558b","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"075c98c8-23b4-43f5-8d6d-8f190f42936d","sourceKey":"quiz:131","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"R-squared (R²) represents:","options":["The intercept of the line","The strength of correlation only","The slope of the regression line","The proportion of variance in y explained by x"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"f26735be-5526-51f0-b3b8-94e2e6e5ade3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"be1051d1-c51b-49a8-8e74-424436e4edb9","sourceKey":"quiz:132","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which challenge involves conclusions from very small samples possibly being due to chance rather than a true pattern?","options":["Confirmation Bias","Correlation vs Causation","Small Sample Size","Data Quality Issues"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"947c38b2-42eb-5fd4-b4d1-2047f4682573","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"54fbd9bc-296d-4a7b-95ac-53c291a15000","sourceKey":"quiz:133","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"The tendency to interpret data in a way that confirms pre-existing beliefs is called:","options":["Small Sample Size","Correlation vs Causation","Data Quality Issues","Confirmation Bias"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"fc5a44a4-dea0-5f41-b86b-24c59e33d8d3","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"dfc0edd2-b69f-4d5a-8725-8b6415d683a0","sourceKey":"quiz:134","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Which analytical method breaks users into groups sharing characteristics to track behaviour over time?","options":["Thematic Analysis","Cohort Analysis","Content Analysis","Narrative Analysis"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"51d75b64-2ce4-5ad2-8d97-50cc0d94239a","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"f1527962-05bb-4a24-8bec-988e3a7d3d78","sourceKey":"quiz:135","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Identifying recurring themes/ideas in text data such as reviews or interviews is called:","options":["Regression Analysis","Thematic Analysis","Cohort Analysis","Content Analysis"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"e9fba496-8d0f-5987-9524-d9ae2e6f4fe5","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"89c4297f-e735-495a-8560-d57a63a3e372","sourceKey":"quiz:136","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Quantifying the presence of words, concepts, or themes in texts/images is called:","options":["Narrative Analysis","Thematic Analysis","Cohort Analysis","Content Analysis"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Section H — Data Analysis, Interpretation & Regression"}},{"itemId":"42bd1029-7f56-5b8d-824a-92f2405670f1","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"course_overview","questionId":null,"sourceKey":"course-overview","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"title":"Philosophy, Logic and Human Existence","body":"A foundational course examining what philosophy is and why it matters, moving from its nature and scope through the classical branches — metaphysics, epistemology, ethics and aesthetics, logic and argument, and the philosophy of other disciplines — before turning outward to socio-political philosophy, African philosophy, the philosophy and history of science, and philosophy's broader relevance to technology and society. None — compulsory General Studies course.","topics":[{"chapter":"1","description":"Conception, Nature, Scope and Problems of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"2","description":"A Survey of the History of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"3","description":"Metaphysics as a Branch of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"4","description":"Epistemology as a Branch of Philosophy"},{"chapter":"5","description":"Ethics and Aesthetics"},{"chapter":"6","description":"Logic and Its Importance"},{"chapter":"7","description":"Inference and Argument Types in Logic"},{"chapter":"8","description":"Laws of Thought, Elementary Formal Rules of Reasoning and Fallacies"},{"chapter":"9","description":"The Philosophy of Other Disciplines"},{"chapter":"10","description":"Socio-Political Philosophy"},{"chapter":"11","description":"African Philosophy"},{"chapter":"12","description":"The Concept and Nature of Philosophy of Science"},{"chapter":"13","description":"The History and Method of Science"},{"chapter":"14","description":"Philosophy, Science and Technology"},{"chapter":"15","description":"Philosophy and Its Relevance to Society"}],"examFocus":["The distinction between the narrow (technical) and broad (worldview) senses of philosophy","Why 'What is philosophy?' is treated as philosophy's own most fundamental problem","The shift from Pre-Socratic cosmology (Thales, Anaximander, Heraclitus, Parmenides) to Socrates' turn toward ethics","Why metaphysics lost independent status to the positive sciences, and how it remains their 'ground'","The rationalist vs empiricist divide, and Plato's account of knowledge as acquaintance with Forms","The three subdivisions of ethics (descriptive, meta-ethics, normative) and teleological vs deontological theories","Why logic is not the same discipline as the psychology of reasoning","The distinction between deductive validity and inductive strength/correctness","The three Laws of Thought (Identity, Contradiction, Excluded Middle) and their symbolic forms","How philosophy functions as the 'infrastructure' underlying law, history, religion, and science","Why prescribing the organisation of the ideal state is socio-political philosophy's central task","The narrow and broad senses of philosophy in African philosophy, and its four core content areas","Why philosophy should not become subservient ('handmaiden') to science, and what 'Philosophical Science' means","The trajectory of Greek scientific thought from Thales through Plato and Aristotle","Why philosophy's relevance to society lies in providing an organised, coherent view of a disjointed world"]}},{"itemId":"cba25b3c-f8ec-5df1-b865-2f79b781e406","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"5c96c0ac-63a9-4ded-9988-bc73cba10e9f","sourceKey":"quiz:1","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"“Cogito ergo sum” is ascribed to","options":["Aristotle","Thales","Spinoza","Descartes"],"correctOption":3,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"a2ad07d2-6206-5fa9-a246-65778d75a69d","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"650eaa50-f1f0-4465-b483-6c28ac97823a","sourceKey":"quiz:2","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"For an argument to be called a sound argument, it must meet two conditions:","options":["The conclusion must be false and the premise false","The premises must be coherent","The conclusion must follow from the premise and its premises must be true","The conclusion and premise must be avoided"],"correctOption":2,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"e4c8f86e-60f4-5f07-8da1-495089c0b8ab","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"35c6f997-06e6-4023-8fa0-7002eeab015f","sourceKey":"quiz:3","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Reality does not change according to","options":["Zeno","Parmenides","Anaximander","Aristotle"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank"}},{"itemId":"6c3e8c69-885e-559e-804e-6f642d5e7e47","courseId":"40000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013","kind":"cbt_question","questionId":"4d4adead-7608-4724-9a5a-89e61a705cae","sourceKey":"quiz:4","parentItemId":null,"payload":{"prompt":"Berkeley believes in","options":["synthetic a priori knowledge","a posteriori knowledge","a synthetic a posteriori knowledge","a priori knowledge"],"correctOption":1,"section":"Master Question Bank